window.REALTEST_DATA = {"id":3,"title":"Đề kiểm tra 3","minutes":120,"questions":[{"id":1,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A software company stores daily subscription purchase records in a MySQL database. The sales team also manages a large set of qualified opportunities in Salesforce. The analytics team wants to correlate the two datasets to identify accounts that are likely to convert into new customers.</p>\n\n<p>The company receives 3 GB of new purchase records each day and maintains 120 GB of Salesforce opportunity data. A data engineer must create a nightly process to extract both datasets, correlate the records, and produce the analysis with the least operational overhead.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use AWS Glue to extract MySQL records and correlate the datasets, and use Step Functions to orchestrate the process</p>","<p>Use Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use AWS Glue to extract MySQL records and correlate the datasets, and use Amazon MWAA to orchestrate the process</p>","<p>Use AWS Database Migration Service to continuously stream MySQL changes to Amazon S3, export Salesforce data manually each night, and correlate the files with Athena scheduled queries</p>","<p>Use Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use Kinesis Data Streams to extract MySQL records, and use Managed Service for Apache Flink to correlate the datasets</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use AWS Glue to extract MySQL records and correlate the datasets, and use Step Functions to orchestrate the process</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon AppFlow is a managed integration service that can transfer data from SaaS applications such as Salesforce to AWS destinations. This makes it a good fit for extracting the Salesforce opportunity dataset without building and maintaining custom Salesforce API ingestion code.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue can connect to the MySQL database by using JDBC and can run the nightly ETL logic that reads the daily purchase records, joins them with the Salesforce opportunity data, and produces the correlated output. The 3 GB daily sales records and 120 GB opportunity dataset are suitable for a batch ETL process.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Step Functions can orchestrate the end-to-end workflow with managed state transitions. This keeps the orchestration serverless and avoids the additional operational work of maintaining an Airflow environment when the requirement is a once-per-night workflow with a few managed service steps.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/34ba96a2605cb94f504d2426da0159e94b927e9c0b561c8278e2e08296c5e109.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/34ba96a2605cb94f504d2426da0159e94b927e9c0b561c8278e2e08296c5e109.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Database Migration Service to continuously stream MySQL changes to Amazon S3, export Salesforce data manually each night, and correlate the files with Athena scheduled queries</strong> - AWS Database Migration Service can replicate database changes to AWS targets, and Athena can query files in Amazon S3. This can be useful for data lake ingestion and SQL-based analysis.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because it introduces continuous replication for a nightly workload and relies on a manual Salesforce export. The requirement asks for least operational overhead, so a managed Salesforce connector through AppFlow is a better fit.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use AWS Glue to extract MySQL records and correlate the datasets, and use Amazon MWAA to orchestrate the process</strong> - Amazon AppFlow and AWS Glue are appropriate services for the Salesforce and MySQL portions of the workflow. The extraction and correlation components in this option align with the data sources.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is the orchestration choice. Amazon MWAA requires an Airflow environment and more operational management than Step Functions. For a nightly managed workflow, Step Functions provides simpler orchestration with less overhead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use Kinesis Data Streams to extract MySQL records, and use Managed Service for Apache Flink to correlate the datasets</strong> - Kinesis Data Streams and Managed Service for Apache Flink can process streaming data. This architecture is useful when events must be analyzed continuously with low latency.</p>\n\n<p>This does not match the requirement because the process runs once each night. Streaming MySQL records into Kinesis and correlating them with Flink adds unnecessary complexity for a scheduled batch analysis.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/salesforce.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/salesforce.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-jdbc-home.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-jdbc-home.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connectors-chapter.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connectors-chapter.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html</a></p>"},{"id":2,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A social media startup uses AWS Cloud to manage its IT infrastructure. The data engineering team at the startup wants to perform weekly database rollovers for a MySQL database server using a serverless cron job that typically takes about 5 minutes to execute the database rollover script written in Python. The database rollover will archive the past week’s data from the production database to keep the database small while still keeping its data accessible.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following would you recommend as the MOST cost-efficient and reliable solution?</p>","answers":["<p>Provision an Amazon EC2 on-demand instance to run the database rollover script to be run via an OS-based weekly cron expression</p>","<p>Create a time-based schedule option within an AWS Glue job to invoke itself every week and run the database rollover script</p>","<p>Provision an Amazon EC2 spot instance to run the database rollover script to be run via an OS-based weekly cron expression</p>","<p>Schedule a weekly Amazon EventBridge event cron expression to invoke an AWS Lambda function that runs the database rollover job</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Schedule a weekly Amazon EventBridge event cron expression to invoke an AWS Lambda function that runs the database rollover job</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Lambda lets you run code without provisioning or managing servers. You pay only for the compute time you consume. AWS Lambda supports standard rate and cron expressions for frequencies of up to once per minute.</p>\n\n<p>Schedule expressions using rate or cron:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/743d563de6bbf088ab89a4a0c5fcacc38382f96e3f0f04442e354fe964fdddbb.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/743d563de6bbf088ab89a4a0c5fcacc38382f96e3f0f04442e354fe964fdddbb.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a time-based schedule option within an AWS Glue job to invoke itself every week and run the database rollover script</strong> - AWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for customers to prepare and load their data for analytics. AWS Glue job is meant to be used for batch ETL data processing and it's not the right fit for running a database rollover script. Although AWS Glue is also serverless, AWS Lambda is a more cost-effective option compared to AWS Glue.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Provision an Amazon EC2 spot instance to run the database rollover script to be run via an OS-based weekly cron expression</strong> - A Spot Instance is an unused Amazon EC2 instance that is available for less than the On-Demand price. Because Spot Instances enable you to request unused Amazon EC2 instances at steep discounts, you can lower your Amazon EC2 costs significantly (up to 90% off the On-Demand price).\nAs the Spot Instance runs whenever capacity is available, there is no guarantee that the weekly job will be executed during the defined time window. Additionally, the given use case requires a serverless solution, therefore this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Provision an Amazon EC2 on-demand instance to run the database rollover script to be run via an OS-based weekly cron expression</strong> - An Amazon EC2 on-demand instance can certainly be used to run the database rollover script which is run via an OS-based weekly cron expression. However, the given use case requires a serverless solution, therefore this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/\">https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-cloudwatchevents-expressions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-cloudwatchevents-expressions.html</a></p>"},{"id":3,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A financial services company receives daily account activity files in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company must automatically load the files into an Amazon Redshift table every night. The files include sensitive fields such as tax identifiers, email addresses, and phone numbers. Users must be able to query the Redshift table, but unauthorized users must not be able to view the sensitive fields as plaintext.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Enable Amazon Macie for the S3 bucket, configure a sensitive data discovery job, and use an AWS Glue job to load the discovered files into Amazon Redshift</p>","<p>Configure an AWS Glue job to read the S3 files, detect and permanently obfuscate sensitive values, and load only masked data into Amazon Redshift</p>","<p>Configure an AWS Glue job to load the S3 files into Amazon Redshift, identify sensitive columns, create Redshift masking policies, and apply dynamic data masking</p>","<p>Use Amazon Athena views to hide sensitive columns in the S3 files, and configure Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the views instead of loading data into Redshift</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure an AWS Glue job to load the S3 files into Amazon Redshift, identify sensitive columns, create Redshift masking policies, and apply dynamic data masking</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift dynamic data masking is the correct feature because the requirement says users must not view sensitive data as plaintext in Amazon Redshift. Dynamic data masking applies masking rules at query time, so different users or roles can see different representations of the same underlying column values.</p>\n\n<p>The AWS Glue job can handle the scheduled ingestion from Amazon S3 into Amazon Redshift. After the data is available in Redshift, the company can create masking policies for sensitive columns and attach those policies to the appropriate users or roles.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational overhead because it uses native Redshift security controls instead of building a custom masking pipeline. The company can protect sensitive values in Redshift without permanently changing the stored data and without coordinating Macie findings, Lambda scanners, or separate masked copies.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/15772ec6290720aad21774e2bb26467d1290dfad86e2f7236cb7625c869e8240.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/15772ec6290720aad21774e2bb26467d1290dfad86e2f7236cb7625c869e8240.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/86de92958e4bfe2d55cf1fc76a09f71b28717528fb9342f22f6f1e23a3a4e0de.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/86de92958e4bfe2d55cf1fc76a09f71b28717528fb9342f22f6f1e23a3a4e0de.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable Amazon Macie for the S3 bucket, configure a sensitive data discovery job, and use an AWS Glue job to load the discovered files into Amazon Redshift</strong> - Amazon Macie can discover sensitive data in Amazon S3 and generate findings. This can help the company identify whether files contain sensitive values before or after ingestion.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option only discovers sensitive data and loads the files into Amazon Redshift. It does not apply masking policies in Redshift, so unauthorized users could still query plaintext sensitive values.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure an AWS Glue job to read the S3 files, detect and permanently obfuscate sensitive values, and load only masked data into Amazon Redshift</strong> - AWS Glue Sensitive Data Detection can identify and mask sensitive values during ETL processing. This can be useful when the company wants to permanently obfuscate sensitive values before storing them in a target system.</p>\n\n<p>However, the original solution pattern requires obfuscation in Amazon Redshift. Permanent masking during ETL removes the ability to apply role-specific Redshift access behavior, such as allowing approved users to see plaintext while masking values for other users.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Athena views to hide sensitive columns in the S3 files, and configure Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the views instead of loading data into Redshift</strong> - Athena views can hide selected columns from query results, and Redshift Spectrum can query external data in Amazon S3. This can help in some lakehouse access patterns.</p>\n\n<p>However, the requirement says the company must load data into an Amazon Redshift table. This option avoids the Redshift table ingestion requirement and does not use Redshift dynamic data masking.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_ddm.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_ddm.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_MASKING_POLICY.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_MASKING_POLICY.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_ddm-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_ddm-considerations.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-redshift-home.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-redshift-home.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/</a></p>"},{"id":4,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A company uses an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster as its database service and the cluster uses key distribution. While running the maintenance tasks on the cluster, a data engineer noticed that of the four ra3 cluster nodes, one node had a CPU load of over 90% most of the time, while the other three nodes were averaging around 10%. The company has tasked the data engineer to balance the load more evenly across all four compute nodes.</p>\n\n<p>What changes are needed if provisioning additional nodes is not an option?</p>","answers":["<p>Update the sort key to equal distribution key</p>","<p>Change the distribution style to ALL for the cluster</p>","<p>The distribution key should be changed to the table column that has the largest dimension</p>","<p>Configure the sort key to be the column most used in JOIN of tables</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>The distribution key should be changed to the table column that has the largest dimension</strong></p>\n\n<p>In key distribution, the rows are distributed according to the values in one column. The leader node places matching values on the same node slice. If you distribute a pair of tables on the joining keys, the leader node collocates the rows on the slices according to the values in the joining columns. This way, matching values from the common columns are physically stored together.</p>\n\n<p>Choosing the right distribution key that matches the data characteristics and the patterns in which queries are made can lead to a more balanced distribution of data and workload. This approach helps avoid scenarios where one node is overloaded while others remain underutilized.</p>\n\n<p>How to choose the correct key:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/4b66d6439cccd521166f844e8a5a06b082dbabca92f2cffb991dc33fa92ec7b4.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/4b66d6439cccd521166f844e8a5a06b082dbabca92f2cffb991dc33fa92ec7b4.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>An example showcasing how the key can cause distribution skew:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/01133cfeae8e83044a7e46a6cbe3623b1655aacaad8c0caed1ad892702fea9a0.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/01133cfeae8e83044a7e46a6cbe3623b1655aacaad8c0caed1ad892702fea9a0.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure the sort key to be the column most used in JOIN of tables</strong> - When you create a table, you can alternatively define one or more of its columns as sort keys. When data is initially loaded into the empty table, the rows are stored on disk in sorted order. Information about sort key columns is passed to the query planner, and the planner uses this information to construct plans that exploit the way that the data is sorted. The general rule is to Set the DISTKEY to the column most commonly used in JOIN and Set the SORTKEY to the column most used in WHERE.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Change the distribution style to ALL for the cluster</strong> - In ALL distribution, a copy of the entire table is distributed to every node. Where EVEN distribution or KEY distribution places only a portion of a table's rows on each node, ALL distribution ensures that every row is collocated for every join that the table participates in. ALL distribution multiplies the storage required by the number of nodes in the cluster, so it takes much longer to load, update, or insert data into multiple tables. ALL distribution is appropriate only for relatively slow-moving tables; that is, tables that are not updated frequently or extensively. This would necessitate provisioning additional nodes, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Update the sort key to equal distribution key</strong> - Updating the sort key will not have an impact on the distribution skew seen in the use case.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/viewing-distribution-styles.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/viewing-distribution-styles.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Sorting_data.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Sorting_data.html</a></p>"},{"id":5,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>An e-commerce company stores a copy of its order details in an Amazon S3 bucket (Orders bucket). The company wants to log all writes to the Orders bucket into another Amazon S3 bucket (Audit bucket) that is in the same AWS Region.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will address this requirement with the LEAST operational effort?</p>","answers":["<p>Configure Amazon S3 Event notification to trigger Amazon EventBridge. The EventBridge event will trigger an AWS Lambda function to copy the Orders bucket object metadata to the Audit bucket. The EventBridge event will then log the event into AWS CloudTrail logs</p>","<p>Configure Amazon S3 Event notification to trigger an AWS Lambda function on the <code>New object created</code> event for all the objects in the Orders bucket. The Lambda function will write the data event to the Audit bucket. The IAM role assigned to the Lambda function should have full access privileges on both the S3 buckets</p>","<p>Create a trail to log data events using the AWS CloudTrail console. Configure the trail to receive data events from the Orders bucket by specifying an empty prefix and the option to log <code>Write</code> data events. Configure the Audit bucket as the destination bucket for the trail</p>","<p>Create a trail to log data events using the AWS CloudTrail console. Configure the trail to receive data events from the Orders bucket by specifying the prefix as <code>All-objects</code> and the option to log <code>Write</code> data events. Configure the Audit bucket as the destination bucket for the trail</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a trail to log data events using the AWS CloudTrail console. Configure the trail to receive data events from the Orders bucket by specifying an empty prefix and the option to log <code>Write</code> data events. Configure the Audit bucket as the destination bucket for the trail</strong></p>\n\n<p>An event in CloudTrail is the record of an activity in an AWS account. This activity can be an action taken by an IAM identity, or service that is monitorable by CloudTrail. By default, trails and event data stores log management events, but not data or Insights events. Additional charges apply for data events. Data events provide visibility into the resource operations performed on or within a resource. These are also known as data plane operations. Data events are often high-volume activities.</p>\n\n<p>Data event types available for Amazon S3 are Amazon S3 object-level API activity (for example, GetObject, DeleteObject, and PutObject API operations) on buckets and objects in buckets.</p>\n\n<p>If you have to log write events on all objects in an S3 bucket then you specify an empty prefix along with the option to log Write data events for the current requirement.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a trail to log data events using the AWS CloudTrail console. Configure the trail to receive data events from the Orders bucket by specifying the prefix as <code>All-objects</code> and the option to log <code>Write</code> data events. Configure the Audit bucket as the destination bucket for the trail</strong> - If you want the data events for all objects in the S3 bucket then the prefix should be left empty. If a prefix is defined, the CloudTrail will only look for objects per the defined prefix. Hence, this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure Amazon S3 Event notification to trigger an AWS Lambda function on the <code>New object created</code> event for all the objects in the Orders bucket. The Lambda function will write the data event to the Audit bucket. The IAM role assigned to the Lambda function should have full access privileges on both the S3 buckets</strong> - Using the AWS Lambda function adds unnecessary complexity to the solution. You can simply leverage AWS CloudTrail integration with Amazon S3 as the most elegant solution to meet this requirement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure Amazon S3 Event notification to trigger Amazon EventBridge. The EventBridge event will trigger an AWS Lambda function to copy the Orders bucket object metadata to the Audit bucket. The EventBridge event will then log the event into AWS CloudTrail logs</strong> - Using Amazon EventBridge adds unnecessary complexity to the solution. You can simply leverage AWS CloudTrail integration with Amazon S3 as the most elegant solution to meet this requirement.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html#logging-data-events-examples\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html#logging-data-events-examples</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-concepts.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-concepts.html</a></p>"},{"id":6,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A retail company maintains a central Amazon Redshift data warehouse that receives daily sales, inventory, and customer activity data from Amazon RDS for SQL Server and Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL databases. Several reporting teams run the same complex transformation queries each morning to calculate margin trends, regional demand forecasts, and customer segment metrics. A data engineer observes that these queries take several hours to complete and must reduce execution time as much as possible.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Increase the concurrency scaling quota for the Amazon Redshift cluster so more reporting queries can run at the same time</p>","<p>Create Redshift Spectrum external tables over the Redshift warehouse tables and rewrite the complex transformation queries to reference the external tables</p>","<p>Create Amazon Redshift materialized views for the frequently queried transformation patterns and refresh the views after the daily data loads finish</p>","<p>Create additional Amazon Redshift read-only clusters, copy the transformed tables to each cluster after the daily load, and route each reporting team to a separate cluster endpoint</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create Amazon Redshift materialized views for the frequently queried transformation patterns and refresh the views after the daily data loads finish</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift materialized views are designed to improve performance for repeated and predictable analytical workloads. A materialized view stores a precomputed result set from a SQL query, so later queries can reuse the stored result instead of recomputing every join, aggregation, and transformation from the base tables.</p>\n\n<p>This approach fits the scenario because the reporting teams run the same complex transformation patterns each morning after the daily data load. The data engineer can create materialized views for those patterns and refresh the views after the load completes, so reports can read precomputed results.</p>\n\n<p>This solution reduces query execution time as much as possible among the options because it directly avoids repeated execution of expensive transformation logic. Increasing concurrency, exporting data, or moving queries to external tables does not remove the heavy compute work from the repeated queries in the same way.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/00406bbb7faad08e9600aa7629b8544162978f7bf25743d853b5034b525f95c0.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/00406bbb7faad08e9600aa7629b8544162978f7bf25743d853b5034b525f95c0.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/f5b347e91167ee317951ce14a343b8183ff7dd613a22fe1718f8e7cebe13f520.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/f5b347e91167ee317951ce14a343b8183ff7dd613a22fe1718f8e7cebe13f520.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Increase the concurrency scaling quota for the Amazon Redshift cluster so more reporting queries can run at the same time</strong> - Concurrency scaling can help Amazon Redshift support more simultaneous queries when the cluster has queueing pressure. It can improve throughput when many users submit queries at the same time.</p>\n\n<p>However, the problem is that individual complex transformation queries take hours to finish. Increasing a concurrency scaling quota does not precompute results or substantially reduce the work required by each expensive query.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create additional Amazon Redshift read-only clusters, copy the transformed tables to each cluster after the daily load, and route each reporting team to a separate cluster endpoint</strong> - Separate read-only clusters can reduce workload contention between teams. This can help when many teams compete for the same Redshift cluster resources.</p>\n\n<p>However, copying transformed tables to multiple clusters adds data movement, synchronization, cost, and operational overhead. It also does not address the root problem as efficiently as storing frequently used complex results in materialized views.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create Redshift Spectrum external tables over the Redshift warehouse tables and rewrite the complex transformation queries to reference the external tables</strong> - Redshift Spectrum can query external tables over data that resides in Amazon S3. It is useful when Redshift users need to query data lake files without loading all the data into local Redshift tables.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option incorrectly uses Spectrum external tables as a performance layer over Redshift warehouse tables. Spectrum is not intended to create external tables based on Redshift tables, and it would not precompute the complex transformations.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-auto-mv.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-auto-mv.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-query-performance.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-query-performance.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-your-amazon-redshift-query-performance-with-automated-materialized-views/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-your-amazon-redshift-query-performance-with-automated-materialized-views/</a></p>"},{"id":7,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>The data engineering team at an e-commerce company has set up a workflow to ingest the clickstream data into the raw zone of the Amazon S3 data lake. The team wants to run some SQL-based data sanity checks on the raw zone of the data lake.</p>\n\n<p>What AWS services would you recommend for this use-case such that the solution is cost-effective and easy to maintain?</p>","answers":["<p>Load the incremental raw zone data into Amazon Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks</p>","<p>Load the incremental raw zone data into an Amazon EMR-based Spark Cluster on an hourly basis and use SparkSQL to run the SQL based sanity checks</p>","<p>Load the incremental raw zone data into Amazon RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks</p>","<p>Use Amazon Athena to run SQL based analytics against Amazon S3 data</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Athena to run SQL based analytics against Amazon S3 data</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Amazon Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and customers pay only for the queries they run. You can use Athena to process logs, perform ad-hoc analysis, and run interactive queries.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon Athena Benefits:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/4c19f1e2fad379ac423472029092d84b6892af3d365b13d272696f88e9e9bba9.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/4c19f1e2fad379ac423472029092d84b6892af3d365b13d272696f88e9e9bba9.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/athena/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Load the incremental raw zone data into Amazon Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks</strong> - Amazon Redshift is a fully-managed petabyte-scale cloud-based data warehouse product designed for large-scale data set storage and analysis.\nAs the development team would have to maintain and monitor the Amazon Redshift cluster size and would require significant development time to set up the processes to consume the data periodically, so this option is ruled out.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Load the incremental raw zone data into an Amazon EMR-based Spark Cluster on an hourly basis and use SparkSQL to run the SQL based sanity checks</strong> - Amazon EMR is the industry-leading cloud big data platform for processing vast amounts of data using open-source tools such as Apache Spark, Apache Hive, Apache HBase, Apache Flink, Apache Hudi, and Presto. Amazon EMR uses Hadoop, an open-source framework, to distribute your data and processing across a resizable cluster of Amazon EC2 instances.\nUsing an Amazon EMR cluster would imply managing the underlying infrastructure so it’s ruled out because the correct solution for the given use-case should require the least amount of development effort and ongoing maintenance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Load the incremental raw zone data into Amazon RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks</strong> - Loading the incremental data into Amazon RDS implies data migration jobs will have to be written via a AWS Lambda function or an Amazon EC2 based process. This goes against the requirement that the solution should involve the least amount of development effort and ongoing maintenance. Hence this option is not correct.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/athena/</a></p>"},{"id":8,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>An insurance company uses an Amazon S3 bucket as a central landing zone for files from several policy administration systems. The company must clean, standardize, and transform the previous day's data before making the curated data available to external reporting partners. The reporting partners need to run SQL queries against the prepared data during normal business hours. The company wants the most cost-effective solution for the overnight transformations and the daytime SQL query workload.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use an AWS Glue Flex job after business hours to process the previous day's files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into Amazon Redshift Serverless</p>","<p>Use an AWS Glue Flex job after business hours to process the previous day's files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster</p>","<p>Use a provisioned Amazon EMR cluster after business hours to process the previous day's files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into Amazon Redshift Serverless</p>","<p>Use Amazon EMR Serverless after business hours to transform the previous day's files, store the curated output in Amazon S3, and require partners to query the files directly with Athena</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use an AWS Glue Flex job after business hours to process the previous day's files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into Amazon Redshift Serverless</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue Flex jobs are designed for non-urgent data integration workloads. Because the company processes the previous day's data after normal business hours, the job does not need the fastest startup time or dedicated execution capacity.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift Serverless is a good fit for the partner query workload because the partners run SQL queries during business hours. Redshift Serverless can automatically provision and scale capacity for the query workload without requiring the company to operate a provisioned cluster.</p>\n\n<p>This solution is the most cost-effective option because it combines lower-cost overnight ETL with a serverless SQL analytics layer. The company avoids paying for a continuously running EMR cluster or Redshift provisioned cluster when the workload pattern is scheduled and time-bound.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/30da45056fab13ff95593f530fb89af279031b5c1271d565913f97720f0c6358.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/30da45056fab13ff95593f530fb89af279031b5c1271d565913f97720f0c6358.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use a provisioned Amazon EMR cluster after business hours to process the previous day's files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into Amazon Redshift Serverless</strong> - Amazon EMR can run distributed data processing jobs and can process large datasets from Amazon S3. It can also write curated results into Amazon Redshift Serverless for partner queries.</p>\n\n<p>However, a provisioned EMR cluster requires cluster lifecycle management, capacity planning, patching, and monitoring. For scheduled overnight processing, AWS Glue Flex provides a lower-maintenance and more cost-effective ETL option.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon EMR Serverless after business hours to transform the previous day's files, store the curated output in Amazon S3, and require partners to query the files directly with Athena</strong> - Amazon EMR Serverless can run analytics applications without managing EMR clusters. Athena can also query curated data directly in Amazon S3.</p>\n\n<p>However, the requirement asks for multiple partners to run SQL queries on the prepared data, and the source options center on loading the prepared data into Amazon Redshift for partner access. This option changes the query layer and does not use the lower-cost AWS Glue Flex job class for the overnight transformation workload.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use an AWS Glue Flex job after business hours to process the previous day's files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster</strong> - AWS Glue Flex is appropriate for the overnight transformation work. It can process the previous day's data with lower cost for non-urgent jobs.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option loads the output into an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster. A provisioned cluster can continue to incur cost even outside the partner query window, while Redshift Serverless better matches a business-hours query pattern.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/serverless-whatis.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/serverless-whatis.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/serverless-billing-on-demand.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/serverless-billing-on-demand.html</a></p>"},{"id":9,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A company has developed an end-to-end AWS cloud-based Internet-of-Things (IoT) solution that provides customers with integrated IoT functionality in devices including baby monitors, security cameras, and entertainment systems. The company is using Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) to process IoT data from these devices. Multiple consumer applications are using the incoming data streams and the data engineers have noticed a performance lag in the data delivery speed between producers and consumers of the data streams.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following would you suggest to improve the performance for the given use case?</p>","answers":["<p>Swap out Kinesis Data Streams with SQS Standard queues to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications</p>","<p>Swap out Kinesis Data Streams with SQS FIFO queues to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications</p>","<p>Swap out Kinesis Data Streams with Kinesis Data Firehose to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications</p>","<p>Use Enhanced Fanout feature of Kinesis Data Streams to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Enhanced Fanout feature of Kinesis Data Streams to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) is a massively scalable and durable real-time data streaming service. KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events. By default, the 2MB/second/shard output is shared between all of the applications consuming data from the stream.</p>\n\n<p>You should use enhanced fan-out if you have multiple consumers retrieving data from a stream in parallel. With enhanced fan-out developers can register stream consumers to use enhanced fan-out and receive their own 2MB/second pipe of read throughput per shard, and this throughput automatically scales with the number of shards in a stream.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/9122fdc5243e0b0e1a6301fa8442a95d47238d6994001d134abef92870349861.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/9122fdc5243e0b0e1a6301fa8442a95d47238d6994001d134abef92870349861.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/72c1ee4584659e05a2873b2abacfca92d44c80e5dc1e9be067767c900d26f95d.png\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/72c1ee4584659e05a2873b2abacfca92d44c80e5dc1e9be067767c900d26f95d.png\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Swap out Kinesis Data Streams with Kinesis Data Firehose to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications</strong> -  Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is the easiest way to reliably load streaming data into data lakes, data stores, and analytics tools. It is a fully managed service that automatically scales to match the throughput of your data and requires no ongoing administration. It can also batch, compress, transform, and encrypt the data before loading it, minimizing the amount of storage used at the destination and increasing security. Kinesis Data Firehose can only write to S3, Redshift, Elasticsearch or Splunk. You can't have applications consuming data streams from Kinesis Data Firehose, that's the job of Kinesis Data Streams. Therefore this option is not correct.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Swap out Kinesis Data Streams with SQS Standard queues to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Swap out Kinesis Data Streams with SQS FIFO queues to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) is a fully managed message queuing service that enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. SQS offers two types of message queues. Standard queues offer maximum throughput, best-effort ordering, and at-least-once delivery. SQS FIFO queues are designed to guarantee that messages are processed exactly once, in the exact order that they are sent. As multiple applications are consuming the same stream concurrently, both SQS Standard and SQS FIFO are not the right fit for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/d695b6151765b5ea65fc9cde6f4c19438dad59e0bc8a89f5857549c74e4ada05.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/d695b6151765b5ea65fc9cde6f4c19438dad59e0bc8a89f5857549c74e4ada05.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/<p></p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/</a></p>"},{"id":10,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A data engineer is configuring Amazon Athena to create a table for each file stored under the same prefix in Amazon S3. After running CREATE TABLE statement in Athena with expected columns and their data types, the engineer has issued a SELECT query. However, the query has returned zero records.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is the right way to configure the Amazon S3 location path?</p>","answers":["<p>S3 table location path should be similar to this: <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/myprefix//input//</code></p>","<p>S3 table location should be similar to the following - <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/table1.csv</code>, <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/table2.csv</code></p>","<p>Create individual S3 prefixes for each table like so - <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/table1/table1.csv</code>, <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/table2/table2.csv</code></p>","<p>S3 file names should be similar to the following - <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/athena/inputdata/_file1</code></p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create individual S3 prefixes for each table like so - <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/table1/table1.csv</code>, <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/table2/table2.csv</code></strong> - Athena query returns zero records if your table location is similar to the following:</p>\n\n<p>s3://doc-example-bucket/table1.csv\ns3://doc-example-bucket/table2.csv</p>\n\n<p>To resolve this issue, create individual S3 prefixes for each table similar to the following:</p>\n\n<p>s3://doc-example-bucket/table1/table1.csv\ns3://doc-example-bucket/table2/table2.csv</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/fe600b361d192dab4fc3ac569b8586376099736f2d9433338f6bc6741564d5c4.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/fe600b361d192dab4fc3ac569b8586376099736f2d9433338f6bc6741564d5c4.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/athena-empty-results/\">https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/athena-empty-results/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>S3 table location should be similar to the following - <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/table1.csv</code>, <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/table2.csv</code></strong> - Glue crawlers create separate tables for data that is stored in the same S3 prefix. However, when you query those tables in Athena, you get zero records.</p>\n\n<p>For example, your Athena query returns zero records if your table location is similar to the following:</p>\n\n<p>s3://doc-example-bucket/table1.csv\ns3://doc-example-bucket/table2.csv</p>\n\n<p>To resolve this issue, create individual S3 prefixes for each table similar to the following:</p>\n\n<p>s3://doc-example-bucket/table1/table1.csv\ns3://doc-example-bucket/table2/table2.csv</p>\n\n<p><strong>S3 table location path should be similar to this: <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/myprefix//input//</code></strong> - Athena doesn't support table location paths that include a double slash (//). For example, the following LOCATION path returns empty results:</p>\n\n<p>s3://doc-example-bucket/myprefix//input//</p>\n\n<p>To resolve this issue, copy the files to a location that doesn't have double slashes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>S3 file names should be similar to the following - <code>s3://doc-example-bucket/athena/inputdata/_file1</code></strong> - If the files in your S3 path have names that start with an underscore or a dot, then Athena considers these files as placeholders. Athena ignores these files when processing a query. If all the files in your S3 path have names that start with an underscore or a dot, then you get zero records.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/athena-empty-results/\">https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/athena-empty-results/</a></p>"},{"id":11,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A media analytics company receives daily CSV and JSON files in an Amazon S3 bucket from several advertising partners. The incoming files have schema variations across partners and occasionally contain missing values, malformed fields, inconsistent date formats, and statistical outliers.</p>\n\n<p>A data engineer needs a repeatable process to profile the uploaded files, monitor quality metrics, and apply reusable rule-based checks across multiple datasets. The solution must minimize manual effort and avoid custom validation code.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use AWS Glue DataBrew to configure profile jobs, reusable recipes, and rule-based checks, then schedule the jobs for each S3 dataset</p>","<p>Use AWS Glue ETL jobs with DynamicFrames and ResolveChoice to normalize schema variations and detect all quality issues during transformation</p>","<p>Use AWS Glue DataBrew dashboards only, review each dataset manually, and ask analysts to record null counts and outlier patterns</p>","<p>Use Amazon Athena scheduled queries to scan each S3 prefix, write SQL expressions for every expected data quality condition, and email query outputs to analysts</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue DataBrew to configure profile jobs, reusable recipes, and rule-based checks, then schedule the jobs for each S3 dataset</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue DataBrew is designed for visual data preparation, profiling, and repeatable data quality workflows. It can work with datasets in Amazon S3 and can profile data to produce metrics that help identify missing values, inconsistencies, distributions, and outlier patterns.</p>\n\n<p>Reusable recipes and rule-based checks help the data engineer standardize quality validation across multiple datasets. This is important because the company receives both CSV and JSON files with schema variations, and the process must be repeatable instead of being recreated manually for every dataset.</p>\n\n<p>Scheduling the DataBrew jobs allows the company to run the profiling and rule-based process every day as new files arrive. This meets the requirement with minimal custom code and less manual effort than building and maintaining validation logic in Spark, Lambda, or SQL.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/3617d611129ae12e0d18ad2a679ddaac86cbd797bbe6ffae0e97f2b5cf0f5878.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/3617d611129ae12e0d18ad2a679ddaac86cbd797bbe6ffae0e97f2b5cf0f5878.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/setting-up-automated-data-quality-workflows-and-alerts-using-aws-glue-databrew-and-aws-lambda/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/setting-up-automated-data-quality-workflows-and-alerts-using-aws-glue-databrew-and-aws-lambda/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Athena scheduled queries to scan each S3 prefix, write SQL expressions for every expected data quality condition, and email query outputs to analysts</strong> - Amazon Athena can query CSV and JSON data in Amazon S3 by using SQL. Scheduled queries can support recurring checks when data quality rules are simple and expressed cleanly in SQL.</p>\n\n<p>The issue with this approach is that the team must manually write and maintain SQL validation logic for each dataset and schema variation. It does not provide the same reusable visual profiling and recipe-based data preparation experience as DataBrew.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue ETL jobs with DynamicFrames and ResolveChoice to normalize schema variations and detect all quality issues during transformation</strong> - DynamicFrames and ResolveChoice can help handle schema ambiguity and type variations in AWS Glue ETL jobs. They are useful when semi-structured data contains fields that Spark DataFrames cannot immediately resolve.</p>\n\n<p>This option does not provide a complete repeatable quality profiling process. ResolveChoice helps align schemas, but it does not automatically provide reusable profiling metrics for null values, format inconsistencies, and outliers across multiple datasets.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue DataBrew dashboards only, review each dataset manually, and ask analysts to record null counts and outlier patterns</strong> - AWS Glue DataBrew can provide visual profiling information and dashboards that help users understand dataset quality. This can help analysts inspect data characteristics.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because the process relies on manual review. The requirement asks for a repeatable rule-based process that scales across datasets with minimal manual effort, not a dashboard-only inspection workflow.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.recipe.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.recipe.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.configuration.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.configuration.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enforce-customized-data-quality-rules-in-aws-glue-databrew/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enforce-customized-data-quality-rules-in-aws-glue-databrew/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/setting-up-automated-data-quality-workflows-and-alerts-using-aws-glue-databrew-and-aws-lambda/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/setting-up-automated-data-quality-workflows-and-alerts-using-aws-glue-databrew-and-aws-lambda/</a></p>"},{"id":12,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A global retail company processes checkout transactions, catalog changes, warehouse events, and clickstream logs across several AWS services. The company needs a scalable, fully managed orchestration solution to coordinate complex ETL workflows. The workflows must use AWS Glue and Amazon EMR for data processing, store curated outputs in Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3, and support task dependencies, automated retries, operational monitoring, and event-driven execution patterns.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Deploy Apache Airflow on Amazon EC2 instances, install the AWS providers package manually, create DAGs for the AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR steps, and configure custom scripts for retries, monitoring, and worker scaling</p>","<p>Create AWS Lambda functions for every ETL step, configure Amazon EventBridge to invoke AWS Glue jobs, and use the Lambda functions to move data through the pipeline</p>","<p>Use AWS Step Functions Express Workflows to invoke the AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR processing steps, and configure each workflow execution to coordinate all transformation and loading tasks</p>","<p>Use Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create Apache Airflow DAGs that orchestrate the AWS Glue jobs, Amazon EMR processing steps, Amazon Redshift loads, Amazon S3 outputs, dependencies, retries, and monitoring</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create Apache Airflow DAGs that orchestrate the AWS Glue jobs, Amazon EMR processing steps, Amazon Redshift loads, Amazon S3 outputs, dependencies, retries, and monitoring</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow provides a managed Apache Airflow environment. The company can define Directed Acyclic Graphs, or DAGs, to represent task dependencies across AWS Glue jobs, Amazon EMR processing steps, Amazon Redshift loading tasks, and Amazon S3 output operations.</p>\n\n<p>Apache Airflow DAGs support dependency management, retries, scheduling, sensors, and task-level monitoring patterns. Amazon MWAA also integrates with Amazon CloudWatch for environment metrics and Airflow logs, which helps the operations team monitor pipeline health and troubleshoot failed ETL steps.</p>\n\n<p>This solution fits the requirement because the company needs a fully managed orchestration layer for complex data workflows that span multiple AWS analytics services. Amazon MWAA removes the need to self-manage Airflow infrastructure while preserving Airflow's DAG-based orchestration model for complex ETL pipelines.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/be1e5efe187c0ef3e5c839a142794fbc504e8ae2463d22dd466040d5079e4bbc.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/be1e5efe187c0ef3e5c839a142794fbc504e8ae2463d22dd466040d5079e4bbc.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-complex-workflows-with-amazon-mwaa-aws-step-functions-aws-glue-and-amazon-emr/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-complex-workflows-with-amazon-mwaa-aws-step-functions-aws-glue-and-amazon-emr/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Step Functions Express Workflows to invoke the AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR processing steps, and configure each workflow execution to coordinate all transformation and loading tasks</strong> - AWS Step Functions can orchestrate AWS services and can invoke AWS Glue and Amazon EMR tasks. However, the option specifies Express Workflows, which are designed for high-event-rate workloads and short-duration executions rather than long-running, complex ETL orchestration across big data processing jobs.</p>\n\n<p>This option also does not provide the same DAG-centric data engineering interface that Apache Airflow provides for complex ETL dependency modeling. For this source pattern, Amazon MWAA is the better fit for coordinating Glue, EMR, Redshift, and S3 pipeline activities with managed Airflow capabilities.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create AWS Lambda functions for every ETL step, configure Amazon EventBridge to invoke AWS Glue jobs, and use the Lambda functions to move data through the pipeline</strong> - AWS Lambda and Amazon EventBridge can support event-driven processing. Lambda functions can also call AWS service APIs to start AWS Glue jobs or coordinate lightweight pipeline steps.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option builds custom orchestration logic across Lambda functions and EventBridge rules. The company would need to implement dependency handling, retries, state tracking, and monitoring logic manually, which increases operational overhead for complex ETL workflows.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Deploy Apache Airflow on Amazon EC2 instances, install the AWS providers package manually, create DAGs for the AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR steps, and configure custom scripts for retries, monitoring, and worker scaling</strong> - Self-managed Apache Airflow on Amazon EC2 can orchestrate AWS Glue, Amazon EMR, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon S3 tasks by using Airflow operators. DAGs can also represent dependencies and retries.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option is not fully managed. The company would need to manage EC2 instances, Airflow installation, upgrades, plugins, worker scaling, scheduler availability, metadata database operations, security patches, and monitoring. Amazon MWAA provides the Airflow orchestration model with less operational effort.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/what-is-mwaa.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/what-is-mwaa.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/monitoring-airflow.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/monitoring-airflow.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/configuring-dag-folder.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/configuring-dag-folder.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-complex-workflows-with-amazon-mwaa-aws-step-functions-aws-glue-and-amazon-emr/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-complex-workflows-with-amazon-mwaa-aws-step-functions-aws-glue-and-amazon-emr/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-end-to-end-etl-pipeline-using-amazon-s3-aws-glue-and-amazon-redshift-serverless-with-amazon-mwaa/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-end-to-end-etl-pipeline-using-amazon-s3-aws-glue-and-amazon-redshift-serverless-with-amazon-mwaa/</a></p>"},{"id":13,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A company wants to use AWS for its connected cab application that would collect sensor data from its electric cab fleet to give drivers dynamically updated map information. The company would like to build its new sensor service by leveraging fully serverless components that are provisioned and managed automatically by AWS. The development team at the company does not want an option that requires the capacity to be manually provisioned, as it does not want to respond manually to changing volumes of sensor data. The company has hired you to provide consultancy for this strategic initiative.</p>\n\n<p>Given these constraints, which of the following solutions would you suggest as the BEST fit to develop this service?</p>","answers":["<p>Ingest the sensor data in Kinesis Data Firehose, which directly writes the data into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing</p>","<p>Ingest the sensor data in an Amazon SQS standard queue, which is polled by an application running on an EC2 instance, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing</p>","<p>Ingest the sensor data in a Kinesis Data Stream, which is polled by an application running on an EC2 instance, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing</p>","<p>Ingest the sensor data in an Amazon SQS standard queue, which is polled by a Lambda function in batches, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:\n<strong>Ingest the sensor data in an Amazon SQS standard queue, which is polled by a Lambda function in batches, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Lambda lets you run code without provisioning or managing servers. You pay only for the compute time you consume. Amazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) is a fully managed message queuing service that enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. SQS offers two types of message queues. Standard queues offer maximum throughput, best-effort ordering, and at-least-once delivery. SQS FIFO queues are designed to guarantee that messages are processed exactly once, in the exact order that they are sent.</p>\n\n<p>You can use an AWS Lambda function to process messages in an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue. Lambda event source mappings support standard queues and first-in, first-out (FIFO) queues. With Amazon SQS, you can offload tasks from one component of your application by sending them to a queue and processing them asynchronously.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/d0c25dd4c98f7bfc1356e4277431b5e49e9344a01cbadd05885c2db68458491e.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/d0c25dd4c98f7bfc1356e4277431b5e49e9344a01cbadd05885c2db68458491e.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>AWS manages all ongoing operations and underlying infrastructure needed to provide a highly available and scalable message queuing service. With SQS, there is no upfront cost, no need to acquire, install, and configure messaging software, and no time-consuming build-out and maintenance of supporting infrastructure. SQS queues are dynamically created and scale automatically so you can build and grow applications quickly and efficiently.\nAs there is no need to manually provision the capacity, this is the correct option.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ingest the sensor data in Kinesis Data Firehose, which directly writes the data into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is a fully managed service for delivering real-time streaming data to destinations such as Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3), Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, Splunk, and any custom HTTP endpoint or HTTP endpoints owned by supported third-party service providers, including Datadog, Dynatrace, LogicMonitor, MongoDB, New Relic, and Sumo Logic.</p>\n\n<p>Firehose cannot directly write into a DynamoDB table, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ingest the sensor data in an Amazon SQS standard queue, which is polled by an application running on an EC2 instance, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Ingest the sensor data in a Kinesis Data Stream, which is polled by an application running on an EC2 instance, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing</strong></p>\n\n<p>Using an application on an EC2 instance is ruled out as the company wants to use fully serverless components. So both these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:\n<a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/sqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/sqs/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/</a></p>"},{"id":14,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A financial analytics company runs an Amazon Redshift data warehouse that more than 150 employees access each day. Users connect by assuming different IAM roles that represent departments such as finance, risk, operations, and executive reporting.</p>\n\n<p>The company needs to manage access to schemas, tables, and other database objects based on each user’s job function, assigned permissions, and the sensitivity of the underlying data. The security team wants a scalable permission model that avoids managing privileges separately for every individual user.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon Redshift row-level security policies to filter table rows for each user based on department, region, and assigned business unit</p>","<p>Use AWS Lake Formation LF-Tags to manage Redshift database object permissions for all internal users who query native Redshift tables</p>","<p>Use Amazon Redshift role-based access control to grant database privileges to roles and assign the roles to users</p>","<p>Use Amazon Redshift column-level privileges to grant or deny access to sensitive columns for each user and reporting group</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Redshift role-based access control to grant database privileges to roles and assign the roles to users</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift role-based access control is the correct solution because the company needs to control access to database objects for many users based on job role, permissions, and data sensitivity. RBAC lets administrators create database roles, grant privileges to those roles, and assign the roles to users.</p>\n\n<p>This model scales better than managing privileges user by user. For example, the company can create roles for finance analysts, risk reviewers, operations users, and executives, then grant each role the appropriate access to schemas, tables, views, or other database objects.</p>\n\n<p>RBAC directly matches the requirement because it provides a broad permission-management framework for many Redshift users. Row-level security, column-level security, and dynamic data masking can provide additional fine-grained controls, but RBAC is the primary feature for managing database access by role.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/2120b06ac8886b1aba64874eacb40954ee2a1e0c08163b05e78ae60fffd4b566.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/2120b06ac8886b1aba64874eacb40954ee2a1e0c08163b05e78ae60fffd4b566.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/292cb0efcbbdada35bbaef57617ea7bc93a721d8fb6419798a11a3b1d33439ee.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/292cb0efcbbdada35bbaef57617ea7bc93a721d8fb6419798a11a3b1d33439ee.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Redshift row-level security policies to filter table rows for each user based on department, region, and assigned business unit</strong> - Row-level security can restrict which rows a user or role can access within a table. It is useful when different users should see different records from the same table.</p>\n\n<p>This option is too narrow for the requirement. The company needs to manage access to database objects based on job roles and permissions across more than 100 users, not only filter rows inside selected tables.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Lake Formation LF-Tags to manage Redshift database object permissions for all internal users who query native Redshift tables</strong> - AWS Lake Formation LF-Tags can help manage access to data lake resources and can support tag-based governance for cataloged data. This is useful for centralized governance of data lake tables.</p>\n\n<p>This does not match the requirement because the company needs to control user access to native objects in an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. Amazon Redshift RBAC is the appropriate permission model for Redshift database users and objects.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Redshift column-level privileges to grant or deny access to sensitive columns for each user and reporting group</strong> - Column-level security can restrict access to specific columns in a table. This can help when users may access a table but should not see certain sensitive attributes.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because the requirement is broader than column access. The company needs scalable access control based on job role and permissions across Redshift objects, which is the purpose of RBAC.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Roles.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Roles.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_roles-managing.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_roles-managing.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_tutorial-RBAC.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_tutorial-RBAC.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_Database_objects.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_Database_objects.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/</a></p>"},{"id":15,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>The data engineering team at a social media company has noticed that while some of the images stored in Amazon S3 are frequently accessed, others sit idle for a considerable time.</p>\n\n<p>What is your recommendation to build the MOST cost-effective solution?</p>","answers":["<p>Store the images using the Amazon S3 Standard-IA storage class</p>","<p>Create a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance in the same region as the bucket storing the images. The application is invoked daily via Amazon CloudWatch and it changes the storage class of infrequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 Standard-IA and the frequently accessed objects are migrated to Amazon S3 Standard class</p>","<p>Store the images using the Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class</p>","<p>Create a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance in the same region as the bucket storing the images. The application is invoked daily via Amazon CloudWatch and it changes the storage class of infrequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA and the frequently accessed objects are migrated to Amazon S3 Standard class</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the images using the Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class</strong></p>\n\n<p>The Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class is designed to optimize costs by automatically moving data to the most cost-effective access tier, without performance impact or operational overhead. It works by storing objects in two access tiers: one tier that is optimized for frequent access and another lower-cost tier that is optimized for infrequent access.</p>\n\n<p>For a small monthly monitoring and automation fee per object, Amazon S3 monitors access patterns of the objects in S3 Intelligent-Tiering and moves the ones that have not been accessed for 30 consecutive days to the infrequent access tier. If an object in the infrequent access tier is accessed, it is automatically moved back to the frequent access tier. Therefore using the Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class is the correct solution for the given problem statement.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 Storage Classes Overview:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/8e60dd73fc8b86832996d65cb20dc7a959db149930599272f5f638957925920d.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/8e60dd73fc8b86832996d65cb20dc7a959db149930599272f5f638957925920d.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the images using the Amazon S3 Standard-IA storage class</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 Standard-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. Amazon S3 Standard-IA offers high durability, high throughput, and low latency of Amazon S3 Standard, with a low per GB storage price and per GB retrieval fee. This combination of low cost and high performance makes Amazon S3 Standard-IA ideal for long-term storage, backups, and as a data store for disaster recovery files. The minimum storage duration charge is 30 days. As some of the objects are frequently accessed, the per GB retrieval fee for Amazon S3 Standard-IA can cause the costs to shoot up, hence this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance in the same region as the bucket storing the images. The application is invoked daily via Amazon CloudWatch and it changes the storage class of infrequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA and the frequently accessed objects are migrated to Amazon S3 Standard class</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance in the same region as the bucket storing the images. The application is invoked daily via Amazon CloudWatch and it changes the storage class of infrequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 Standard-IA and the frequently accessed objects are migrated to Amazon S3 Standard class</strong></p>\n\n<p>Creating a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance for managing the desired Amazon S3 storage class entails significant development cost as well as infrastructure maintenance effort. The Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class does the job cost-effectively. Therefore both these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/</a></p>"},{"id":16,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A retail company is building a new data lake ingestion pipeline for supplier, inventory, and sales data. A business analyst must clean, standardize, and prepare incoming datasets before the data engineering team adds advanced transformations and orchestration. The analyst needs a solution that supports no-code data preparation and can remain part of the AWS Glue pipeline.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational effort?</p>","answers":["<p>Use AWS Glue Studio with data preparation recipe transformations and allow data engineers to extend the same visual ETL pipeline with additional transformations</p>","<p>Use Python and pandas in an AWS Glue Studio notebook to prepare the datasets and ask data engineers to add the remaining pipeline transformations</p>","<p>Use Amazon Athena SQL views to standardize the raw datasets, then allow data engineers to copy the view results into curated S3 prefixes for downstream transformations</p>","<p>Use Amazon SageMaker Canvas and SageMaker Data Wrangler to prepare a new dataset, then ask data engineers to rebuild the remaining pipeline in AWS Glue</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue Studio with data preparation recipe transformations and allow data engineers to extend the same visual ETL pipeline with additional transformations</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue Studio with data preparation recipe transformations is the best fit because it gives analysts a no-code visual experience for preparing data. The analyst can define data cleaning and standardization steps without writing new code.</p>\n\n<p>This approach also keeps the preparation work inside the AWS Glue ecosystem. The data preparation recipe can be part of a visual ETL job, and data engineers can add additional AWS Glue transformations before or after the preparation step.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational effort because it avoids handoffs that require engineers to reimplement rules from documents or notebooks. It also avoids using a separate ML-focused preparation environment when the final pipeline must continue in AWS Glue.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Python and pandas in an AWS Glue Studio notebook to prepare the datasets and ask data engineers to add the remaining pipeline transformations</strong> - AWS Glue Studio notebooks can support interactive development, and Python with pandas can prepare data if the analyst has the required coding skills. This can be useful for engineering-driven experimentation.</p>\n\n<p>However, the requirement says the analyst needs a solution that does not require writing new code. Python and pandas do not meet the no-code requirement and would increase dependence on engineering support.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon SageMaker Canvas and SageMaker Data Wrangler to prepare a new dataset, then ask data engineers to rebuild the remaining pipeline in AWS Glue</strong> - Amazon SageMaker Canvas and data preparation capabilities can help users prepare datasets for machine learning workflows. This can be useful when the primary goal is model development or ML-driven analysis.</p>\n\n<p>However, the company is building an AWS Glue data lake pipeline. Preparing data in a separate SageMaker workflow and then rebuilding the remaining ETL process in AWS Glue adds avoidable handoff and integration overhead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Athena SQL views to standardize the raw datasets, then allow data engineers to copy the view results into curated S3 prefixes for downstream transformations</strong> - Athena SQL views can standardize some datasets by projecting columns, renaming fields, or applying SQL expressions. They can be useful for query-time transformations over data in Amazon S3.</p>\n\n<p>However, SQL views do not provide the same no-code preparation experience for analysts. This design also requires extra engineering work to materialize view results into curated S3 locations before the rest of the pipeline can proceed.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-studio-data-preparation.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-studio-data-preparation.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-studio-data-preparation-recipe-transform-tutorial.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-studio-data-preparation-recipe-transform-tutorial.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/edit-jobs-transforms.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/edit-jobs-transforms.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/integrate-your-data-and-collaborate-using-data-preparation-in-aws-glue-studio/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/integrate-your-data-and-collaborate-using-data-preparation-in-aws-glue-studio/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/author-data-integration-jobs-with-an-interactive-data-preparation-experience-with-aws-glue-visual-etl/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/author-data-integration-jobs-with-an-interactive-data-preparation-experience-with-aws-glue-visual-etl/</a></p>"},{"id":17,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A company uses several analytics services from AWS such as AWS Glue, Amazon Athena, Amazon Redshift Spectrum, and Amazon EMR to query the data. The company wants to build a data lake in AWS that can provide row-level and column-level data access to specific teams/groups that use the aforementioned AWS services.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution can address these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Set up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Define the Amazon S3 access point for each AWS service. Configure each access point with distinct permissions and network controls needed for each AWS service</p>","<p>Use Amazon S3 for data lake storage. Use Apache Ranger through Amazon EMR to restrict data access by rows and columns. Provide data access by using Apache Pig</p>","<p>Set up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Configure AWS Lake Formation permissions to provide fine-grained row and column-wise access. Provide access to data for the AWS services via Lake Formation permissions only</p>","<p>Set up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in Amazon S3 files without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. Configure access control through the IAM roles attached to the Redshift cluster</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Configure AWS Lake Formation permissions to provide fine-grained row and column-wise access. Provide access to data for the AWS services via Lake Formation permissions only</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Lake Formation helps you centrally govern, secure, and globally share data for analytics and machine learning. With Lake Formation, you can manage fine-grained access control for your data lake data on Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and its metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Lake Formation permissions are enforced using granular controls at the column, row, and cell levels across AWS analytics and machine learning services, including Amazon Athena, Amazon QuickSight, Amazon Redshift Spectrum, Amazon EMR, and AWS Glue.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Lake Formation permissions management workflow:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/5dfef0e1fb2f134c748ff7d710da0d60399cad0bd100e0db64e2dd362e349bdc.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/5dfef0e1fb2f134c748ff7d710da0d60399cad0bd100e0db64e2dd362e349bdc.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in Amazon S3 files without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. Configure access control through the IAM roles attached to the Redshift cluster</strong> - IAM roles cannot provide granular row-level and column-level data access permissions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Define the Amazon S3 access point for each AWS service. Configure each access point with distinct permissions and network controls needed for each AWS service</strong> - Each access point has distinct permissions and network controls that S3 applies for any request that is made through that access point. However, fine-grained access (such as row-level and column-level data access) is not possible via S3 access points.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon S3 for data lake storage. Use Apache Ranger through Amazon EMR to restrict data access by rows and columns. Provide data access by using Apache Pig</strong> - Amazon EMR natively integrates with Apache Ranger, allowing you to define, enforce, and audit fine-grained data access control. With this feature, you can define and enforce database, table, and column-level authorization policies for Apache Spark and Apache Hive users to access data through Hive Metastore. Apache Pig is a platform for analyzing large data sets that consists of a high-level language for expressing data analysis programs, coupled with infrastructure for evaluating these programs. The salient property of Pig programs is that their structure is amenable to substantial parallelization, which in turn enables them to handle very large data sets. For the given use case, configuring Amazon EMR adds a lot of operational overhead and additional costs compared to just implementing the required access through Lake Formation.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/service-integrations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/service-integrations.html</a></p>"},{"id":18,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>An e-commerce company uses Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to process real-time clickstream data from its website. The company needs to perform near real-time analytics of the streaming data in conjunction with the previous day's data. The streaming data should be stored in Amazon Redshift Serverless service.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Use federated queries feature of Amazon Redshift to retrieve and efficiently query the streaming data for analytics</p>","<p>Direct the Kinesis Data Streams output into a Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream. Leverage the direct integration of Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon Redshift Serverless to deliver the streaming data</p>","<p>Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to retrieve and efficiently query the streaming data for analytics</p>","<p>Use the streaming ingestion feature of Amazon Redshift</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use the streaming ingestion feature of Amazon Redshift</strong></p>\n\n<p>Streaming ingestion provides low-latency, high-speed ingestion of stream data from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka into an Amazon Redshift provisioned or Amazon Redshift Serverless materialized view. It lowers the time it takes to access data and it reduces storage cost. You can configure streaming ingestion for your Amazon Redshift cluster or for Amazon Redshift Serverless and create a materialized view. After that, using materialized-view refresh, you can ingest hundreds of megabytes of data per second. This results in fast access to external data that is quickly refreshed.</p>\n\n<p>Use cases for Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion involve working with data that is generated continually (streamed) and must be processed within a short period (latency) of its generation. This is called near real-time analytics. Sources of data can vary and include IoT devices, system telemetry data, or clickstream data from a busy website or application.</p>\n\n<p>With streaming ingestion, you bypass several steps that involve other service(s) needed between Kinesis Data Streams and Redshift. The advantages of this configuration are:</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li><p>You don't have to send data to an Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream, because with streaming ingestion, data can be sent directly from Kinesis Data Streams to a materialized view in a Redshift database.</p></li>\n<li><p>You don't have to land streamed data in Amazon S3, because streaming ingestion data goes directly to the Redshift materialized view.</p></li>\n<li><p>You don't have to write and run COPY commands because the data in the materialized view is refreshed directly from the stream. Loading data from Amazon S3 to Redshift isn't part of the process.</p></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/7d27763ceaaf365128975e2a9b07832d027356d989698594a0ca85f5aaba91fb.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/7d27763ceaaf365128975e2a9b07832d027356d989698594a0ca85f5aaba91fb.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use federated queries feature of Amazon Redshift to retrieve and efficiently query the streaming data for analytics</strong> - By using federated queries in Amazon Redshift, you can query and analyze data across operational databases, data warehouses, and data lakes. With the Federated Query feature, you can integrate queries from Amazon Redshift on live data in external databases with queries across your Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3 environments. This option acts as a distractor since Redshift federated queries cannot be used to process streaming data from external sources.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Direct the Kinesis Data Streams output into a Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream. Leverage the direct integration of Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon Redshift Serverless to deliver the streaming data</strong> - This option introduces an unnecessary hop in the form of Kinesis Data Firehose to the solution. The ideal solution should have the LEAST operational overhead, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to retrieve and efficiently query the streaming data for analytics</strong> - Using Amazon Redshift Spectrum, you can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. This option acts as a distractor since Redshift Spectrum cannot be used to process streaming data from external sources.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-create-sql-command.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-create-sql-command.html</a></p>"},{"id":19,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A logistics company stores delivery status data in an Amazon S3 bucket and uses AWS Glue Data Catalog tables so Amazon Athena can query the data. The data is partitioned by status code.</p>\n\n<p>A data engineer manually moves all objects from the partition path status=pending to the partition path status=completed. The status=pending S3 prefix is now empty, but the status=pending partition still appears in the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata. The data engineer must correct the metadata discrepancy in Athena.</p>\n\n<p>Which Athena command should the data engineer run?</p>","answers":["<p>Run ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION for the status=completed prefix and leverage Athena to automatically remove the empty status=pending partition</p>","<p>Run ALTER TABLE DROP PARTITION to remove the stale status=pending partition from the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata</p>","<p>Run MSCK REPAIR TABLE to remove the stale status=pending partition from the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata</p>","<p>Run ALTER TABLE SET TBLPROPERTIES to update the table properties so Athena ignores the empty status=pending S3 prefix</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run ALTER TABLE DROP PARTITION to remove the stale status=pending partition from the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata</strong></p>\n\n<p>ALTER TABLE DROP PARTITION is the correct Athena command for removing a stale partition from table metadata. In this scenario, the data engineer moved the objects from status=pending to status=completed, so the old partition location is empty but still registered in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.</p>\n\n<p>Athena uses the partition metadata in the Data Catalog to decide which S3 locations to scan. If an old partition remains registered after the S3 data is moved, Athena can still show that partition even though the underlying prefix no longer contains data.</p>\n\n<p>Dropping the stale partition removes the incorrect metadata entry without changing the objects in Amazon S3. This directly resolves the mismatch between the S3 layout and the Glue Data Catalog partition metadata.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run ALTER TABLE SET TBLPROPERTIES to update the table properties so Athena ignores the empty status=pending S3 prefix</strong> - ALTER TABLE SET TBLPROPERTIES can update table-level properties. This is useful for settings such as classification, projection configuration, or other metadata properties.</p>\n\n<p>This option does not remove a specific stale partition from the Data Catalog. The problem is a partition metadata entry that still points to an empty S3 prefix, so the data engineer must drop that partition.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run MSCK REPAIR TABLE to remove the stale status=pending partition from the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata</strong> - MSCK REPAIR TABLE can discover Hive-style partitions that exist in Amazon S3 and add missing partition metadata to the Data Catalog. It is commonly used when new partition folders are added outside Athena.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is that MSCK REPAIR TABLE does not remove stale partitions after data is deleted or moved from S3. It is not the right command for cleaning up a partition that still exists in metadata but no longer has data in its S3 location.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION for the status=completed prefix and leverage Athena to automatically remove the empty status=pending partition</strong> - ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION can register a new partition location in the Data Catalog. This is useful when a new S3 prefix exists and Athena needs metadata for that new partition.</p>\n\n<p>Adding the status=completed partition does not remove the stale status=pending partition. Athena will not automatically delete the old partition metadata simply because another partition was added.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-drop-partition.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-drop-partition.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/troubleshooting-athena.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/troubleshooting-athena.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-add-partition.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-add-partition.html</a></p>"},{"id":20,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A retail company recently migrated to a data lake design based on Amazon S3. Amazon Redshift and Amazon QuickSight are being used by the company's data engineering team to analyze data for better insights. To ensure access to the most up-to-date actionable data, the team has now shifted to a nightly Amazon Redshift refresh utilizing terabytes of the previous day's changes. The team has noticed that post the switchover to nightly refresh, several popular dashboards that had good performance earlier, are now seeing degraded performance during business hours as well. Amazon CloudWatch shows no notifications regarding the performance metrics.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following represents the MOST LIKELY cause for this issue?</p>","answers":["<p>The nightly data refreshes left the dashboard tables in need of a vacuum operation that could not be automatically performed by Amazon Redshift due to ongoing user workloads</p>","<p>The Redshift cluster is undersized for the queries being executed by the dashboards</p>","<p>The nightly data refreshes are causing the queries to hang during the business hours</p>","<p>Inefficient SQL queries are being run by the dashboards</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>The nightly data refreshes left the dashboard tables in need of a vacuum operation that could not be automatically performed by Amazon Redshift due to ongoing user workloads</strong></p>\n\n<p>The Redshift vacuum operation re-sorts rows and reclaims space in either a specified table or all tables in the current database. Amazon Redshift does not reclaim free space automatically. Such available space is created whenever you delete or update rows on a table. This process is a design choice inherited from PostgreSQL and a routine maintenance process that you need to follow for your tables to maximize the performance of your Redshift cluster. By running a vacuum command on one of your tables, you can reclaim any free space that is the result of delete and update operations. At the same time, the data of the table gets sorted. Thus, you end up with a compact and sorted table, which is useful for the performance of your cluster.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/7a346a5bbc95e673658721b269363543a7901b4d366ea38dd69b5c583b26d6c1.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/7a346a5bbc95e673658721b269363543a7901b4d366ea38dd69b5c583b26d6c1.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>You should note that the automatic vacuum operations can pause if the cluster experiences a period of high load. So, in case the cluster continues to see consistently high loads during the nightly refresh, the vacuum operations won't complete, thereby causing persistent performance degradation.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/dbd71f0b61354371ac016dd74586fa7552eeb47ef0c2bfc4d4cbedae3d7b9e51.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/dbd71f0b61354371ac016dd74586fa7552eeb47ef0c2bfc4d4cbedae3d7b9e51.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>The nightly data refreshes are causing the queries to hang during the business hours</strong> - The nightly data refreshes cannot cause the queries to hang during the business hours since these refreshes are carried out via ETL steps that could use long-running queries such as a COPY command. This can only cause the queries to hang for the duration of the nightly refresh and not during business hours.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/6787eaf13fe74908f1d1f3510b78e9778be35fc40a5c390d17ae9ade4105791b.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/6787eaf13fe74908f1d1f3510b78e9778be35fc40a5c390d17ae9ade4105791b.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/queries-troubleshooting.html#queries-troubleshooting-query-hangs\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/queries-troubleshooting.html#queries-troubleshooting-query-hangs</a><p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Inefficient SQL queries are being run by the dashboards</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>The Redshift cluster is undersized for the queries being executed by the dashboards</strong></p>\n\n<p>These two options have been added as distractors. Had inefficient SQL queries or an undersized Redshift cluster been the reasons behind the cluster's performance deterioration, these would have impacted the cluster even before the switchover to the nightly refresh process. Therefore, both these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2018/12/amazon-redshift-automatic-vacuum/\">https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2018/12/amazon-redshift-automatic-vacuum/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/queries-troubleshooting.html#queries-troubleshooting-query-hangs\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/queries-troubleshooting.html#queries-troubleshooting-query-hangs</a></p>"},{"id":21,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A retail company uses Amazon RDS to store sales data. For the analytics workloads that require high performance, only the last six months of data (approximately 50 TB) will be frequently queried. At the end of each month, the monthly sales data will be merged with the historical sales data for the last 5 years, which should also be available for analysis. The CTO at the company is looking at a cost-optimal solution that offers the best performance for this use case.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following would you select for the given requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Export RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Load and store the last six months of data from S3 in Amazon Redshift. Configure an Amazon Redshift Spectrum table to connect to all the historical data in S3</p>","<p>Use AWS data pipeline to incrementally load the last six months of data into Amazon Redshift and execute more frequent queries on Redshift. Set up a read replica of the RDS database to run queries on the historical data</p>","<p>Configure a read replica of the RDS database to store the last six months of data and execute more frequent queries on the read replica. Export RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Configure an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the historical data in S3</p>","<p>Export RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Configure an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the entire data in S3</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Export RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Load and store the last six months of data from S3 in Amazon Redshift. Configure an Amazon Redshift Spectrum table to connect to all the historical data in S3</strong></p>\n\n<p>You can use the AWS data pipeline to automate the movement and transformation of data. It allows you to quickly define a dependent chain of data sources, destinations, and predefined or custom data processing activities called a pipeline. Based on a schedule you define, your pipeline regularly performs processing activities such as distributed data copy, SQL transforms, MapReduce applications, or custom scripts against destinations such as Amazon S3, Amazon RDS, Amazon Redshift or Amazon DynamoDB.</p>\n\n<p>You can use the AWS data pipeline to set up a one-time export of RDS data to S3 as well as an ongoing incremental copy of RDS data to S3. You can also use the AWS data pipeline to load data from S3 to Redshift.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/8a19ffcd234507dc3ed34c18184ecc7bc2c469fc6fc763aaaf99f4bcab2f744e.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/8a19ffcd234507dc3ed34c18184ecc7bc2c469fc6fc763aaaf99f4bcab2f744e.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/what-is-datapipeline.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/what-is-datapipeline.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, scalable data warehouse that enables secure analytics at scale. For the given use case, you can use the AWS data pipeline to load data from S3 to Redshift and then use Redshift to query the last six months of data to ensure high performance. Further, you can use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the entire historical data in S3 which would be a cost-effective solution to facilitate analysis of the historical data.</p>\n\n<p>Using Redshift Spectrum, Amazon Redshift customers can easily query their data in Amazon S3. Redshift Spectrum is a built-in feature of Amazon Redshift, and your existing queries and BI tools will continue to work seamlessly. Under the hood, AWS manages a fleet of thousands of Redshift Spectrum nodes spread across multiple Availability Zones. These are transparently scaled and allocated to your queries based on the data that you need to process, with no provisioning or commitments. Redshift Spectrum is also highly concurrent—you can access your Amazon S3 data from any number of Amazon Redshift clusters.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/77cf4bb8f4d46ffadc3ce6c624024687d2e60529003fe834c0ed0c6a27766b73.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/77cf4bb8f4d46ffadc3ce6c624024687d2e60529003fe834c0ed0c6a27766b73.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure a read replica of the RDS database to store the last six months of data and execute more frequent queries on the read replica. Export RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Configure an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the historical data in S3</strong> - You cannot use RDS to facilitate queries on the last six months of data as it would not match the high performance offered by Redshift, which is a better option for the given requirement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS data pipeline to incrementally load the last six months of data into Amazon Redshift and execute more frequent queries on Redshift. Set up a read replica of the RDS database to run queries on the historical data</strong> - Using a read replica on RDS to store and analyze the entire historical data would turn out to be costlier than storing the data on S3 and enable analysis using Redshift spectrum. So this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Export RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Configure an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the entire data in S3</strong> - It is certainly possible to query the entire data in S3 using Athena, however, it will not be able to match the high performance offered by Redshift to query the last six months of data. So this option is not the best fit for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/what-is-datapipeline.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/what-is-datapipeline.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-template-incrementalcopyrdstos3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-template-incrementalcopyrdstos3.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-template-redshiftrdsincremental.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-template-redshiftrdsincremental.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-copydata-redshift-create.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-copydata-redshift-create.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/</a></p>"},{"id":22,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A global manufacturing company is creating a new Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio domain for its analytics and AI teams. Each division must independently manage its own projects, assets, metadata, and permissions. Some approved datasets must be discoverable and shareable with other divisions through a governed request and approval process. The company also requires centralized user authentication and mapping between identities and the appropriate organizational units.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Create one shared project for all divisions in the SageMaker Unified Studio domain, use project membership to separate assets and metadata, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and instruct project owners to manually track dataset access approvals outside the domain</p>","<p>Configure each division as a domain unit with owner permissions, restrict projects so only owners can access assets, prevent sharing across domain units, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and map user profiles to the corresponding domain units</p>","<p>Configure each division as a domain unit, manage permissions for assets, projects, and metadata within each unit, use AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, map user profiles to the appropriate domain units, and enable cross-division sharing through access requests that domain unit owners approve or deny</p>","<p>Configure each division as a separate SageMaker Unified Studio domain, isolate administrative policies across domains, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and delegate administration at the domain level</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure each division as a domain unit, manage permissions for assets, projects, and metadata within each unit, use AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, map user profiles to the appropriate domain units, and enable cross-division sharing through access requests that domain unit owners approve or deny</strong></p>\n\n<p>Domain units in Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio support organizational separation within a domain. A company can represent divisions or business units as domain units so that each unit can manage permissions, projects, assets, metadata, and governance responsibilities within its own scope.</p>\n\n<p>SageMaker Unified Studio supports governed asset sharing through discovery and access request workflows. Users can discover assets across domain units, request access to specific datasets, and rely on domain unit owners or authorized approvers to approve or deny those requests. This meets the requirement for sharing selected datasets without removing governance controls.</p>\n\n<p>AWS IAM Identity Center provides centralized authentication for Identity Center-based SageMaker Unified Studio domains. User profiles map identities to the domain so the company can associate users with the appropriate domain units and apply the right governance policies. This combination satisfies isolated business-unit control, governed sharing, and centralized identity management.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/2420d98570c2c6ee92575f027768543335328f56312e17369ebefc0428862482.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/2420d98570c2c6ee92575f027768543335328f56312e17369ebefc0428862482.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0b80d922fbe20ee4b2cc054781ed57f829f71472fcbaf705477481f87addaf48.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/0b80d922fbe20ee4b2cc054781ed57f829f71472fcbaf705477481f87addaf48.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure each division as a separate SageMaker Unified Studio domain, isolate administrative policies across domains, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and delegate administration at the domain level</strong> - Separate domains can isolate environments, users, and administrative boundaries. However, the requirement asks for each division to have isolated control within a new SageMaker Unified Studio domain, not for each division to run its own separate domain.</p>\n\n<p>This option also makes governed sharing across divisions harder because the divisions do not operate as domain units inside the same organizational structure. Domain units provide the intended model for business-unit separation and delegated governance within a domain.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure each division as a domain unit with owner permissions, restrict projects so only owners can access assets, prevent sharing across domain units, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and map user profiles to the corresponding domain units</strong> - This option correctly uses domain units and IAM Identity Center. However, it prevents sharing across domain units, which conflicts with the requirement to share specific datasets with other business units upon approval.</p>\n\n<p>The company needs both isolation and governed collaboration. Restricting projects exclusively to owners blocks the subscription and access request workflow that enables controlled sharing of approved datasets.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create one shared project for all divisions in the SageMaker Unified Studio domain, use project membership to separate assets and metadata, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and instruct project owners to manually track dataset access approvals outside the domain</strong> - A single shared project does not provide isolated control over each division's assets, projects, and metadata. Project membership can control access within a project, but it does not represent the intended business-unit governance boundary.</p>\n\n<p>Manual approval tracking outside the domain also weakens governance and auditability. The company needs a built-in access request and approval process that domain unit owners can manage, not an external manual process.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/concepts.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/concepts.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/working-with-domains.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/working-with-domains.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/user-management.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/user-management.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/catalog-iam-manage-access.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/catalog-iam-manage-access.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/security-accesss-control-patterns.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/security-accesss-control-patterns.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/</a></p>"},{"id":23,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A utilities company collects a large volume of smart meter telemetry from thousands of field devices. The company must retain the telemetry data indefinitely for historical analysis and regulatory reporting. Analysts use an Amazon Redshift cluster to analyze the data, and the company wants the most cost-effective storage and query architecture.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?</p>","answers":["<p>Store the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format and configure query access by using Amazon Redshift Spectrum</p>","<p>Store the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in JSON format and configure auto-copy ingestion from Amazon S3 into the Redshift cluster</p>","<p>Store the telemetry data directly in Amazon Redshift managed storage, compress the tables with automatic compression encodings, and resize the cluster as historical data volume increases</p>","<p>Store the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format and configure auto-copy ingestion from Amazon S3 into the Redshift cluster</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format and configure query access by using Amazon Redshift Spectrum</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift Spectrum allows Amazon Redshift users to query data directly from Amazon S3. This lets the company keep indefinitely retained IoT telemetry in Amazon S3 instead of loading all historical data into Redshift managed storage.</p>\n\n<p>Apache Parquet is a columnar storage format. When analytical queries read only selected columns, Parquet can reduce the amount of data that Redshift Spectrum scans. This improves query efficiency and helps lower query cost compared with row-based formats such as JSON or CSV.</p>\n\n<p>This solution is the most cost-effective fit because it combines low-cost, durable Amazon S3 storage with Redshift-based analytics access. The company can retain data indefinitely in S3 and use Redshift Spectrum to query the data only when analysts need it, instead of continuously loading and storing all data inside the Redshift cluster.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the telemetry data directly in Amazon Redshift managed storage, compress the tables with automatic compression encodings, and resize the cluster as historical data volume increases</strong> - Amazon Redshift managed storage can store and analyze large datasets, and compression encodings can reduce table storage requirements. Resizing can also help the cluster handle larger workloads.</p>\n\n<p>However, storing all indefinite historical telemetry directly in Redshift can be more expensive than retaining the data in Amazon S3. This option also ties long-term storage growth to the Redshift environment instead of separating low-cost storage from query compute.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format and configure auto-copy ingestion from Amazon S3 into the Redshift cluster</strong> - Apache Parquet is an efficient format for analytical workloads, and Amazon Redshift auto-copy can automate loading files from Amazon S3 into Redshift. However, this option loads the telemetry data into the Redshift cluster.</p>\n\n<p>The company needs to retain a large amount of IoT sensor data indefinitely. Loading all historical data into Redshift managed storage can increase storage costs compared with keeping the data in Amazon S3 and querying it through Redshift Spectrum.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in JSON format and configure auto-copy ingestion from Amazon S3 into the Redshift cluster</strong> - Auto-copy ingestion can help automate data loading from Amazon S3 into Amazon Redshift. This can be useful when the data must become part of local Redshift tables for frequent, low-latency analytics.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option uses JSON and loads the data into the cluster. It combines a less efficient analytical file format with higher long-term Redshift storage usage, which is not the most cost-effective approach for indefinite retention.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-spectrum-data-files.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-spectrum-data-files.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-spectrum-external-performance.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-spectrum-external-performance.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/data-lake.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/data-lake.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/10-best-practices-for-amazon-redshift-spectrum/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/10-best-practices-for-amazon-redshift-spectrum/</a></p>"},{"id":24,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A sports analytics firm uses AWS DynamoDB to store information about user's favorite sports teams and allows the information to be searchable from their home page. The data engineering team at the firm has a requirement wherein all 1 million records in a DynamoDB staging table should be deleted and then re-loaded at 3:00 AM each night.</p>\n\n<p>Which option is an efficient way to delete with minimal costs?</p>","answers":["<p>Use the purge table option</p>","<p>Delete then re-create the table</p>","<p>Scan and delete items using batch mode</p>","<p>Scan and delete items individually</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Delete then re-create the table</strong></p>\n\n<p>The DeleteTable operation deletes a table and all of its items. After a <code>DeleteTable</code> request, the specified table is in the <code>DELETING</code> state until DynamoDB completes the deletion. This is the most cost-efficient option.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Scan and delete items using batch mode</strong> - Scan is a very slow operation for 10 million items and this is not the best-fit option for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Scan and delete items individually</strong> - Scan is a very slow operation for 10 million items and this is not the best-fit option for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use the purge table option</strong> - This is a made-up option and has been added as a distractor.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/dynamodb/delete-table.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/dynamodb/delete-table.html</a></p>"},{"id":25,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A grocery delivery company stores current-day order transactions in an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The company stores historical fulfillment and sales analytics in Amazon Redshift. Reporting teams need daily dashboards that combine live order activity with historical demand patterns, delivery trends, and customer segments. The company needs near real-time insights while minimizing data transfer costs and maintenance overhead.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Athena federated queries to query the RDS for MySQL database, unload historical Redshift tables to Amazon S3 each day, and join both datasets through Athena views</p>","<p>Configure AWS DMS to continuously replicate the RDS for MySQL database into Amazon Redshift and run consolidated dashboard queries in Redshift</p>","<p>Use Amazon Redshift federated queries to directly query current-day RDS for MySQL transactions and join the results with existing Redshift historical tables</p>","<p>Export current-day RDS for MySQL data to Amazon S3 on a schedule, load the files into Redshift staging tables, and join the staged data with historical tables</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Redshift federated queries to directly query current-day RDS for MySQL transactions and join the results with existing Redshift historical tables</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift federated queries allow Redshift to query live operational data in Amazon RDS for MySQL. The reporting query can retrieve current-day transactions from the MySQL database and join those rows with historical analytics tables that already exist in Redshift.</p>\n\n<p>This approach supports near real-time insights because the company does not need to wait for a scheduled batch extract, file export, or replication load before current-day transactions become available for reporting. The query can access the latest operational records directly from the RDS database.</p>\n\n<p>This solution also minimizes data transfer costs and maintenance overhead. The company avoids building and operating an incremental ETL pipeline, maintaining staging tables, exporting files to Amazon S3, or continuously copying all current-day transactions into Redshift only to support combined reporting.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure AWS DMS to continuously replicate the RDS for MySQL database into Amazon Redshift and run consolidated dashboard queries in Redshift</strong> - AWS DMS can continuously replicate data from an Amazon RDS for MySQL source into Amazon Redshift. This can be useful when the company needs a persistent analytical copy of operational data in the warehouse.</p>\n\n<p>However, this solution introduces a replication task, target table management, change processing, monitoring, and additional data movement. The requirement emphasizes near real-time insights with minimal transfer cost and maintenance, which makes Redshift federated queries a better fit for joining live MySQL data with existing Redshift history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Athena federated queries to query the RDS for MySQL database, unload historical Redshift tables to Amazon S3 each day, and join both datasets through Athena views</strong> - Athena federated queries can query external data sources, and Athena can query data stored in Amazon S3. This can support some cross-source serverless analytics patterns.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option requires unloading Redshift historical data to S3 each day before Athena can join it with MySQL data. That adds extra movement, scheduled data preparation, and maintenance when Redshift federated queries can perform the join directly from the Redshift environment.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Export current-day RDS for MySQL data to Amazon S3 on a schedule, load the files into Redshift staging tables, and join the staged data with historical tables</strong> - Exporting MySQL data to Amazon S3 and loading it into Redshift with COPY can support warehouse reporting. This pattern can work for batch analytics when data latency requirements are relaxed.</p>\n\n<p>However, the company needs near real-time insights. Scheduled exports and staging loads introduce delay and operational work. This option also moves data through S3 and Redshift staging tables before users can analyze it.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/federated-query.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/federated-query.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/getting-started-federated-mysql.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/getting-started-federated-mysql.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/federated-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/federated-overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/federated_query_example.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/federated_query_example.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-amazon-redshift-federated-querying-to-amazon-aurora-mysql-and-amazon-rds-for-mysql/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-amazon-redshift-federated-querying-to-amazon-aurora-mysql-and-amazon-rds-for-mysql/</a></p>"},{"id":26,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A logistics company stores time-sensitive delivery event data in Amazon S3. The company partitions the dataset by event_date and event_hour, and analysts use Amazon Athena with AWS Glue Data Catalog tables to run near real-time delivery performance analysis. During an investigation, a data engineer notices that some recently arrived records do not appear in Athena query results, and some partitioned query results seem out of date. The source files exist in the correct S3 prefixes. The data engineer must identify the most likely cause of the issue.</p>\n\n<p>What is the likely cause?</p>","answers":["<p>The S3 bucket does not have versioning enabled, so Athena cannot identify the latest delivery event objects in each partition</p>","<p>The AWS Glue Data Catalog does not include the latest S3 partition changes, so Athena queries do not read all current partition locations</p>","<p>The Athena workgroup stores query results in the same S3 bucket, so Athena replaces recent delivery records with cached query output</p>","<p>The AWS Glue job schedules overlap, so Athena automatically reads older partitions until all overlapping jobs finish successfully</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>The AWS Glue Data Catalog does not include the latest S3 partition changes, so Athena queries do not read all current partition locations</strong></p>\n\n<p>Athena uses table and partition metadata from the AWS Glue Data Catalog when it queries partitioned data in Amazon S3. If new S3 prefixes exist but the corresponding partitions are not present or updated in the Data Catalog, Athena will not automatically know to include those locations in query planning.</p>\n\n<p>This explains why the source files exist in Amazon S3 but some records are missing or out of date in query results. The physical data can be present, but Athena depends on current catalog metadata to discover which partitions to scan.</p>\n\n<p>The data engineer should update the partition metadata by using an AWS Glue crawler, Athena partition repair for Hive-style partitions, partition projection, or explicit partition registration through Data Catalog APIs or DDL statements. Keeping S3 partition layout and catalog metadata synchronized is essential for time-sensitive analysis.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>The S3 bucket does not have versioning enabled, so Athena cannot identify the latest delivery event objects in each partition</strong> - S3 Versioning preserves multiple versions of objects in a bucket. It helps recover from accidental overwrites or deletions and can support data protection requirements.</p>\n\n<p>However, Athena does not require S3 Versioning to read the latest objects from a partitioned dataset. Missing or stale Athena results are more likely caused by stale partition metadata than by the absence of versioning.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Athena workgroup stores query results in the same S3 bucket, so Athena replaces recent delivery records with cached query output</strong> - Athena workgroups can define query result locations, encryption settings, and usage controls. Query result locations should be managed carefully so they do not interfere with source datasets.</p>\n\n<p>However, Athena does not replace source delivery records with cached query output just because a workgroup writes results to Amazon S3. Query result storage is separate from the partition metadata problem described in the scenario.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The AWS Glue job schedules overlap, so Athena automatically reads older partitions until all overlapping jobs finish successfully</strong> - Overlapping AWS Glue jobs can create operational issues if multiple jobs write to the same output location or update the same metadata at the same time. In some pipelines, scheduling conflicts can cause incomplete outputs.</p>\n\n<p>However, Athena does not automatically choose older partitions because Glue job schedules overlap. The most likely issue is that the Glue Data Catalog does not reflect the latest partition changes in Amazon S3.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/updates-and-partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/updates-and-partitions.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/</a></p>"},{"id":27,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>The content division at a digital media agency has an application that generates a large number of files on Amazon S3, each approximately 10 megabytes in size. The agency mandates that the files be stored for 5 years before they can be deleted. The files are frequently accessed in the first 30 days of the object creation but are rarely accessed after the first 30 days, however, immediate accessibility is always required. The files contain critical business data that is not easy to reproduce.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution is the MOST cost-effective for the given use case?</p>","answers":["<p>Set up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation</p>","<p>Set up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. Archive the files to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive 5 years after object creation</p>","<p>Set up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation</p>","<p>Set up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 Standard-IA class is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. Amazon S3 Standard-IA offers the high durability, high throughput, and low latency of S3 Standard, with a low per gigabyte storage price and per GB retrieval charge.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/5d4d73ba8b829486bba876b32a0211071d313b1827ac37ecda7849d7e5bdf6dc.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/5d4d73ba8b829486bba876b32a0211071d313b1827ac37ecda7849d7e5bdf6dc.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>For the given use case, you can set up an Amazon S3 lifecycle configuration and create a transition action to move objects from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. You can set up an expiration action to delete the object 5 years after object creation.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/1948a8626e6be6766687c924d4bbcea318f2996c396c6c49b5aa33c6260003e2.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/1948a8626e6be6766687c924d4bbcea318f2996c396c6c49b5aa33c6260003e2.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/ef2a4775362c3b9aa48a71effa3344ab51572a5ce362d9f682815b87a494787b.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/ef2a4775362c3b9aa48a71effa3344ab51572a5ce362d9f682815b87a494787b.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation</strong> - Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval storage class has the best case retrieval time of the order of minutes, so this option is incorrect for the given requirement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. Archive the files to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive 5 years after object creation</strong> - The files can simply be deleted 5 years after object creation instead of archiving the files to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive. There is no need to incur the cost of archival.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation</strong> - Unlike other Amazon S3 Storage Classes which store data in a minimum of three Availability Zones (AZs), Amazon S3 One Zone-IA stores data in a single AZ and costs 20% less than Amazon S3 Standard-IA. Amazon S3 One Zone-IA is a good choice for storing secondary backup copies of on-premises data or easily re-creatable data. The given scenario clearly states that the business-critical data is not easy to reproduce, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/glacier/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/glacier/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html</a></p>"},{"id":28,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A media analytics company needs to process streaming clickstream data by using Apache Spark in a Kubernetes environment. The company already runs shared platform services on Amazon EKS and wants the new streaming workload to integrate with that existing Kubernetes infrastructure.</p>\n\n<p>The solution must support event-driven scaling when streaming volume increases and must reduce unused compute capacity when traffic decreases. The company wants to optimize resource utilization while minimizing the operational work required to run Spark on Kubernetes.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon EMR on EKS with Kubernetes Cluster Autoscaler to scale Spark applications only from Kubernetes Metrics Server resource metrics</p>","<p>Use Amazon EMR on EKS with Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling to scale Spark streaming applications based on event-source demand</p>","<p>Use native Amazon EKS Horizontal Pod Autoscaler with Kubernetes Metrics Server to scale self-managed Spark driver and executor pods for streaming workloads</p>","<p>Run Apache Spark on Amazon EKS with AWS Auto Scaling groups and custom scaling policies based only on Kubernetes Metrics Server data</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon EMR on EKS with Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling to scale Spark streaming applications based on event-source demand</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon EMR on EKS is the best fit because it lets the company run Apache Spark workloads on existing Amazon EKS infrastructure. The company can use Amazon EMR capabilities for Spark job execution instead of operating a fully self-managed Spark runtime on Kubernetes.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/88aad2bf9543cfa47af3180daafd4c9bc09873af32c3fa0a3bee16e737cb3ea4.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/88aad2bf9543cfa47af3180daafd4c9bc09873af32c3fa0a3bee16e737cb3ea4.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-on-EKS-DevelopmentGuide/emr-eks.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-on-EKS-DevelopmentGuide/emr-eks.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling, or KEDA, supports event-driven scaling for Kubernetes workloads. It can scale workloads based on event sources or custom metrics, which aligns with the requirement to increase Spark processing capacity when streaming demand rises.</p>\n\n<p>This option provides the least operational overhead among the choices because Amazon EMR on EKS reduces Spark platform management work, and KEDA addresses event-driven scaling. The company can integrate with its existing EKS environment while optimizing resource utilization for variable streaming traffic.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/ab24ad59b39237516036d554c40d6c17146b2b2fcff10bd232b87500782ae851.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/ab24ad59b39237516036d554c40d6c17146b2b2fcff10bd232b87500782ae851.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/f48d8863ca3a192878728d996c26e72d66b86f4e150f3b3349f27e1deadc5e5e.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/f48d8863ca3a192878728d996c26e72d66b86f4e150f3b3349f27e1deadc5e5e.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use native Amazon EKS Horizontal Pod Autoscaler with Kubernetes Metrics Server to scale self-managed Spark driver and executor pods for streaming workloads</strong> - Amazon EKS Horizontal Pod Autoscaler can scale Kubernetes pods based on observed metrics such as CPU or memory utilization. This can help with some containerized workloads that have predictable resource-based scaling behavior.</p>\n\n<p>This option does not meet the requirement as well as EMR on EKS with KEDA because it relies on self-managed Spark and resource-based scaling instead of event-driven scaling. The company would need to manage Spark deployment, Spark runtime operations, and scaling behavior, which adds operational overhead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run Apache Spark on Amazon EKS with AWS Auto Scaling groups and custom scaling policies based only on Kubernetes Metrics Server data</strong> - AWS Auto Scaling groups can add or remove EC2 capacity behind Kubernetes worker nodes. Kubernetes Metrics Server can provide resource metrics such as CPU and memory usage.</p>\n\n<p>This does not provide the best event-driven scaling model for Spark applications. It also requires custom scaling logic and does not use Amazon EMR on EKS to reduce Spark management overhead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon EMR on EKS with Kubernetes Cluster Autoscaler to scale Spark applications only from Kubernetes Metrics Server resource metrics</strong> - Amazon EMR on EKS helps run Spark workloads on EKS, and Kubernetes Cluster Autoscaler can adjust node capacity when pods cannot be scheduled.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because Cluster Autoscaler reacts primarily to cluster scheduling needs, not directly to event-source demand. The requirement specifically calls for event-driven scaling, which KEDA addresses more directly.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-on-EKS-DevelopmentGuide/emr-eks.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-on-EKS-DevelopmentGuide/emr-eks.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/emr/features/eks/\">https://aws.amazon.com/emr/features/eks/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/solutions/event-driven-application-autoscaling-with-keda-on-amazon-eks/\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/solutions/event-driven-application-autoscaling-with-keda-on-amazon-eks/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/autoscaling-kubernetes-workloads-with-keda-using-amazon-managed-service-for-prometheus-metrics/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/autoscaling-kubernetes-workloads-with-keda-using-amazon-managed-service-for-prometheus-metrics/</a></p>"},{"id":29,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>An Amazon Athena query is lagging in performance. The query has a join on two tables using the equality condition. You have been hired as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to optimize the query. Upon analysis, you have noticed that one of the tables is large with millions of rows of data and the other table is small.</p>\n\n<p>What do you recommend to address the given requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Update the Athena workgroup settings for the given query, so it has access to the most stable version of Athena engine</p>","<p>Update the Athena workgroup settings for the given query, so it has access to the latest version of Athena engine</p>","<p>Specify the larger table on the left side of the join and the smaller table on the right side</p>","<p>Specify the smaller table on the left side of the join and the larger table on the right side</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Specify the larger table on the left side of the join and the smaller table on the right side</strong></p>\n\n<p>Choosing the right join order is critical for better query performance. When you join two tables using the equality condition, specify the larger table on the left side of the join and the smaller table on the right side. For the most common type of joins that use equality conditions, Athena builds a lookup table from the table on the right and distributes it to the worker nodes. It then streams the table on the left, joining rows by looking up matching values in the lookup table. This is called a distributed hash join. Because the lookup table built from the table on the right side is kept in memory, the smaller that table is, the less memory will be used, and the faster the join will run.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/e34ab0d49a2028eb84a0646f6fad1e6f4a0c694becb521e92c72f006cd51c023.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/e34ab0d49a2028eb84a0646f6fad1e6f4a0c694becb521e92c72f006cd51c023.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Update the Athena workgroup settings for the given query, so it has access to the latest version of Athena engine</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Update the Athena workgroup settings for the given query, so it has access to the most stable version of Athena engine</strong></p>\n\n<p>You can use workgroups to separate users, teams, applications, or workloads, to set limits on the amount of data each query or the entire workgroup can process, and to track costs. Because workgroups act as resources, you can use resource-level identity-based policies to control access to a specific workgroup. You can also view query-related metrics in Amazon CloudWatch, control costs by configuring limits on the amount of data scanned, create thresholds, and trigger actions, such as Amazon SNS, when these thresholds are breached.</p>\n\n<p>Athena occasionally releases a new engine version to provide improved performance, functionality, and code fixes. When a new engine version is available, Athena notifies you in the console. You can choose to let Athena decide when to upgrade, or manually specify an Athena engine version per workgroup.</p>\n\n<p>These two options have been added as distractors since the version of the Athena engine used for the query is not relevant to the given use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Specify the smaller table on the left side of the join and the larger table on the right side</strong> - This violates the aforementioned recommendation for optimizing join queries having two tables of asymmetric sizes.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning.html</a></p>"},{"id":30,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A logistics company has multiple AWS accounts hosting its portfolio of IT applications that serve the company's retail and enterprise customers. A CloudWatch Logs agent is installed on each of the EC2 instances running these IT applications. The company wants to aggregate all security events in a centralized AWS account dedicated to log storage. The centralized operations team at the company needs to perform near-real-time gathering and collating events across multiple AWS accounts.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following solutions would you recommend to address these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Set up CloudWatch Logs streams in each application AWS account to forward events to CloudWatch Logs in the centralized logging AWS account. In the centralized logging AWS account, subscribe a Kinesis Data Firehose stream to Amazon CloudWatch Events and further use the Firehose stream to store the log data in S3</p>","<p>Set up a new IAM role in each application AWS account with permissions to view CloudWatch Logs. Create a Lambda function to assume this new role and perform an hourly export of each AWS account's CloudWatch Logs data to an S3 bucket in the centralized logging AWS account</p>","<p>Set up CloudWatch Logs agents to publish data to a Kinesis Data Firehose stream in the centralized logging AWS account. Create a Lambda function to read messages from the stream and push messages to Kinesis Data Firehose and then store the data in S3</p>","<p>Set up Kinesis Data Firehose in the logging account and then subscribe the delivery stream to CloudWatch Logs streams in each application AWS account via subscription filters. Persist the log data in an Amazon S3 bucket inside the logging AWS account</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up Kinesis Data Firehose in the logging account and then subscribe the delivery stream to CloudWatch Logs streams in each application AWS account via subscription filters. Persist the log data in an Amazon S3 bucket inside the logging AWS account</strong></p>\n\n<p>You can configure Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to aggregate and collate CloudWatch Logs from different AWS accounts and receive their log events in a centralized logging AWS Account (this is known as cross-account data sharing) by using a CloudWatch Logs destination and then creating a Subscription Filter. This log event data can be read from a centralized Amazon Kinesis Firehose delivery stream to perform downstream processing and analysis.</p>\n\n<p>You can collaborate with an owner of a different AWS account and receive their log events on your AWS resources, such as an Amazon Kinesis or Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose stream (this is known as cross-account data sharing). You can use a subscription filter with Kinesis Streams, Lambda, or Kinesis Data Firehose. Logs that are sent to a receiving service through a subscription filter are Base64 encoded and compressed with the gzip format.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up a new IAM role in each application AWS account with permissions to view CloudWatch Logs. Create a Lambda function to assume this new role and perform an hourly export of each AWS account's CloudWatch Logs data to an S3 bucket in the centralized logging AWS account</strong> - As the Lambda function is performing an hourly export, it's not a near-real-time solution. In addition, Lambda is not the right choice to build a high volume and high-velocity streaming solution which is better handled by using the Kinesis Family of services.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up CloudWatch Logs agents to publish data to a Kinesis Data Firehose stream in the centralized logging AWS account. Create a Lambda function to read messages from the stream and push messages to Kinesis Data Firehose and then store the data in S3</strong> - The CloudWatch Logs agent (on the path to deprecation) supports the collection of logs from only servers running Linux. It is recommended to use the unified CloudWatch agent. It enables you to collect both logs and advanced metrics with one agent. It offers support across operating systems, including servers running Windows Server. This agent also provides better performance. CloudWatch Logs agent cannot publish data to a Kinesis Data Firehose stream, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up CloudWatch Logs streams in each application AWS account to forward events to CloudWatch Logs in the centralized logging AWS account. In the centralized logging AWS account, subscribe a Kinesis Data Firehose stream to Amazon CloudWatch Events and further use the Firehose stream to store the log data in S3</strong> - You can use a subscription filter with Kinesis Streams, Lambda, or Kinesis Data Firehose. So you cannot just forward events directly to CloudWatch Logs in another account. In addition, the Kinesis Data Firehose stream cannot subscribe to CloudWatch Events, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/CrossAccountSubscriptions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/CrossAccountSubscriptions.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/stream-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-to-a-centralized-account-for-audit-and-analysis/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/stream-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-to-a-centralized-account-for-audit-and-analysis/</a></p>"},{"id":31,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>An Internet-of-Things (IoT) devices company uses Amazon S3 as the data lake to store the input data that is ingested from the field devices on an hourly basis. The ingested data has attributes such as the device type, ID of the device, the status of the device, the timestamp of the event, the source IP address, etc. The data runs into millions of records per day and the company wants to run complex analytical queries on this data on a daily basis for product improvements for each device type.</p>\n\n<p>Which is the most optimal way to save this data to get the best performance from the millions of data points saved on a daily basis?</p>","answers":["<p>Store the data in compressed .csv, partitioned by date and sorted by the status of the device</p>","<p>Store the data in Apache ORC, partitioned by date and sorted by device type of the device</p>","<p>Store the data in compressed .csv, partitioned by date and sorted by device type</p>","<p>Store the data in Apache Parquet, partitioned by device type and sorted by date</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the data in Apache ORC, partitioned by date and sorted by device type of the device</strong> - Apache Parquet and ORC are columnar storage formats that are optimized for fast retrieval of data and used in AWS analytical applications.</p>\n\n<p>By partitioning your data, you can restrict the amount of data scanned by each query, thus improving performance and reducing cost. You can partition your data by any key. A common practice is to partition the data based on time, often leading to a multi-level partitioning scheme. For example, a customer who has data coming in every hour might decide to partition by year, month, date, and hour. Another customer, who has data coming from many different sources but that is loaded only once per day, might partition by a data source identifier and date.</p>\n\n<p>For the given use case, as the company does daily analysis, so it only needs to look at the data generated for a given date. Hence partitioning by date offers significant performance and cost advantages. Since the company also wants to do analysis for product improvements for each device type, it is better to keep the data sorted by device type, so it allows faster query execution.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the data in Apache Parquet, partitioned by device type and sorted by date</strong> -  Apache Parquet is a columnar storage format that is optimized for fast retrieval of data and used in AWS analytical applications. However, partitioning by device type is incorrect for this use case, and partitioning by date is optimal.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the data in compressed .csv, partitioned by date and sorted by the status of the device</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the data in compressed .csv, partitioned by date and sorted by device type</strong></p>\n\n<p>Both the above options are not columnar storage formats, they are row-based formats that are not optimal for big data retrievals for complex analytical queries.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html</a></p>"},{"id":32,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A healthcare company collects user health information on a daily basis and stores this data on Amazon S3 which is then queried by Amazon Athena for analysis. Any user health information older than a week is never used in the queries. The data engineering team at the company has now set up a Glue crawler to automate the process but the crawler has been running for several hours and is still unable to identify the schema of the data store.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following options can be used to fix this issue?</p>","answers":["<p>Use an exclude pattern for the Glue crawler to filter out the unwanted files</p>","<p>Compressed files, like Apache Parquet, take longer to crawl. Instead, use uncompressed files</p>","<p>Change the <code>Scanning rate</code> parameter of Glue Crawler to a higher value for the Crawler to complete the scan faster</p>","<p>Split larger files into smaller ones, to reduce the overhead of reading large files for the Glue Crawler</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use an exclude pattern for the Glue crawler to filter out the unwanted files</strong></p>\n\n<p>An exclude pattern tells the crawler to skip certain files or paths. Exclude patterns reduce the number of files that the crawler must list, making the crawler run faster. For example, use an exclude pattern to exclude metafiles and files that have already been crawled.</p>\n\n<p>More on crawler exclude patterns:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/1a543ffa2005e8584f3d8babf21c0c1f4100cfb6ffe7521342f5fff4ce8bfe8b.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/1a543ffa2005e8584f3d8babf21c0c1f4100cfb6ffe7521342f5fff4ce8bfe8b.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/define-crawler.html#crawler-data-stores-exclude\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/define-crawler.html#crawler-data-stores-exclude</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Compressed files, like Apache Parquet, take longer to crawl. Instead, use uncompressed files</strong> - Compressed files take longer to crawl. That's because the crawler must download the file and decompress it before reading the first megabyte or listing the file. For Apache Parquet, Apache Avro, and Apache ORC files, the crawler doesn't crawl the first megabyte. Instead, the crawler reads the metadata stored in each file. So this option is not relevant for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Change the <code>Scanning rate</code> parameter of Glue Crawler to a higher value for the Crawler to complete the scan faster</strong> - Scanning rate specifies the percentage of the configured read capacity units to use by the AWS Glue crawler. This parameter is available only for DynamoDB data stores.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Split larger files into smaller ones, to reduce the overhead of reading large files for the Glue Crawler</strong> - This statement is incorrect. It takes more time to crawl a large number of small files than a small number of large files. That's because the crawler must list each file and must read the first megabyte of each new file.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/long-running-glue-crawler/\">https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/long-running-glue-crawler/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/define-crawler.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/define-crawler.html</a></p>"},{"id":33,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A travel booking company uses an Amazon RDS for Oracle database to store reservation, payment, customer, and support case data for an ecommerce platform. The company needs to make data from all database tables available for future analytics in a cost-effective storage layer. The company also needs to process changes from a table named refund_requests in near real time and send each relevant change to an external fraud review service through a REST API.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?</p>","answers":["<p>Create one AWS DMS task to send all Oracle table data to Amazon S3, create another AWS DMS task to stream refund_requests changes to Kinesis Data Streams, and use Lambda to call the REST API</p>","<p>Use Oracle export utilities on a scheduled EC2 instance to copy all tables to Amazon S3, then run a containerized service on Amazon ECS to poll refund_requests and call the REST API</p>","<p>Create an AWS DMS task to replicate the Oracle database to Amazon Redshift, use the Redshift Data API to query refund_requests changes, and unload all warehouse data to Amazon S3</p>","<p>Use AWS DMS to replicate the Oracle database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance, then run Lambda queries against the refund_requests table and call the REST API</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create one AWS DMS task to send all Oracle table data to Amazon S3, create another AWS DMS task to stream refund_requests changes to Kinesis Data Streams, and use Lambda to call the REST API</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Database Migration Service can replicate data from an Oracle source to Amazon S3. Amazon S3 is a cost-effective storage layer for future analytics because the company can store all database table data in a data lake and later query or process the data with analytics services.</p>\n\n<p>AWS DMS can also stream source database changes to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. The data engineer can configure table mappings so a separate DMS task captures changes from only the refund_requests table and sends those changes to a Kinesis data stream.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Lambda can consume records from Kinesis Data Streams and call the external REST API for near real-time processing. This solution is cost-effective because it uses Amazon S3 for broad analytical storage and a streaming path only for the table that needs immediate downstream processing.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/8de2dc02ddaa92fccca14eaf3b43db59eb1a65bf039296d4f2c2a7d92ba511d9.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/8de2dc02ddaa92fccca14eaf3b43db59eb1a65bf039296d4f2c2a7d92ba511d9.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/ae4bb4d64070c6c812a70a672ca48aa2f564726f5cd63fa6175413efb32464b5.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/ae4bb4d64070c6c812a70a672ca48aa2f564726f5cd63fa6175413efb32464b5.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS DMS to replicate the Oracle database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance, then run Lambda queries against the refund_requests table and call the REST API</strong> - AWS DMS can replicate data from Oracle to PostgreSQL, and Lambda can query a relational database if the function has the required network access and credentials. This could make the source data available in another database engine.</p>\n\n<p>However, creating another RDS database for all tables is less cost-effective than using Amazon S3 for future analytics storage. The Lambda query approach also requires polling or custom logic to identify new refund_requests changes near real time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an AWS DMS task to replicate the Oracle database to Amazon Redshift, use the Redshift Data API to query refund_requests changes, and unload all warehouse data to Amazon S3</strong> - AWS DMS can load data into Amazon Redshift, and Redshift can support analytics over structured data. The Redshift Data API can run SQL statements without requiring a persistent database connection.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option adds an Amazon Redshift warehouse when the requirement only says future analytics storage for all tables and near real-time REST API processing for one table. Querying Redshift for refund_requests changes and unloading data back to S3 adds unnecessary cost and operational complexity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Oracle export utilities on a scheduled EC2 instance to copy all tables to Amazon S3, then run a containerized service on Amazon ECS to poll refund_requests and call the REST API</strong> - Oracle export utilities and EC2-based automation could copy database contents to Amazon S3. A containerized service could also poll the source database or a derived store for refund_requests changes.</p>\n\n<p>However, this approach requires the company to manage EC2 instances, export scripts, container services, polling state, retries, and failure handling. AWS DMS, Kinesis Data Streams, Lambda, and Amazon S3 provide a more managed and cost-effective architecture for the stated requirements.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.S3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.S3.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.Kinesis.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.Kinesis.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Source.Oracle.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Source.Oracle.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Task.CDC.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Task.CDC.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/</a></p>"},{"id":34,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A company's data engineer is tasked with enhancing the performance of SQL table queries on data stored in an Amazon Redshift cluster. Due to budget constraints, expanding the cluster size is not an option. The company utilizes the EVEN distribution style for loading data across multiple tables, with some tables containing hundreds of GB of data while others have less than 20 MB.</p>\n\n<p>What solution would address these needs effectively?</p>","answers":["<p>Opt for ALL distribution style for large tables. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables</p>","<p>Update the distribution style for all tables to AUTO. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables</p>","<p>For the rarely updated small tables, opt for ALL distribution style. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables</p>","<p>Switch to Amazon Redshift Spectrum to efficiently query and retrieve data from the tables</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>For the rarely updated small tables, opt for ALL distribution style. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables</strong></p>\n\n<p>For ALL distribution, a copy of the entire table is distributed to every node. While EVEN distribution or KEY distribution places only a portion of a table's rows on each node, ALL distribution ensures that every row is collocated for every join that the table participates in.</p>\n\n<p>ALL distribution multiplies the storage required by the number of nodes in the cluster, so it takes much longer to load, update, or insert data into multiple tables. ALL distribution is appropriate only for relatively slow-moving tables; that is, tables that are not updated frequently or extensively. Because the cost of redistributing small tables during a query is low, there isn't a significant benefit to defining small dimension tables as DISTSTYLE ALL.</p>\n\n<p>Hence, ALL distribution is suited for the rarely updated small tables while maintaining the EVEN distribution style for the larger tables.</p>\n\n<p>Define primary key and foreign key constraints between tables wherever appropriate. Even though they are informational only, the query optimizer uses those constraints to generate more efficient query plans.</p>\n\n<p>Note: Do not define primary key and foreign key constraints unless your application enforces the constraints. Amazon Redshift does not enforce unique, primary-key, or foreign-key constraints.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Opt for ALL distribution style for large tables. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables</strong> - ALL distribution multiplies the storage required by the number of nodes in the cluster, and so it takes much longer to load, update, or insert data into multiple tables. ALL distribution is appropriate only for relatively slow-moving tables; that is, tables that are not updated frequently or extensively.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Update the distribution style for all tables to AUTO. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables</strong> - If a table is largely denormalized and does not participate in joins, or if you don't have a clear choice for another distribution style, use AUTO distribution. For this use case, AUTO is not an optimal choice since tables have clearly defined access patterns.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Switch to Amazon Redshift Spectrum to efficiently query and retrieve data from the tables</strong> - Using Amazon Redshift Spectrum, you can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. This option acts as a distractor since the given use case does not mention using Amazon S3 for storing data.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_designating_distribution_styles.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_designating_distribution_styles.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-defining-constraints.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-defining-constraints.html</a></p>"},{"id":35,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A company has hired you to help with redesigning a real-time data processor. The company wants to build custom applications that process and analyze the streaming data for its specialized needs.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will you recommend to address this use-case?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor</p>","<p>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor</p>","<p>Use Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor</p>","<p>Use Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is useful for rapidly moving data off data producers and then continuously processing the data, be it to transform the data before emitting to a data store, run real-time metrics and analytics, or derive more complex data streams for further processing. Kinesis data streams can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events.</p>\n\n<p>Kinesis Data Streams Overview:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/82bd94cc900d62610f42fd3b01a02e17d4b9fff9e0a797aefd3cdcd76aef70f6.png\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/82bd94cc900d62610f42fd3b01a02e17d4b9fff9e0a797aefd3cdcd76aef70f6.png\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor</strong> - Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) is a highly available, durable, secure, fully managed pub/sub messaging service that enables you to decouple microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. SNS cannot be used to decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor as described in the given use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor</strong> - Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) offers a secure, durable, and available hosted queue that lets you integrate and decouple distributed software systems and components. SQS cannot be used to decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor as described in the given use case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor</strong> - Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is the easiest way to load streaming data into data stores and analytics tools. Kinesis Firehose cannot be used to process and analyze the streaming data in custom applications. It can capture, transform, and load streaming data into Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon Elasticsearch Service, and Splunk, enabling near real-time analytics.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose Overview\n<img src=\"exams/assets/86656f260eb689aa4bb4bc87cbcb28cd537002c059d19be4f303f141c4d132e2.png\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/86656f260eb689aa4bb4bc87cbcb28cd537002c059d19be4f303f141c4d132e2.png\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/</a></p>"},{"id":36,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A financial technology company is building machine learning models to predict payment fraud. The company stores model training datasets as files in an Amazon S3 bucket. A data engineer must apply data quality rules to the training files when new datasets arrive. The solution must require the least operational overhead and must not require the team to write custom validation code.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Create an Amazon EMR provisioned cluster, install an open source Python data quality library, copy the S3 training files to the cluster, and run custom validation scripts</p>","<p>Create an AWS Glue DataBrew project for the S3 training dataset, create a data quality ruleset, run a profile job, and use EventBridge to start the job when new data arrives</p>","<p>Create Lambda functions to evaluate each data quality rule, use Step Functions to orchestrate the functions, and publish notifications when the training data fails validation</p>","<p>Use Amazon Athena scheduled queries to evaluate rule conditions against the S3 training files and write failed records to a separate S3 prefix for later inspection</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an AWS Glue DataBrew project for the S3 training dataset, create a data quality ruleset, run a profile job, and use EventBridge to start the job when new data arrives</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue DataBrew is designed for visual data preparation and data profiling without requiring users to write code. A data engineer can create a DataBrew project for the training dataset that is stored in Amazon S3 and define the required data quality rules in a ruleset.</p>\n\n<p>A DataBrew profile job can run the ruleset against the dataset and generate data quality validation results. This is well suited for ML training data because the company can validate completeness, valid values, ranges, patterns, uniqueness, and other quality expectations before the data enters model development workflows.</p>\n\n<p>EventBridge can trigger an automated workflow when new data arrives in the S3 bucket. This keeps the process automated while still avoiding custom validation code, custom orchestration, or cluster management, which makes it the lowest operational overhead option among the choices.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/9d04c770a2cd485d584316562781774cccf72e71b484b72d9c2e9856021d43de.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/9d04c770a2cd485d584316562781774cccf72e71b484b72d9c2e9856021d43de.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create Lambda functions to evaluate each data quality rule, use Step Functions to orchestrate the functions, and publish notifications when the training data fails validation</strong> - Lambda and Step Functions can build a flexible validation workflow. The team could create one function per rule or combine several rules into a larger validation function.</p>\n\n<p>However, this approach requires custom code for the validation logic, workflow design, error handling, retries, and notifications. The requirement asks for a solution that does not require new code and has the least operational overhead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Amazon EMR provisioned cluster, install an open source Python data quality library, copy the S3 training files to the cluster, and run custom validation scripts</strong> - Amazon EMR can run distributed data processing workloads, and open source Python packages can validate data quality when engineers write the required logic. This approach can be powerful for highly customized validation requirements.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option requires the company to manage a provisioned cluster, install and maintain libraries, copy or read data from S3, and write validation scripts. That is significantly more operational work than using DataBrew rulesets and profile jobs.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Athena scheduled queries to evaluate rule conditions against the S3 training files and write failed records to a separate S3 prefix for later inspection</strong> - Athena can query files in Amazon S3 and can evaluate rule conditions by using SQL. This can work for teams that already express validation logic as SQL queries.</p>\n\n<p>However, scheduled queries require the team to write and maintain SQL for every validation rule and manage failed-record outputs. This adds more operational effort than configuring DataBrew rulesets and running profile jobs.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules-create.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules-create.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-ruleset-create-a-profile-job.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-ruleset-create-a-profile-job.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/</a></p>"},{"id":37,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A data engineer is encountering slow query performance while executing Amazon Athena queries on datasets stored in an Amazon S3 bucket, with AWS Glue Data Catalog serving as the metadata repository. The data engineer has identified the root cause of the sluggish performance as the excessive number of partitions in the S3 bucket, leading to increased Athena query planning times.</p>\n\n<p>What are the two possible approaches to mitigate this issue and enhance query efficiency (Select two)?</p>","answers":["<p>Transform the data in each partition to Apache ORC format</p>","<p>Compress the files in gzip format to improve query performance against the partitions</p>","<p>Set up Athena partition projection based on the S3 bucket prefix</p>","<p>Perform bucketing on the data in each partition</p>","<p>Set up an AWS Glue partition index and leverage partition filtering via the GetPartitions call</p>"],"correct":[2,4],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up an AWS Glue partition index and leverage partition filtering via the GetPartitions call</strong></p>\n\n<p>When you create a partition index, you specify a list of partition keys that already exist on a given table. The partition index is sub list of partition keys defined in the table. A partition index can be created on any permutation of partition keys defined in the table. For the above sales_data table, the possible indexes are (country, category, creationDate), (country, category, year), (country, category), (country), (category, country, year, month), and so on.</p>\n\n<p>Let's take a sales_data table as an example which is partitioned by the keys Country, Category, Year, Month, and creationDate. If you want to obtain sales data for all the items sold for the Books category in the year 2020 after 2020-08-15, you have to make a GetPartitions request with the expression \"Category = 'Books' and creationDate &gt; '2020-08-15'\" to the Data Catalog.</p>\n\n<p>If no partition indexes are present on the table, AWS Glue loads all the partitions of the table and then filters the loaded partitions using the query expression provided by the user in the GetPartitions request. The query takes more time to run as the number of partitions increases on a table with no indexes. With an index, the GetPartitions query will try to fetch a subset of the partitions instead of loading all the partitions in the table.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/6abd850ed404dbc789c4078458678687ecf02c16653144e050f6d8144f3b7503.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/6abd850ed404dbc789c4078458678687ecf02c16653144e050f6d8144f3b7503.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/partition-indexes.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/partition-indexes.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up Athena partition projection based on the S3 bucket prefix</strong></p>\n\n<p>Processing partition information can be a bottleneck for Athena queries when you have a very large number of partitions and aren’t using AWS Glue partition indexing. You can use partition projection in Athena to speed up query processing of highly partitioned tables and automate partition management. Partition projection helps minimize this overhead by allowing you to query partitions by calculating partition information rather than retrieving it from a metastore. It eliminates the need to add partitions’ metadata to the AWS Glue table.</p>\n\n<p>In partition projection, partition values, and locations are calculated from configuration rather than read from a repository like the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Because in-memory operations are usually faster than remote operations, partition projection can reduce the runtime of queries against highly partitioned tables. Depending on the specific characteristics of the query and underlying data, partition projection can significantly reduce query runtime for queries that are constrained by partition metadata retrieval.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/a0257a3a7d6d24757f7f0b078bfc12e86c53a81de8dc04cc825e0893ef6f947a.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/a0257a3a7d6d24757f7f0b078bfc12e86c53a81de8dc04cc825e0893ef6f947a.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Transform the data in each partition to Apache ORC format</strong> - Apache ORC is a popular file format for analytics workloads. It is a columnar file format because it stores data not by row, but by column. ORC format also allows query engines to reduce the amount of data that needs to be loaded in different ways. For example, by storing and compressing columns separately, you can achieve higher compression ratios and only the columns referenced in a query need to be read. However, the data is being transformed within the existing partitions, this option does not resolve the root cause of under-performance (that is, the excessive number of partitions in the S3 bucket).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Compress the files in gzip format to improve query performance against the partitions</strong> - Compressing your data can speed up your queries significantly. The smaller data sizes reduce the data scanned from Amazon S3, resulting in lower costs of running queries. It also reduces the network traffic from Amazon S3 to Athena. Athena supports a variety of compression formats, including common formats like gzip, Snappy, and zstd. However, the data is being compressed within the existing partitions, this option does not resolve the root cause of under-performance (that is, the excessive number of partitions in the S3 bucket).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Perform bucketing on the data in each partition</strong> - Bucketing is a way to organize the records of a dataset into categories called buckets. This meaning of bucket and bucketing is different from, and should not be confused with Amazon S3 buckets. In data bucketing, records that have the same value for a property go into the same bucket. Records are distributed as evenly as possible among buckets so that each bucket has roughly the same amount of data. In practice, the buckets are files, and a hash function determines the bucket that a record goes into. A bucketed dataset will have one or more files per bucket per partition. The bucket that a file belongs to is encoded in the file name. Bucketing is useful when a dataset is bucketed by a certain property and you want to retrieve records in which that property has a certain value. Because the data is bucketed, Athena can use the value to determine which files to look at. For example, suppose a dataset is bucketed by customer_id and you want to find all records for a specific customer. Athena determines the bucket that contains those records and only reads the files in that bucket.</p>\n\n<p>Good candidates for bucketing occur when you have columns that have high cardinality (that is, have many distinct values), are uniformly distributed, and that you frequently query for specific values.</p>\n\n<p>Since bucketing is being done within the existing partitions, this option does not resolve the root cause of under-performance (that is, the excessive number of partitions in the S3 bucket).</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/partition-indexes.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/partition-indexes.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing.html</a></p>"},{"id":38,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A ride-sharing company wants to use an Amazon DynamoDB table for data storage. The table will not be used during the night hours whereas the read and write traffic will often be unpredictable during day hours. When traffic spikes occur they will happen very quickly.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following will you recommend as the best-fit solution?</p>","answers":["<p>Set up Amazon DynamoDB global table in the provisioned capacity mode</p>","<p>Set up Amazon DynamoDB table in the on-demand capacity mode</p>","<p>Set up Amazon DynamoDB table with a global secondary index</p>","<p>Set up Amazon DynamoDB table in the provisioned capacity mode with auto-scaling enabled</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up Amazon DynamoDB table in the on-demand capacity mode</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon DynamoDB has two read/write capacity modes for processing reads and writes on your tables:</p>\n\n<p>On-demand</p>\n\n<p>Provisioned (default, free-tier eligible)</p>\n\n<p>Amazon DynamoDB on-demand is a flexible billing option capable of serving thousands of requests per second without capacity planning. DynamoDB on-demand offers pay-per-request pricing for read and write requests so that you pay only for what you use.</p>\n\n<p>The on-demand mode is a good option if any of the following are true:</p>\n\n<p>You create new tables with unknown workloads.</p>\n\n<p>You have unpredictable application traffic.</p>\n\n<p>You prefer the ease of paying for only what you use.</p>\n\n<p>If you choose provisioned mode, you specify the number of reads and writes per second that you require for your application. You can use auto-scaling to adjust your table’s provisioned capacity automatically in response to traffic changes. This helps you govern your DynamoDB use to stay at or below a defined request rate to obtain cost predictability.</p>\n\n<p>Provisioned mode is a good option if any of the following are true:</p>\n\n<p>You have predictable application traffic.</p>\n\n<p>You run applications whose traffic is consistent or ramps gradually.</p>\n\n<p>You can forecast capacity requirements to control costs.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/a1cc3e16891f8f76f9a8b5f66051e5658cade5fe6bf2d929c51fea97ab05b36f.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/a1cc3e16891f8f76f9a8b5f66051e5658cade5fe6bf2d929c51fea97ab05b36f.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/amazon-dynamodb-auto-scaling-performance-and-cost-optimization-at-any-scale/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/amazon-dynamodb-auto-scaling-performance-and-cost-optimization-at-any-scale/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>With on-demand, Amazon DynamoDB instantly allocates capacity as it is needed. There is no concept of provisioned capacity, and there is no delay waiting for Amazon CloudWatch thresholds or the subsequent table updates. On-demand is ideal for bursty, new, or unpredictable workloads whose traffic can spike in seconds or minutes, and when underprovisioned capacity would impact the user experience. On-demand is a perfect solution if your team is moving to a NoOps or serverless environment.</p>\n\n<p>The given use case clearly states that when the traffic spikes occur they happen very quickly, thereby implying an unpredictable traffic pattern, therefore the on-demand capacity mode is the correct option for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up Amazon DynamoDB table in the provisioned capacity mode with auto-scaling enabled</strong> - As mentioned in the explanation above, you should use the provisioned capacity mode (even with auto-scaling) only when you have predictable application traffic.</p>\n\n<p>When you create Amazon DynamoDB table, auto-scaling is the default capacity setting, but you can also enable auto-scaling on any table that does not have it active. Behind the scenes, as illustrated in the following diagram, DynamoDB auto scaling uses a scaling policy in Application Auto Scaling. To configure auto-scaling in DynamoDB, you set the minimum and maximum levels of read and write capacity in addition to the target utilization percentage. Auto-scaling uses Amazon CloudWatch to monitor a table’s read and write capacity metrics. To do so, it creates CloudWatch alarms that track consumed capacity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up Amazon DynamoDB table with a global secondary index</strong> - A global secondary index (GSI) is an index with a partition key and a sort key that can be different from those on the base table. A global secondary index is considered \"global\" because queries on the index can span all of the data in the base table, across all partitions. A global secondary index is stored in its own partition space away from the base table and scales separately from the base table. GSI cannot be used to handle an unpredictable load on a DynamoDB table.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up Amazon DynamoDB global table in the provisioned capacity mode</strong> - Global tables build on the global Amazon DynamoDB footprint to provide you with a fully managed, multi-Region, and multi-active database that delivers fast, local, read and write performance for massively scaled, global applications. Global tables replicate your DynamoDB tables automatically across your choice of AWS Regions.</p>\n\n<p>Global tables eliminate the difficult work of replicating data between Regions and resolving update conflicts, enabling you to focus on your application's business logic. In addition, global tables enable your applications to stay highly available even in the unlikely event of isolation or degradation of an entire Region.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon DynamoDB global tables:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/1dd10b3e8a0ef24ffd9f0cbec943adf36630ffcdf99a121484ed3640c8b907a9.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/1dd10b3e8a0ef24ffd9f0cbec943adf36630ffcdf99a121484ed3640c8b907a9.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/global-tables/\">https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/global-tables/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Amazon DynamoDB global table cannot be used to handle an unpredictable load on a Amazon DynamoDB table.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.ReadWriteCapacityMode.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.ReadWriteCapacityMode.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/amazon-dynamodb-auto-scaling-performance-and-cost-optimization-at-any-scale/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/amazon-dynamodb-auto-scaling-performance-and-cost-optimization-at-any-scale/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/global-tables/\">https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/global-tables/</a></p>"},{"id":39,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>An e-commerce company performs analytics on the company's data using the Amazon Redshift cluster. The Redshift cluster has two important tables: the orders table and the product table which have millions of rows each. A few small tables with supporting data are also present. The team is looking for the right distribution patterns for the tables, to optimize query speed.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following are the key points to consider while planning for the best distribution style for your data? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>Small Dimension tables should be marked to use KEY distribution style, which will cause them to be replicated to each physical node in the cluster</p>","<p>A fact table with multiple distribution keys is useful when multiple dimension tables have to be joined to it</p>","<p>If a dimension table cannot be collocated with the fact table or other important joining tables, use ALL distribution style for such tables</p>","<p>Choose a column with low cardinality in the filtered result set</p>","<p>Data should be distributed in such a way that the rows that participate in joins are already collocated on the nodes with their joining rows in other tables</p>"],"correct":[2,4],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Data should be distributed in such a way that the rows that participate in joins are already collocated on the nodes with their joining rows in other tables</strong></p>\n\n<p>A prime goal of data distribution is to minimize data movement when queries run. Therefore, if the rows that participate in joins or aggregates are already collocated on the nodes with their joining rows in other tables, the optimizer doesn't need to redistribute as much data when queries run.</p>\n\n<p><strong>If a dimension table cannot be collocated with the fact table or other important joining tables, use ALL distribution style for such tables</strong></p>\n\n<p>When you execute a query, the query optimizer redistributes the rows to the compute nodes as needed to perform any joins and aggregations. The goal in selecting a table distribution style is to minimize the impact of the redistribution step by locating the data where it needs to be before the query is run.</p>\n\n<p>One of the best approaches is to change some dimension tables to use ALL distribution. If a dimension table cannot be collocated with the fact table or other important joining tables, you can improve query performance significantly by distributing the entire table to all of the nodes. Using ALL distribution multiplies storage space requirements and increases load times and maintenance operations, so you should weigh all factors before choosing ALL distribution.</p>\n\n<p>Choosing the best distribution style:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/fbc160165902dc2a1ca30a75373ca24c493974a14868000f89344adb8f50abcd.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/fbc160165902dc2a1ca30a75373ca24c493974a14868000f89344adb8f50abcd.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Choose a column with low cardinality in the filtered result set</strong> - This is incorrect. A column with high cardinality should be considered in the filtered result set. If you distribute a sales table on a date column, for example, you should probably get fairly even data distribution, unless most of your sales are seasonal. However, if you commonly use a range-restricted predicate to filter for a narrow date period, most of the filtered rows occur on a limited set of slices and the query workload is skewed.</p>\n\n<p><strong>A fact table with multiple distribution keys is useful when multiple dimension tables have to be joined to it</strong> - Your fact table can have only one distribution key. Any tables that join on another key aren't collocated with the fact table. Choose one dimension to collocate based on how frequently it is joined and the size of the joining rows.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Small Dimension tables should be marked to use KEY distribution style, which will cause them to be replicated to each physical node in the cluster</strong> - In a KEY distribution style, the rows are distributed according to the values in one column. The leader node places matching values on the same node slice. If you distribute a pair of tables on the joining keys, the leader node collocates the rows on the slices according to the values in the joining columns. This way, matching values from the common columns are physically stored together.</p>\n\n<p>A copy of the entire table is distributed to every node in ALL distribution styles.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html</a></p>"},{"id":40,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>An IT company needs to set up a data lake on Amazon S3 for a healthcare client. The data lake is split into raw and curated zones. For compliance reasons, the source data needs to be kept for a minimum of 5 years. The source data arrives in the raw zone and is then processed via an AWS Glue-based ETL job into the curated zone. The data engineering team runs ad-hoc queries only on the data in the curated zone using Athena. The team is concerned about the cost of data storage in both the raw and curated zones as the data is increasing at a rate of 2 TB daily in each zone.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following options would you implement together as the MOST cost-optimal solution? (Select two)</p>","answers":["<p>Use Glue ETL job to write the transformed data in the curated zone using a compressed file format</p>","<p>Setup a lifecycle policy to transition the curated zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation</p>","<p>Use Glue ETL job to write the transformed data in the curated zone using CSV format</p>","<p>Setup a lifecycle policy to transition the raw zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation</p>","<p>Create a Lambda function based job to delete the raw zone data after 1 day</p>"],"correct":[0,3],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Setup a lifecycle policy to transition the raw zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation</strong></p>\n\n<p>You can manage your objects so that they are stored cost-effectively throughout their lifecycle by configuring their Amazon S3 Lifecycle. An S3 Lifecycle configuration is a set of rules that define actions that Amazon S3 applies to a group of objects. For example, you might choose to transition objects to the S3 Standard-IA storage class 30 days after you created them, or archive objects to the S3 Glacier storage class one year after creating them.</p>\n\n<p>For the given use-case, the raw zone consists of the source data, so it cannot be deleted due to compliance reasons. Therefore, you should use a lifecycle policy to transition the raw zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation.</p>\n\n<p>Please read more about S3 Object Lifecycle Management:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/268818288fea47fd4f152f159efd555f1f7952018074cd7b66e4bce729eb1818.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/268818288fea47fd4f152f159efd555f1f7952018074cd7b66e4bce729eb1818.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Glue ETL job to write the transformed data in the curated zone using a compressed file format</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for customers to prepare and load their data for analytics.</p>\n\n<p>You cannot transition the curated zone data into Glacier Deep Archive because it is used by business analysts for ad-hoc querying. Therefore, the best optimization is to have the curated zone data stored in a compressed format via the Glue job. The compressed data would reduce the storage cost incurred on the data in the curated zone.</p>\n\n<p>Please see this example for a Glue ETL Pipeline:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/c9d1a7edf053b6a46c235881c47230aa1883d4161bb6773217e91cbf396c6835.png\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/c9d1a7edf053b6a46c235881c47230aa1883d4161bb6773217e91cbf396c6835.png\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/\">https://aws.amazon.com/glue/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a Lambda function-based job to delete the raw zone data after 1 day</strong> - As mentioned in the use case, the source data needs to be kept for a minimum of 5 years for compliance reasons. Therefore the data in the raw zone cannot be deleted after 1 day.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Setup a lifecycle policy to transition the curated zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation</strong> - You cannot transition the curated zone data into Glacier Deep Archive because it is used by the business analysts for ad-hoc querying. Hence this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Glue ETL job to write the transformed data in the curated zone using CSV format</strong> - It is cost-optimal to write the data in the curated zone using a compressed format instead of CSV format. The compressed data would reduce the storage cost incurred on the data in the curated zone. So, this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/\">https://aws.amazon.com/glue/</a></p>"},{"id":41,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A financial services company runs a real-time data pipeline that processes card authorization events. The company needs an alerting mechanism that routes processing errors to different teams based on the error category. Security exceptions must notify the security response team immediately. Schema validation failures must notify the data governance team. Infrastructure failures must notify the platform operations team.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Create Amazon CloudWatch alarms for separate custom metrics that represent each error category, and configure each alarm to notify a different Amazon SNS topic</p>","<p>Create CloudWatch Logs metric filters for each error category, publish matching counts as metrics, and configure CloudWatch alarms to notify team-specific Amazon SNS topics when thresholds are breached</p>","<p>Create Amazon EventBridge rules with separate event patterns for each error category, and route each matching event to a dedicated Amazon SNS topic for the responsible team</p>","<p>Send processing errors to Amazon SQS queues with message attributes, and require each team to poll its assigned queue for messages that match its error category</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create Amazon EventBridge rules with separate event patterns for each error category, and route each matching event to a dedicated Amazon SNS topic for the responsible team</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon EventBridge supports event patterns that match specific fields in an event. The company can publish pipeline error events with an error category field, then create one rule for security exceptions, one rule for schema validation failures, and one rule for infrastructure failures.</p>\n\n<p>Each EventBridge rule can route matching events directly to a dedicated Amazon SNS topic. The security team, data governance team, and platform operations team can subscribe to their respective topics and receive only the alerts that are relevant to their responsibilities.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational overhead because it uses managed event routing and managed notification delivery. The company does not need to write Lambda routing code, operate polling consumers, build queue-processing logic, or wait for metric thresholds to trigger alarms.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create Amazon CloudWatch alarms for separate custom metrics that represent each error category, and configure each alarm to notify a different Amazon SNS topic</strong> - CloudWatch alarms are useful when a metric crosses a threshold. This works well for operational conditions such as high error rates, latency, failed invocations, or resource utilization.</p>\n\n<p>However, the requirement is to notify teams immediately for each error category. Metric-based alarms can introduce delay because they depend on metric publication, evaluation periods, and threshold state changes rather than direct event routing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create CloudWatch Logs metric filters for each error category, publish matching counts as metrics, and configure CloudWatch alarms to notify team-specific Amazon SNS topics when thresholds are breached</strong> - CloudWatch Logs metric filters can identify patterns in log events and turn matching log entries into CloudWatch metrics. This can help teams measure how often different error categories occur.</p>\n\n<p>However, metric filters do not deliver the original event immediately to the appropriate team. The design depends on metric aggregation and alarm evaluation, which is better for threshold-based monitoring than direct real-time alert routing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Send processing errors to Amazon SQS queues with message attributes, and require each team to poll its assigned queue for messages that match its error category</strong> - Amazon SQS can decouple producers and consumers, and message attributes can help classify messages. Separate queues can allow teams or applications to consume only relevant messages.</p>\n\n<p>However, SQS requires consumers to poll queues. That polling model does not satisfy the requirement for immediate notification as directly as EventBridge routing to SNS. It also shifts responsibility to each team to operate a queue consumer or notification process.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-rules.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-rules.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-event-patterns.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-event-patterns.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-targets.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-targets.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/welcome.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/filtering-events-in-amazon-eventbridge-with-wildcard-pattern-matching/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/filtering-events-in-amazon-eventbridge-with-wildcard-pattern-matching/</a></p>"},{"id":42,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A company uses the S3 Standard storage class for all its data storage needs on Amazon S3. A data engineer analyzed the data access patterns and discovered that for the first six months, data files are accessed multiple times daily. From six months to three years, the data files are accessed about once or twice a month. After three years, each file is accessed only once or twice annually. The data engineer is tasked with creating S3 Lifecycle policies to develop new data storage rules, ensuring high availability while seeking cost-effectiveness.</p>\n\n<p>What solution will best meet these needs?</p>","answers":["<p>Move objects to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years</p>","<p>Move objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Archive after three years</p>","<p>Move objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years</p>","<p>Move objects to S3 Intelligent-Tiering after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years</strong></p>\n\n<p>The S3 Standard-IA storage class is designed for long-lived and infrequently accessed data. (IA stands for infrequent access.) S3 Standard-IA and S3 One Zone-IA objects are available for millisecond access (similar to the S3 Standard storage class). Amazon S3 charges a retrieval fee for these objects, so they are most suitable for infrequently accessed data.</p>\n\n<p>Use S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archiving data that rarely need to be accessed. Data stored in the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class has a minimum storage duration period of 180 days and a default retrieval time of 12 hours. It is the lowest-cost storage option in AWS. Storage costs for the S3 Glacier Deep Archive are less expensive than using the S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval storage class.</p>\n\n<p>Since the use case demands high availability while maintaining cost-effectiveness, so both S3 Standard-IA and S3 Glacier Deep Archive offer the best fit.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/a3754edfc0a91e2986ca896b05377f7a3be8306edc78b94c3b2d5e59d9a7e249.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/a3754edfc0a91e2986ca896b05377f7a3be8306edc78b94c3b2d5e59d9a7e249.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move data objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 30 days. Transfer the data objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 1 year</strong> - S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is used for archives where portions of the data might need to be retrieved in minutes. Data stored in the S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval storage class has a minimum storage duration period of 90 days and can be accessed in as little as 1-5 minutes by using expedited retrieval. Storage costs for the S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval are more than the S3 Glacier Deep Archive. Since the use case poses no constraints on the first-byte latency, using S3 Glacier Deep Archive is more cost-effective while still maintaining high availability. So, this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move objects to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years</strong> - Using S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) is ruled out as it does not guarantee high availability. So, this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move objects to S3 Intelligent-Tiering after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years</strong> - For a small monthly object monitoring and automation charge, S3 Intelligent-Tiering monitors access patterns and automatically moves objects that have not been accessed to lower-cost access tiers. S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically stores objects in three access tiers: one tier that is optimized for frequent access, a 40% lower-cost tier that is optimized for infrequent access, and a 68% lower-cost tier optimized for rarely accessed data. Since access patterns are very clear, Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering is not needed for this use case.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html</a></p>"},{"id":43,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A company operates a chain of pet-care shops and it stores all of its data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company uses Amazon Athena to analyze customer preferences to launch new products for the different types of pets. The company's data is of the order of tens of terabytes and the company is looking at reducing the costs of running these queries.</p>\n\n<p>What do you recommend?</p>","answers":["<p>Partition the data by store region</p>","<p>Partition the data by pet type</p>","<p>Partition the data by customer</p>","<p>Migrate the data to Redshift tables for a more cost-efficient analysis</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Partition the data by pet type</strong></p>\n\n<p>By partitioning your data, you can restrict the amount of data scanned by each query, thus improving performance and reducing cost. You can partition your data by any key. A common practice is to partition the data based on time, often leading to a multi-level partitioning scheme. For example, a customer who has data coming in every hour might decide to partition by year, month, date, and hour. Another customer, who has data coming from many different sources but that is loaded only once per day, might partition by a data source identifier and date.</p>\n\n<p>While analyzing the new product launches for the different types of pets, most of the queries will be related to the pet type, so partitioning data by pet type will reduce the amount of data scanned by Athena and hence reduce the associated costs.</p>\n\n<p>Partitioning data in Athena:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/fe813c88fdc66366a616f0153e7ea8f271fd35b3ec023f597ac3528cdcdb1b71.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/fe813c88fdc66366a616f0153e7ea8f271fd35b3ec023f597ac3528cdcdb1b71.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><em>*Migrate the data to Redshift tables for a more cost-efficient analysis</em> - Migrating data to Redshift will increase the costs compared to directly querying data on S3 via Athena.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Partition the data by customer</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Partition the data by store region</strong></p>\n\n<p>Partitioning data by customers or by store region will not help in optimizing the queries relevant to the given use case, so both these options are incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>Reference:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html</a></p>"},{"id":44,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A pharmaceutical research company stores processed clinical analytics data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company uses IAM roles to give research, compliance, and analytics teams different levels of access to the bucket. The company has a strict data access policy that defines which users and roles can read, write, or delete objects.</p>\n\n<p>The security team wants to receive a notification whenever a user violates the data access policy. Each notification must include the identity of the user who performed the unauthorized S3 object-level action.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Enable CloudTrail data events for the S3 bucket, send the events to CloudWatch Logs, and create CloudWatch alarms for policy-violation patterns</p>","<p>Use S3 server access logging, deliver logs to another S3 bucket, and manually query requester fields when the security team investigates violations</p>","<p>Use Amazon Macie to classify sensitive objects in the S3 bucket and send findings to the security team when new sensitive files appear</p>","<p>Use AWS Config managed rules to evaluate S3 bucket compliance, create compliance alarms, and notify the security team when the bucket becomes noncompliant</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable CloudTrail data events for the S3 bucket, send the events to CloudWatch Logs, and create CloudWatch alarms for policy-violation patterns</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS CloudTrail data events can record object-level activity for Amazon S3 buckets. These events can capture actions such as object reads, writes, and deletes, which makes CloudTrail the right source for detecting violations of an S3 object access policy.</p>\n\n<p>CloudTrail event records include identity information about the principal that made the request. This helps the company include the user or role identity in the notification when a policy-violation pattern is detected.</p>\n\n<p>Sending CloudTrail events to CloudWatch Logs allows the team to create metric filters and alarms for specific access patterns. This solution aligns with the requirement to monitor S3 object-level access and notify the company when a user violates the data access policy.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Config managed rules to evaluate S3 bucket compliance, create compliance alarms, and notify the security team when the bucket becomes noncompliant</strong> - AWS Config can evaluate resource configurations against rules. It is useful for detecting configuration drift, such as whether a bucket has public access settings or encryption enabled.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is that AWS Config does not track every S3 object-level access attempt by user. The company needs event-level audit data that includes the identity of the user who performed the action.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use S3 server access logging, deliver logs to another S3 bucket, and manually query requester fields when the security team investigates violations</strong> - S3 server access logs can record requests made to a bucket and can include requester information. They can help with retrospective access analysis.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because the workflow described relies on manual investigation instead of automated notification. CloudTrail data events integrated with CloudWatch Logs provide a more direct monitoring and alerting path for policy-violation patterns.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Macie to classify sensitive objects in the S3 bucket and send findings to the security team when new sensitive files appear</strong> - Amazon Macie can discover and classify sensitive data in Amazon S3. It is useful for identifying sensitive objects and generating findings about data exposure risks.</p>\n\n<p>This does not meet the requirement because the company needs notifications for user access-policy violations. Classifying sensitive files does not identify each unauthorized object-level action with the username that performed it.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-logging.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-logging.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/send-cloudtrail-events-to-cloudwatch-logs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/send-cloudtrail-events-to-cloudwatch-logs.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ServerLogs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ServerLogs.html</a></p>"},{"id":45,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A media company uses an ad-hoc Kinesis Firehose-based solution to ingest raw data in JSON format and then deliver it to an Amazon S3 bucket. The data engineering team at the company uses Apache Spark SQL to analyze this data via Amazon EMR, which is configured to use AWS Glue Data Catalog as the metastore. An AWS Glue crawler runs every four hours to update the schema of the data catalog. The team has noticed that it sometimes obtains outdated data. You have been hired by the company as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to build a solution for ensuring that the team always has access to the current data.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following represents the best solution to meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Modify the execution schedule of the AWS Glue crawler from 4 hours to 1 minute</p>","<p>Use Amazon CloudWatch Events with the rate (5 minutes) expression to execute the AWS Glue crawler every 5 minutes</p>","<p>Use Amazon Athena to directly analyze the current data in Amazon S3</p>","<p>Invoke the AWS Glue crawler via an AWS Lambda function that is triggered by an S3:ObjectCreated:* event notification on the S3 bucket</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Invoke the AWS Glue crawler via an AWS Lambda function that is triggered by an S3:ObjectCreated:* event notification on the S3 bucket</strong></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/b399e10b8388045abe6adefd91f07f2680436755049d1cde960510cadcbe3858.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/b399e10b8388045abe6adefd91f07f2680436755049d1cde960510cadcbe3858.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>An AWS Glue crawler is a program that connects to a data store (source or target) such as Amazon S3, progresses through a prioritized list of classifiers to determine the schema for your data, and then creates metadata tables in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. You can use a Lambda function that is triggered by an S3:ObjectCreated:* event notification on the S3 bucket to invoke the AWS Glue crawler on-demand. This obviates the need to periodically run the crawler on a schedule to update the new data in the existing data catalog tables.</p>\n\n<p>Data Lake Architecture:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/e61ad343ef727be9750880df512235eb793d802eee1667eaa6492dea698244fe.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/e61ad343ef727be9750880df512235eb793d802eee1667eaa6492dea698244fe.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-and-automate-a-serverless-data-lake-using-an-aws-glue-trigger-for-the-data-catalog-and-etl-jobs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-and-automate-a-serverless-data-lake-using-an-aws-glue-trigger-for-the-data-catalog-and-etl-jobs/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon CloudWatch Events with the rate (5 minutes) expression to execute the AWS Glue crawler every 5 minutes</strong> - CloudWatch Events do not support AWS Glue crawler as a destination type, so this option just acts as a distractor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Modify the execution schedule of the AWS Glue crawler from 4 hours to 1 minute</strong> - For AWS Glue crawlers, the minimum precision for a schedule is 5 minutes. So this option just acts as a distractor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Athena to directly analyze the current data in Amazon S3</strong> - The use case mentions that the analysis must be done by using Apache Spark SQL on Amazon EMR, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-data-warehouse-schedule.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-data-warehouse-schedule.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-and-automate-a-serverless-data-lake-using-an-aws-glue-trigger-for-the-data-catalog-and-etl-jobs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-and-automate-a-serverless-data-lake-using-an-aws-glue-trigger-for-the-data-catalog-and-etl-jobs/</a></p>"},{"id":46,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A financial services company uses Amazon DataZone as its business catalog and governance portal. The company stores curated transaction data in an Amazon S3 data lake and catalogs the datasets in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.</p>\n\n<p>A data engineer needs to make AWS Glue Data Quality scores visible to business users in the Amazon DataZone portal. The data quality checks must run on a recurring schedule for specific AWS Glue tables.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Create AWS Glue crawlers for the S3 data lake, schedule crawler runs daily, and enable Amazon DataZone to infer data quality scores from crawler schema changes</p>","<p>Configure AWS Glue ETL jobs with an Evaluate Data Quality transform, define rules inside each job, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source</p>","<p>Create DQDL rulesets for AWS Glue tables, schedule the rulesets daily, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone Amazon Redshift data source</p>","<p>Create a DQDL ruleset for each AWS Glue table, schedule the ruleset to run, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a DQDL ruleset for each AWS Glue table, schedule the ruleset to run, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue Data Quality supports rulesets that use Data Quality Definition Language, or DQDL. The data engineer can define rules for specific AWS Glue Data Catalog tables and schedule the rulesets to run on a recurring basis.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon DataZone can import AWS Glue Data Quality results for AWS Glue assets. To make the scores visible in the Amazon DataZone portal, the engineer must configure the Amazon DataZone project with an AWS Glue data source and enable data quality for that data source.</p>\n\n<p>This solution aligns with the requirement because the company stores data in Amazon S3, catalogs the data in AWS Glue Data Catalog, and wants AWS Glue Data Quality scores to appear in Amazon DataZone. The scheduled AWS Glue Data Quality rules generate the scores, and Amazon DataZone publishes those scores to the business catalog.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure AWS Glue ETL jobs with an Evaluate Data Quality transform, define rules inside each job, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source</strong> - The Evaluate Data Quality transform can evaluate data quality rules inside AWS Glue ETL jobs. This can be useful when a team wants data quality checks embedded directly in a transformation pipeline.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is that the requirement is to publish AWS Glue Data Quality scores for AWS Glue Data Catalog assets to Amazon DataZone. A scheduled DQDL ruleset applied to the Glue table and enabled through an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source is the more direct fit.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create DQDL rulesets for AWS Glue tables, schedule the rulesets daily, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone Amazon Redshift data source</strong> - DQDL rulesets scheduled against AWS Glue tables are appropriate for generating AWS Glue Data Quality scores. This part of the option matches the data quality requirement.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because it configures Amazon DataZone with an Amazon Redshift data source. Amazon DataZone data quality integration through the portal is for AWS Glue inventory assets, so the data source should be AWS Glue rather than Amazon Redshift.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create AWS Glue crawlers for the S3 data lake, schedule crawler runs daily, and enable Amazon DataZone to infer data quality scores from crawler schema changes</strong> - AWS Glue crawlers can discover schemas and update AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata for datasets in Amazon S3. They are useful for keeping table definitions current.</p>\n\n<p>Crawler runs do not generate AWS Glue Data Quality scores. Schema discovery is not the same as executing DQDL rulesets, and Amazon DataZone cannot infer data quality scores only from crawler schema changes.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/datazone-data-quality.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/datazone-data-quality.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/create-glue-data-source.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/create-glue-data-source.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-getting-started.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-getting-started.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-datazone-now-integrates-with-aws-glue-data-quality-and-external-data-quality-solutions/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-datazone-now-integrates-with-aws-glue-data-quality-and-external-data-quality-solutions/</a></p>"},{"id":47,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A retail marketplace company needs to capture ongoing data changes from several Amazon Aurora MySQL databases that support orders, payments, and fulfillment services. The company must make the updates available for analytics in Amazon Redshift Serverless in near real time. The solution must be serverless and must minimize ongoing maintenance for the data engineering team.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Create AWS Glue streaming jobs that poll each Aurora MySQL database for changed rows, write intermediate files to Amazon S3, and load the files into Amazon Redshift Serverless on a frequent schedule</p>","<p>Use AWS DMS to configure binary log replication from Aurora MySQL to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, transform schemas, and load the changes into Amazon Redshift Serverless</p>","<p>Create AWS DMS tasks that perform schema conversion for each Aurora MySQL database and load the converted changes into Amazon Redshift Serverless</p>","<p>Create Aurora zero-ETL integrations with Amazon Redshift Serverless for the Aurora MySQL databases to make ongoing changes available in Redshift</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create Aurora zero-ETL integrations with Amazon Redshift Serverless for the Aurora MySQL databases to make ongoing changes available in Redshift</strong></p>\n\n<p>Aurora zero-ETL integration with Amazon Redshift is designed to make transactional data from Aurora available in Amazon Redshift for analytics without building and maintaining custom ETL pipelines. The company can create integrations from the Aurora MySQL sources to Amazon Redshift Serverless so ongoing database changes become available for analytics.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/dc3ff833baf39687821d1226445320f75840ee2b9a973d5eb24e80f25a3f716a.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/dc3ff833baf39687821d1226445320f75840ee2b9a973d5eb24e80f25a3f716a.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>This solution meets the near real-time requirement because zero-ETL integrations continuously replicate changes from Aurora to Redshift. The analytics teams can query the data in Redshift Serverless without waiting for hourly batch jobs, custom change data capture jobs, or file-based loads.</p>\n\n<p>This option also provides the least operational overhead because AWS manages the integration workflow. The company does not need to manage Kafka connectors, DMS replication tasks, schema conversion jobs, Glue polling logic, or S3-based change file pipelines.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/8170d82be7b45db0587cf17320cabc22e0024f7600caf764f4dcc4365907863c.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/8170d82be7b45db0587cf17320cabc22e0024f7600caf764f4dcc4365907863c.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/9454a548477f6a2d537dfe7d58a44539a15fad91374eba8f6b9f5b1c656c1566.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/9454a548477f6a2d537dfe7d58a44539a15fad91374eba8f6b9f5b1c656c1566.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create AWS Glue streaming jobs that poll each Aurora MySQL database for changed rows, write intermediate files to Amazon S3, and load the files into Amazon Redshift Serverless on a frequent schedule</strong> - AWS Glue jobs can read from JDBC sources and write to Amazon S3 or Amazon Redshift. A Glue-based pipeline could periodically extract changed rows and load them into Redshift.</p>\n\n<p>However, polling each Aurora MySQL database and staging data in S3 adds custom logic and scheduling overhead. It also increases latency compared with a managed zero-ETL integration that continuously replicates source changes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS DMS to configure binary log replication from Aurora MySQL to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, transform schemas, and load the changes into Amazon Redshift Serverless</strong> - AWS DMS can use MySQL binary logs to capture ongoing changes, and Kinesis Data Streams can receive streaming data. This architecture can support a custom near real-time ingestion pipeline.</p>\n\n<p>However, the company would need to manage DMS configuration, stream capacity, schema handling, transformations, and Redshift loading. That creates more operational overhead than using Aurora zero-ETL integrations directly with Amazon Redshift Serverless.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create AWS DMS tasks that perform schema conversion for each Aurora MySQL database and load the converted changes into Amazon Redshift Serverless</strong> - AWS DMS can migrate or replicate data between supported sources and targets, and schema conversion can help with migrations between heterogeneous database engines. This can be useful for database migration projects.</p>\n\n<p>However, this workload needs near real-time analytics from Aurora MySQL into Redshift Serverless, not a schema conversion-heavy migration workflow. DMS tasks require more setup and monitoring than the managed zero-ETL integration.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.creating.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.creating.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.filtering.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.filtering.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/zero-etl-using.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/zero-etl-using.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":48,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A healthcare company stores audit data in Amazon S3 buckets and patient application data in Amazon RDS DB instances. The security team must apply technical controls that create backups for both services and retain the backups for required retention periods. The backups must be protected from accidental or malicious deletion until the required retention periods expire.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Enable S3 Versioning and Cross-Region Replication on the buckets, configure RDS automated backups, and copy additional RDS snapshots to a second Region for long-term retention</p>","<p>Create an AWS Backup plan with backup schedules, windows, lifecycle values, and a backup vault, assign the S3 and RDS resources, and configure AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode</p>","<p>Create EventBridge schedules that invoke Lambda functions to copy S3 objects and create RDS snapshots, then use IAM deny policies to prevent backup deletion during retention periods</p>","<p>Enable S3 Versioning and Same-Region Replication on the buckets, configure RDS automated backups, and create manual DB snapshots for retention periods that exceed automated backup limits</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an AWS Backup plan with backup schedules, windows, lifecycle values, and a backup vault, assign the S3 and RDS resources, and configure AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Backup provides a centralized way to create and manage backups across supported AWS services, including Amazon S3 and Amazon RDS. A backup plan can define backup schedules, backup windows, lifecycle settings, and the backup vault where recovery points are stored.</p>\n\n<p>AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode helps protect recovery points from deletion before the configured retention period expires. This is important when the company must defend backups against accidental deletion, malicious activity, or overly broad administrator permissions.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational overhead because it uses one managed backup control plane for both S3 and RDS. The company does not need to separately manage S3 version protection, RDS snapshot workflows, custom Lambda functions, replication rules, and retention enforcement logic for each service.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/eb705e41f09e44a79c27f959c7f0b830aee49d92385b8bf9d44942d69ec761fc.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/eb705e41f09e44a79c27f959c7f0b830aee49d92385b8bf9d44942d69ec761fc.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/protecting-data-with-aws-backup-vault-lock/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/protecting-data-with-aws-backup-vault-lock/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create EventBridge schedules that invoke Lambda functions to copy S3 objects and create RDS snapshots, then use IAM deny policies to prevent backup deletion during retention periods</strong> - EventBridge and Lambda can automate custom backup workflows. IAM deny policies can reduce the risk that some principals delete backup resources.</p>\n\n<p>However, this approach requires custom code, scheduling, error handling, monitoring, and ongoing maintenance. IAM policies alone are not as strong as AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode for retention-period deletion protection.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable S3 Versioning and Same-Region Replication on the buckets, configure RDS automated backups, and create manual DB snapshots for retention periods that exceed automated backup limits</strong> - Same-Region Replication can copy S3 objects within the same Region and can help support resilience or data duplication needs. RDS automated backups and manual snapshots can provide database recovery points.</p>\n\n<p>However, replication is not a complete backup governance solution. This option still lacks centralized backup scheduling, lifecycle management, and Vault Lock compliance controls across S3 and RDS backups.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable S3 Versioning and Cross-Region Replication on the buckets, configure RDS automated backups, and copy additional RDS snapshots to a second Region for long-term retention</strong> - Cross-Region Replication can improve resilience for S3 objects, and copied RDS snapshots can support recovery in another Region. This can be useful for disaster recovery requirements.</p>\n\n<p>However, the scenario asks for backup generation, retention, and deletion protection with the least operational overhead. Managing replication and copied snapshots separately adds complexity and does not provide the same centralized immutable backup enforcement as AWS Backup Vault Lock.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/about-backup-plans.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/about-backup-plans.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/s3-backups.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/s3-backups.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/rds-backup.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/rds-backup.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/vault-lock.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/vault-lock.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/protecting-data-with-aws-backup-vault-lock/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/protecting-data-with-aws-backup-vault-lock/</a></p>"},{"id":49,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A gaming company is designing an event log table for a player activity service. The service continuously ingests activity records and other internal applications must retrieve specific records through dependable API calls. The table must support more than 5,000 concurrent write operations and 7,000 read operations each second.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Create an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain, index every activity record as a document, configure dedicated master and data nodes, and use search APIs for all high-concurrency record reads and writes</p>","<p>Create an Amazon Redshift table with the EVEN distribution style and use a JDBC connection to perform all read and write operations</p>","<p>Create an Amazon Redshift table with the KEY distribution style and use the Amazon Redshift Data API to perform all read and write operations</p>","<p>Create an Amazon DynamoDB table with provisioned capacity to support the required read and write throughput, and use DynamoDB APIs for all record access</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Amazon DynamoDB table with provisioned capacity to support the required read and write throughput, and use DynamoDB APIs for all record access</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon DynamoDB is the best fit for this workload because it provides managed NoSQL storage with API-based access to specific items. Applications can use DynamoDB APIs such as GetItem, PutItem, UpdateItem, and Query to read or write individual records without opening database connections or scanning files.</p>\n\n<p>The workload requires thousands of concurrent writes and reads each second. DynamoDB provisioned capacity allows the data engineer to configure read capacity units and write capacity units to meet known throughput requirements. The table design should use a partition key that distributes activity records evenly so the workload does not create hot partitions.</p>\n\n<p>This solution meets the requirement because it supports continuous ingestion, high request rates, low-latency item access, and dependable API-based operations. It avoids using analytical systems such as Amazon Redshift, Amazon Athena, or Redshift Spectrum for a high-concurrency operational access pattern.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/e6d8c303ad6f6a4a506b66f961373412e1886c24b21ebea785e34046bec563c0.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/e6d8c303ad6f6a4a506b66f961373412e1886c24b21ebea785e34046bec563c0.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choose-the-right-throughput-strategy-for-amazon-dynamodb-applications/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choose-the-right-throughput-strategy-for-amazon-dynamodb-applications/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Amazon Redshift table with the KEY distribution style and use the Amazon Redshift Data API to perform all read and write operations</strong> - Amazon Redshift is a data warehouse that supports analytical workloads across large datasets. The KEY distribution style can help colocate rows for join performance when the distribution key aligns with query patterns.</p>\n\n<p>However, Redshift is not the best choice for high-concurrency operational writes and API-based access to individual records. The Redshift Data API is useful for running SQL statements without managing persistent connections, but it does not turn Redshift into a low-latency operational key-value store.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain, index every activity record as a document, configure dedicated master and data nodes, and use search APIs for all high-concurrency record reads and writes</strong> - Amazon OpenSearch Service can index documents and provide low-latency search and analytics over log-style data. It is useful when applications need full-text search, filtering, aggregations, or observability dashboards.</p>\n\n<p>However, this requirement focuses on dependable API-based access to specific records and sustained high write and read operations. DynamoDB is a better fit for key-based operational access, while OpenSearch adds index management, shard sizing, and cluster tuning overhead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an Amazon Redshift table with the EVEN distribution style and use a JDBC connection to perform all read and write operations</strong> - Amazon Redshift with EVEN distribution can distribute rows evenly across slices when there is no clear distribution key. JDBC connections can support SQL access from applications and data tools.</p>\n\n<p>However, Redshift remains an analytical data warehouse, not an operational log table for very high concurrent reads and writes. Using JDBC for all record operations can create connection management and concurrency challenges for this application pattern.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choose-the-right-throughput-strategy-for-amazon-dynamodb-applications/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choose-the-right-throughput-strategy-for-amazon-dynamodb-applications/</a></p>"},{"id":50,"section":"Design High-Performing Architectures","question":"<p>The data engineering team is using Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) to process IoT data from the field devices of an agricultural sciences company. Multiple consumer applications are using the incoming data streams and the data engineers have noticed a performance lag for the data delivery speed between producers and consumers of the data streams.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following would you recommend for improving the performance for the given use-case?</p>","answers":["<p>Swap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon SQS Standard queues</p>","<p>Swap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose</p>","<p>Swap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon SQS FIFO queues</p>","<p>Use Enhanced Fanout feature of Amazon Kinesis Data Streams</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Enhanced Fanout feature of Amazon Kinesis Data Streams</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) is a massively scalable and durable real-time data streaming service. KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events.</p>\n\n<p>By default, the 2MB/second/shard output is shared between all of the applications consuming data from the stream. You should use enhanced fan-out if you have multiple consumers retrieving data from a stream in parallel. With enhanced fan-out developers can register stream consumers to use enhanced fan-out and receive their own 2MB/second pipe of read throughput per shard, and this throughput automatically scales with the number of shards in a stream.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon Kinesis Data Streams Fanout:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/7ac4f17ae6a0d4df8447f443d1226f1bd0f26454469d132927c873ee1b824003.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/7ac4f17ae6a0d4df8447f443d1226f1bd0f26454469d132927c873ee1b824003.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Swap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose</strong> -  Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is the easiest way to reliably load streaming data into data lakes, data stores, and analytics tools. It is a fully managed service that automatically scales to match the throughput of your data and requires no ongoing administration. It can also batch, compress, transform, and encrypt the data before loading it, minimizing the amount of storage used at the destination and increasing security. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose can only write to Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon Elasticsearch or Splunk. You can't have applications consuming data streams from Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, that's the job of Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Therefore this option is not correct.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Swap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon SQS Standard queues</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Swap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon SQS FIFO queues</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) is a fully managed message queuing service that enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. Amazon SQS offers two types of message queues. Standard queues offer maximum throughput, best-effort ordering, and at-least-once delivery. Amazon SQS FIFO queues are designed to guarantee that messages are processed exactly once, in the exact order that they are sent. As multiple applications are consuming the same stream concurrently, both Amazon SQS Standard and Amazon SQS FIFO are not the right fit for the given use case.</p>\n\n<p>Exam Alert:</p>\n\n<p>You should understand the differences between the capabilities of Amazon Kinesis Data Streams vs Amazon SQS, as you may be asked scenario-based questions on this topic in the exam.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/cbea91ecf27d8ab376916c5f7ca0cfa2f140d0f17408c9ae8e94f2c25f3a6a54.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/cbea91ecf27d8ab376916c5f7ca0cfa2f140d0f17408c9ae8e94f2c25f3a6a54.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/</a></p>"},{"id":51,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A healthcare company uploads confidential patient billing documents to an Amazon S3 bucket each night. The data engineering team must independently confirm that each uploaded object is identical to the source file and that no corruption occurred during the transfer. The solution must validate object integrity as part of the upload process.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["<p>Change the bucket default encryption to server-side encryption with customer-provided keys and use S3 Bucket Keys to validate uploaded objects</p>","<p>Download every uploaded patient billing document from Amazon S3 after upload and run a custom program to compare the files with source copies</p>","<p>Enable S3 Object Lock in governance mode on the bucket so uploaded patient billing documents cannot be accidentally overwritten or deleted</p>","<p>Calculate a SHA-256 checksum for each document before upload and pass the checksum value to the AWS SDK in each Amazon S3 upload request</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Calculate a SHA-256 checksum for each document before upload and pass the checksum value to the AWS SDK in each Amazon S3 upload request</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 supports checksum-based integrity validation for object uploads. The data engineering team can calculate a SHA-256 checksum for each document before upload and include the checksum value in the upload request through the AWS SDK.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 validates the uploaded object against the checksum value that the client provides. If the checksum does not match, the upload fails instead of storing a corrupted object. This helps confirm that the uploaded object is identical to the data that the client intended to send.</p>\n\n<p>This solution meets the requirement because it independently verifies data integrity during the transfer process. It avoids manual review, avoids downloading all objects after upload, and uses native S3 checksum support instead of building a separate validation workflow.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable S3 Object Lock in governance mode on the bucket so uploaded patient billing documents cannot be accidentally overwritten or deleted</strong> - S3 Object Lock in governance mode can help prevent protected objects from being overwritten or deleted unless a user has special bypass permissions. This is useful for retention and immutability use cases.</p>\n\n<p>However, Object Lock does not verify that the upload completed without corruption. It can preserve an object after upload, but it cannot prove that the stored object matches the original source file.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Change the bucket default encryption to server-side encryption with customer-provided keys and use S3 Bucket Keys to validate uploaded objects</strong> - Server-side encryption with customer-provided keys can encrypt objects at rest by using keys supplied by the customer. Encryption protects confidentiality of stored data.</p>\n\n<p>However, encryption settings do not independently validate that the uploaded data matches the source file. S3 Bucket Keys are also associated with reducing request costs for SSE-KMS, not validating object integrity for SSE-C uploads.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Download every uploaded patient billing document from Amazon S3 after upload and run a custom program to compare the files with source copies</strong> - Downloading every object after upload and comparing it with the source file could validate integrity if implemented correctly. A programmatic comparison is more complete than a manual sample review.</p>\n\n<p>However, this approach requires a second full data transfer, custom comparison code, extra processing, and ongoing operational maintenance. Passing checksums during upload validates integrity more directly and efficiently.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity-upload.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity-upload.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/feature-dataintegrity.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/feature-dataintegrity.html</a></p>"},{"id":52,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A medical research organization stores clinical trial records in an on-premises MySQL database. The organization has built an application that connects to the database to retrieve and update patient study data. The security team requires database credentials to rotate every 45 days to reduce the risk of unauthorized access. The organization wants to avoid application code changes whenever the credentials rotate.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<p>Use AWS KMS to generate encryption keys with automatic key rotation, encrypt the database credentials, and store the encrypted values in an Amazon DynamoDB table</p>","<p>Store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store as secure strings, configure an EventBridge scheduled rule to invoke a custom Lambda function every 45 days, and update the application configuration file after each successful rotation</p>","<p>Use AWS Secrets Manager to store the database credentials, configure automatic rotation with a Lambda rotation function, and update the application to retrieve the current credentials through Secrets Manager API calls</p>","<p>Store the database credentials in an encrypted S3 bucket, rotate the credentials every 45 days by using an S3 Lifecycle rule, and use bucket policies to control access</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Secrets Manager to store the database credentials, configure automatic rotation with a Lambda rotation function, and update the application to retrieve the current credentials through Secrets Manager API calls</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Secrets Manager is designed to store and manage sensitive values such as database credentials. The application can call the Secrets Manager API to retrieve the current credential at runtime instead of storing a static user name and password in code or configuration files.</p>\n\n<p>Secrets Manager supports automatic rotation by invoking a Lambda rotation function on a schedule. For a database credential, the rotation function updates the secret value and updates the credential in the target database or service. This lets the organization maintain its 45-day rotation requirement without manually changing application code for each rotation.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational overhead because Secrets Manager provides purpose-built secret storage, access control through IAM, API-based retrieval, and managed rotation orchestration. For an on-premises MySQL database, the Lambda rotation function must have network access to the database, but the application does not need code changes each time the password changes.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/6d9c24228f2890a0a431f2820bed4915e69644a9c6e9d3ec3e0f1d56cd853414.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/6d9c24228f2890a0a431f2820bed4915e69644a9c6e9d3ec3e0f1d56cd853414.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-to-securely-provide-database-credentials-to-lambda-functions-by-using-aws-secrets-manager/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-to-securely-provide-database-credentials-to-lambda-functions-by-using-aws-secrets-manager/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store as secure strings, configure an EventBridge scheduled rule to invoke a custom Lambda function every 45 days, and update the application configuration file after each successful rotation</strong> - Systems Manager Parameter Store can store secure string parameters and applications can retrieve parameter values. It can be useful for configuration values and some secrets.</p>\n\n<p>However, automatic credential rotation is not built into Parameter Store in the same way as Secrets Manager. The scheduled Lambda function and application configuration update process add custom operational work and increase the risk of stale credentials.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS KMS to generate encryption keys with automatic key rotation, encrypt the database credentials, and store the encrypted values in an Amazon DynamoDB table</strong> - AWS KMS can encrypt and protect cryptographic keys, and automatic KMS key rotation can rotate key material for eligible KMS keys. KMS is important for encryption, but it does not automatically rotate database user passwords.</p>\n\n<p>This option would still require custom logic to generate a new database password, update the MySQL database, update the stored credential, and coordinate application retrieval. That creates more operational overhead than Secrets Manager rotation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the database credentials in an encrypted S3 bucket, rotate the credentials every 45 days by using an S3 Lifecycle rule, and use bucket policies to control access</strong> - Amazon S3 can store encrypted objects, and bucket policies can control access to those objects. However, S3 Lifecycle rules manage object transitions and expiration, not database password rotation.</p>\n\n<p>This design does not update the on-premises MySQL database credential. It also forces the company to build custom rotation and retrieval behavior rather than using a managed secrets service.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotating-secrets.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotating-secrets.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda-functions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda-functions.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/hardcoded-db-creds.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/hardcoded-db-creds.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-to-securely-provide-database-credentials-to-lambda-functions-by-using-aws-secrets-manager/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-to-securely-provide-database-credentials-to-lambda-functions-by-using-aws-secrets-manager/</a></p>"},{"id":53,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A stock trading company uses Amazon Redshift to power the Business Intelligence (BI) specific queries which are run on Redshift. The data engineering team at the company needs to provide the sales team access to a historical trades table whose data is stored in Apache Parquet format in an S3 bucket of the company's data lake. The data engineering team should provide access to only a few specific columns in the historical trades table so that the access does not violate the compliance regulations.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following options should be combined together to build a solution for the given use case? (Select three)</p>","answers":["<p>Grant permissions in Lake Formation to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table</p>","<p>Create an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access AWS Lake Formation</p>","<p>Create an internal schema in Amazon Redshift by using the Amazon Redshift IAM role</p>","<p>Grant permissions in the S3 bucket policy to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table</p>","<p>Create an external schema in Amazon Redshift by using the Amazon Redshift IAM role</p>","<p>Create an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access the S3 bucket having data for the historical trades table</p>"],"correct":[0,1,4],"explanation":"<p>Correct options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access AWS Lake Formation</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an external schema in Amazon Redshift by using the Amazon Redshift IAM role</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Grant permissions in Lake Formation to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, scalable data warehouse that enables secure analytics at scale. Using Redshift Spectrum, Amazon Redshift customers can easily query their data in Amazon S3. Redshift Spectrum is a built-in feature of Amazon Redshift, and your existing queries and BI tools will continue to work seamlessly. Under the hood, AWS manages a fleet of thousands of Redshift Spectrum nodes spread across multiple Availability Zones. These are transparently scaled and allocated to your queries based on the data that you need to process, with no provisioning or commitments. Redshift Spectrum is also highly concurrent—you can access your Amazon S3 data from any number of Amazon Redshift clusters. To access this data on S3 via Redshift Spectrum, you need to create an external schema in Amazon Redshift.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon Redshift Spectrum supports column level access control for data stored in Amazon S3 and managed by AWS Lake Formation. Column-level access control can be used to limit access to only the specific columns of a table rather than allowing access to all columns of a table. To use this feature, an administrator needs to create an IAM role for Amazon Redshift and create the policy to allow Redshift to access AWS Lake Formation. The administrator can then use the Lake Formation console to specify the tables and columns that the role is allowed access to. The column-level access control policies can also be created and managed by the SQL grant statements.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an internal schema in Amazon Redshift by using the Amazon Redshift IAM role</strong> - As mentioned in the explanation above, you need to create an external schema in Amazon Redshift to access the data on S3 via Redshift spectrum.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Grant permissions in the S3 bucket policy to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table</strong> - You need to grant permissions in Lake Formation to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access the S3 bucket having data for the historical trades table</strong> - You need to create an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access AWS Lake Formation.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2019/08/amazon-redshift-spectrum-now-supports-column-level-access-control-with-aws-lake-formation/\">https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2019/08/amazon-redshift-spectrum-now-supports-column-level-access-control-with-aws-lake-formation/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/achieve-finer-grained-data-security-with-column-level-access-control-in-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/achieve-finer-grained-data-security-with-column-level-access-control-in-amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":54,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A financial services company is implementing a data mesh architecture on AWS. The market analytics, credit risk, and regulatory reporting domains each own their own datasets and must share only approved views with other domains. The company has more than 1,200 tables across 60 databases in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. All domains use Amazon Athena for ad hoc analysis and Amazon Redshift for complex reporting. The regulatory reporting team must audit all data access, and personally identifiable information must be hidden from teams that do not have approval to view it. The company needs a scalable solution that supports cross-domain analysis across both query engines.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Use AWS Glue Data Catalog views for shared domain views, manage access with Lake Formation, audit access with CloudTrail, and use security definer views to mask PII</p>","<p>Create Amazon Redshift materialized views for each domain, publish Redshift datashares to the other teams, and use Redshift permissions for all cross-domain analysis</p>","<p>Create separate S3 prefixes for each domain, use IAM policies to control access to each prefix, and require Athena and Redshift users to query only their assigned prefixes</p>","<p>Create standard Athena views for each domain, use those Athena views from Amazon Redshift for cross-domain reporting, enable CloudTrail auditing, and use Lake Formation table permissions</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue Data Catalog views for shared domain views, manage access with Lake Formation, audit access with CloudTrail, and use security definer views to mask PII</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Glue Data Catalog views provide a scalable way to define shared views over data that is registered in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. The company can create approved views for each domain and expose only the columns and rows that other teams are allowed to query.</p>\n\n<p>Data Catalog views support a common view object that users can query from services such as Amazon Athena and Amazon Redshift. This is important because the company needs both ad hoc analysis in Athena and complex reporting in Redshift without creating separate, inconsistent view definitions for each engine.</p>\n\n<p>Lake Formation can manage access to the views and the underlying governed data, while CloudTrail can support auditing of data access. Security definer view semantics help protect underlying tables and allow the company to mask or omit PII fields in shared views while still enabling cross-domain analytics.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/680ed3a4bdd49e30da5efdc7d69614d9e0aa78b47c1d28bd894dad50660e8186.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/680ed3a4bdd49e30da5efdc7d69614d9e0aa78b47c1d28bd894dad50660e8186.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-creating-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-with-aws-sdk-for-data-mesh-use-case/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-creating-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-with-aws-sdk-for-data-mesh-use-case/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create standard Athena views for each domain, use those Athena views from Amazon Redshift for cross-domain reporting, enable CloudTrail auditing, and use Lake Formation table permissions</strong> - Athena views can support on-demand analysis in Athena, and Lake Formation can help govern access to cataloged data. CloudTrail can also support auditing for governance and compliance workflows.</p>\n\n<p>However, standard Athena views are not the best scalable shared view layer for both Athena and Amazon Redshift. The requirement needs a common cross-engine approach for many databases and tables, which AWS Glue Data Catalog views are designed to provide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create separate S3 prefixes for each domain, use IAM policies to control access to each prefix, and require Athena and Redshift users to query only their assigned prefixes</strong> - IAM policies and S3 prefixes can restrict access to data at the storage layer. This approach can work for simple data lake layouts where access boundaries align cleanly with object paths.</p>\n\n<p>However, prefix-level access is too coarse for sharing specific views, masking PII, and supporting fine-grained cross-domain analytics across many tables and databases. It also does not provide a consistent view layer for Athena and Redshift.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create Amazon Redshift materialized views for each domain, publish Redshift datashares to the other teams, and use Redshift permissions for all cross-domain analysis</strong> - Amazon Redshift materialized views and datashares can support reporting and controlled data sharing in Redshift-centered architectures. They can be useful when the primary analytics access pattern is inside Redshift.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option does not provide a shared governance layer for Athena and Redshift together. It also makes Redshift the center of the data mesh access model, while the requirement includes Athena users and AWS Glue Data Catalog resources across many domains.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-views.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-views.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views-glue.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/data-catalog-views-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/data-catalog-views-overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-using-amazon-athena-and-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-using-amazon-athena-and-amazon-redshift/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-creating-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-with-aws-sdk-for-data-mesh-use-case/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-creating-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-with-aws-sdk-for-data-mesh-use-case/</a></p>"},{"id":55,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A healthcare advisory company is building a data pipeline for an application that receives the most traffic during clinic business hours. The application must help users search medical policy documents and find available consultants who can answer questions. The medical policy document repository is refreshed once every night.</p>\n\n<p>The application must provide complex full-text search across the policy documents and must show consultant availability from a scheduling database. Consultant availability must reflect schedule changes within 5 minutes. The company wants to use AWS Glue for ETL processing and wants the solution with the least operational overhead.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Run AWS Glue jobs on a fixed 15-minute schedule for both policy documents and consultant schedules, store all processed records in Amazon S3, and use Athena SQL queries for application search</p>","<p>Use Step Functions to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs from scheduled and event-driven starts, store processed data in Amazon S3, and use OpenSearch Service for full-text search</p>","<p>Use Step Functions to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs from scheduled and event-driven starts, store processed data in Amazon S3, and use Amazon RDS for full-text search</p>","<p>Use AWS Glue workflows with scheduled and event-based triggers, store processed data in DynamoDB, and build custom full-text search by using Lambda functions</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Step Functions to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs from scheduled and event-driven starts, store processed data in Amazon S3, and use OpenSearch Service for full-text search</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Step Functions can orchestrate AWS Glue jobs as part of a managed workflow. This allows the company to run the nightly document processing flow on a schedule and also start the consultant availability flow from event-driven changes.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon S3 is a suitable landing and processed-data store for the pipeline outputs. The document repository changes only once each day, while consultant availability must refresh within 5 minutes, so the workflow needs both scheduled and event-driven processing patterns.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon OpenSearch Service is the right fit for complex full-text search over legal or policy documents. This solution uses managed AWS services for orchestration, ETL, storage, and search, which reduces the need to build and operate custom scheduling, indexing, and search components.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Step Functions to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs from scheduled and event-driven starts, store processed data in Amazon S3, and use Amazon RDS for full-text search</strong> - Amazon RDS can store structured relational data and can support some text-search capabilities depending on the selected database engine. It is useful for transactional workloads and relational application data.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is that the requirement calls for complex full-text search across legal or policy documents. Amazon OpenSearch Service is more appropriate for rich document search, indexing, relevance scoring, and search-oriented query behavior.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run AWS Glue jobs on a fixed 15-minute schedule for both policy documents and consultant schedules, store all processed records in Amazon S3, and use Athena SQL queries for application search</strong> - Athena can query data in Amazon S3 and is useful for interactive analytics over data lake files. Scheduled Glue jobs can also process data at regular intervals.</p>\n\n<p>This does not meet the application search requirement well. Athena is not designed to power complex full-text application search, and a fixed 15-minute schedule does not meet a 5-minute freshness requirement for consultant availability changes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue workflows with scheduled and event-based triggers, store processed data in DynamoDB, and build custom full-text search by using Lambda functions</strong> - AWS Glue workflows can coordinate Glue jobs and can use scheduled or event-based triggers. DynamoDB can support low-latency key-value access for application data.</p>\n\n<p>The issue with this approach is the custom search solution. Building complex full-text search with Lambda functions adds operational overhead and search-engine development work, while OpenSearch Service provides managed full-text search capabilities.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/searching.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/searching.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html</a></p>"},{"id":56,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A retail analytics company collects a large volume of clickstream events from its website and mobile application. The company needs to filter invalid events, apply simple streaming transformations, and deliver the processed data to Amazon S3 for downstream analysis. The company wants the most operationally efficient solution with minimal custom infrastructure to manage.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Deploy an Apache Spark Streaming application on an Amazon EMR cluster to consume the clickstream data, transform the records, and write the processed output to Amazon S3</p>","<p>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with provisioned capacity and Lambda consumers to run custom filtering logic and write the processed events to Amazon S3</p>","<p>Use Amazon Data Firehose with built-in processing capabilities to transform and filter the stream, then deliver the processed data directly to Amazon S3</p>","<p>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to perform stateful processing, event filtering, and output delivery to Amazon S3</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Data Firehose with built-in processing capabilities to transform and filter the stream, then deliver the processed data directly to Amazon S3</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Data Firehose is the most operationally efficient choice when the company needs a managed streaming delivery service that can process streaming data and deliver the results to Amazon S3. Firehose manages scaling, buffering, retries, and delivery without requiring the team to manage consumers or stream processing infrastructure.</p>\n\n<p>The requirement focuses on filtering and transformation before storage in Amazon S3. Firehose can apply processing as part of the delivery stream and then write the processed records directly to the S3 destination.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational overhead because the company does not need to provision Kinesis consumers, operate EMR clusters, run containerized workers, or manage a separate stream processing application. Firehose provides a managed path from streaming ingestion to S3 delivery.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with provisioned capacity and Lambda consumers to run custom filtering logic and write the processed events to Amazon S3</strong> - Kinesis Data Streams with Lambda consumers can process streaming records and write output to Amazon S3. This pattern can work when the team needs custom event handling logic.</p>\n\n<p>However, this design requires the team to manage stream capacity, Lambda consumer behavior, error handling, retries, and custom S3 writes. Firehose provides a more managed delivery path with less operational effort.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to perform stateful processing, event filtering, and output delivery to Amazon S3</strong> - Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink is well suited for complex, stateful stream processing, such as joins, windows, and rolling aggregations. It can process data from Kinesis streams and write results to downstream destinations.</p>\n\n<p>However, the requirement asks for the most operationally efficient solution for managed filtering, transformation, and S3 delivery. Firehose is simpler when the processing requirement does not require a full stream processing application.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Deploy an Apache Spark Streaming application on an Amazon EMR cluster to consume the clickstream data, transform the records, and write the processed output to Amazon S3</strong> - Apache Spark Streaming on Amazon EMR can process large streaming datasets and write transformed output to Amazon S3. This approach can support advanced processing logic.</p>\n\n<p>However, an EMR cluster introduces cluster provisioning, scaling, patching, and monitoring responsibilities. That is more operational overhead than using a fully managed Firehose delivery stream.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/data-transformation.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/data-transformation.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/create-destination.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/create-destination.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/basic-deliver.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/basic-deliver.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/firehose/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/firehose/faqs/</a></p>"},{"id":57,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A marketing analytics company receives customer enrichment records from an external data provider and loads the records into an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. The same warehouse already stores campaign data and purchase history. Analysts want to combine these datasets to identify customers who are likely to respond to future offers.</p>\n\n<p>During ingestion, a data engineer discovers that one attribute in the third-party feed contains nested JSON values with varying structures. The company wants to load this field into Amazon Redshift with the least transformation effort.</p>\n\n<p>How should the data engineer load the JSON field into the data warehouse?</p>","answers":["<p>Define the target column with the SUPER data type and load the semi-structured JSON values into the Redshift table</p>","<p>Store the JSON files in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the nested attributes separately from the Redshift warehouse</p>","<p>Use AWS Glue to flatten every JSON attribute into separate relational columns before loading the data into the Redshift target table</p>","<p>Convert the JSON attribute into a VARCHAR column, store the raw JSON string, and parse every nested field manually in each downstream SQL query</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Define the target column with the SUPER data type and load the semi-structured JSON values into the Redshift table</strong></p>\n\n<p>The SUPER data type is the best fit because Amazon Redshift supports semi-structured data such as JSON through SUPER columns. The data engineer can store nested and schema-flexible JSON values in the same Redshift table as structured customer attributes.</p>\n\n<p>This approach minimizes transformation work. The engineer does not need to flatten every nested JSON key before loading the data, which is useful when the third-party field has varying structures across records.</p>\n\n<p>After loading the JSON values into a SUPER column, analysts can query the semi-structured attributes together with structured warehouse tables such as purchases, products, and campaigns. This meets the requirement to combine the third-party data with existing Redshift data while using the least effort.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/19bc1b73273cc695da60e62a136b5340d25c195548e9768a42e7d8fb9e3826fe.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/19bc1b73273cc695da60e62a136b5340d25c195548e9768a42e7d8fb9e3826fe.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/5b869f6c4c9f74c8c682e8e7d1c5c8993ec2ccbfaf87c2b7e176611e67db097f.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/5b869f6c4c9f74c8c682e8e7d1c5c8993ec2ccbfaf87c2b7e176611e67db097f.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Store the JSON files in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the nested attributes separately from the Redshift warehouse</strong> - Amazon Athena can query data directly in Amazon S3 and can work well for ad hoc analysis over data lake files. This can be useful when teams do not need to load all data into a warehouse.</p>\n\n<p>This option does not meet the requirement because the company needs to load the customer data into Amazon Redshift and combine it with existing warehouse tables. Keeping the JSON data outside Redshift adds extra query coordination and does not provide the least-effort warehouse loading path.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use AWS Glue to flatten every JSON attribute into separate relational columns before loading the data into the Redshift target table</strong> - AWS Glue can transform JSON data and flatten nested fields before loading the results into Amazon Redshift. This approach can work when the target schema is stable and analysts need fully relational columns.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is that flattening all JSON attributes increases ETL development and maintenance. Because the JSON field has varying structures, using a SUPER column avoids unnecessary schema redesign and custom transformation logic.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Convert the JSON attribute into a VARCHAR column, store the raw JSON string, and parse every nested field manually in each downstream SQL query</strong> - A VARCHAR column can store raw JSON text, and Redshift provides functions that can extract values from JSON strings. This might work for simple JSON fields with predictable structures.</p>\n\n<p>This does not provide the least-effort or best-supported approach for nested semi-structured data. AWS recommends using SUPER for semi-structured data instead of storing JSON as plain text and repeatedly parsing it in downstream queries.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/super-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/super-overview.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/query-super.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/query-super.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/</a></p>"},{"id":58,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A data engineer created an Amazon Athena table named audit_cloudtrail_events to query AWS CloudTrail logs for a centralized security account. The audit team wants a summary of API errors that have occurred since the beginning of 2025.</p>\n\n<p>The query must include only events that contain an error code. The query must group the results by API action, error code, and error message, and must return the 10 error groups with the highest occurrence counts.</p>\n\n<p>Which query will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p><code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE eventtime &gt;= '2025-01-01T00:00:00Z' GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10</code></p>","<p><code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode IS NOT NULL AND eventtime &gt;= '2025-01-01T00:00:00Z' GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage LIMIT 10</code></p>","<p><code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode = NULL AND eventtime &gt;= '2025-01-01T00:00:00Z' GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10</code></p>","<p><code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode IS NOT NULL AND eventtime &gt;= '2025-01-01T00:00:00Z' GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10</code></p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong><code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode IS NOT NULL AND eventtime &gt;= '2025-01-01T00:00:00Z' GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10</code></strong></p>\n\n<p>This query meets all stated requirements. It filters the CloudTrail table to include only records where errorcode is not null, which means the result focuses only on events that produced an error code.</p>\n\n<p>The query also filters eventtime to include only events that occurred on or after the beginning of 2025. This satisfies the audit team’s requirement to analyze errors from the specified time window.</p>\n\n<p>Finally, the query groups by eventname, errorcode, and errormessage, counts the events in each group, orders the groups by TotalEvents in descending order, and limits the output to 10 rows. This returns the 10 most frequent error groups.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong><code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE eventtime &gt;= '2025-01-01T00:00:00Z' GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10</code></strong> - This query groups the events correctly and orders the grouped results by TotalEvents in descending order. It also filters the results to events since the beginning of 2025.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is that it does not filter out records where errorcode is null. As a result, the query could include non-error events or grouped records that do not contain an actual error code, which does not meet the requirement.</p>\n\n<p><strong><code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode IS NOT NULL AND eventtime &gt;= '2025-01-01T00:00:00Z' GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage LIMIT 10</code></strong> - This query correctly filters for non-null error codes and includes only events from the required time period. It also groups the results by API action, error code, and error message.</p>\n\n<p>This option falls short because it does not sort the grouped results by TotalEvents in descending order. Without the ORDER BY clause, the query does not guarantee that the returned 10 rows are the error groups with the highest occurrence counts.</p>\n\n<p><strong><code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode = NULL AND eventtime &gt;= '2025-01-01T00:00:00Z' GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10</code></strong> - This query attempts to count and group error-related fields, and it orders the grouped results by TotalEvents in descending order. It also applies the required eventtime filter.</p>\n\n<p>This query is incorrect because SQL comparisons with NULL require IS NULL or IS NOT NULL. The expression errorcode = NULL does not correctly identify records with missing error codes, and the requirement is to include events where an error code exists.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/query-examples-cloudtrail-logs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/query-examples-cloudtrail-logs.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/cloudtrail-logs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/cloudtrail-logs.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automating-aws-service-logs-table-creation-and-querying-them-with-amazon-athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automating-aws-service-logs-table-creation-and-querying-them-with-amazon-athena/</a></p>"},{"id":59,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A healthcare analytics company maintains an Amazon S3 based data lake that contains datasets for clinical operations, finance, research, and compliance teams. The data lake receives several million patient interaction records each day. A data engineer must design an access control solution that allows each department to access only the datasets and columns that the department is authorized to use. The solution must enforce least privilege and minimize ongoing permission management effort.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational effort?</p>","answers":["<p>Set up AWS Lake Formation, assign LF-Tags to AWS Glue Data Catalog resources, and enable Lake Formation tag-based access control for department-specific permissions</p>","<p>Use Amazon Redshift and Amazon Redshift Spectrum as the only query layer for the data lake, and configure one Redshift IAM role with access to the S3 datasets</p>","<p>Deploy an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database with the aws_s3 extension, use AWS Step Functions to invoke Lambda functions that synchronize department-specific S3 data into tables, and grant database permissions by department</p>","<p>Use S3 Access Points for each department, configure one access point policy per department, and require data engineers to update the access point policies whenever datasets, columns, or department permissions change</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up AWS Lake Formation, assign LF-Tags to AWS Glue Data Catalog resources, and enable Lake Formation tag-based access control for department-specific permissions</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Lake Formation provides centralized governance for data lakes that use Amazon S3 and the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Lake Formation permissions can control access to databases, tables, columns, and underlying S3 data for supported analytics services.</p>\n\n<p>Lake Formation tag-based access control, or LF-TBAC, lets the company assign LF-Tags to Data Catalog resources and grant permissions based on tag expressions. For example, the company can tag datasets by department, sensitivity level, region, or data domain, then grant each department access only to resources that match approved tag values.</p>\n\n<p>This solution has the least operational effort for a large, multi-department data lake because it avoids maintaining large numbers of resource-specific IAM policies. As new datasets arrive, data stewards can classify the resources with LF-Tags, and the existing tag-based permissions can continue to enforce least privilege at scale.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/6ced6f50eed2a279096f723c5487a9e8a7ad1d3d6a1e94e6ccd01a81bb6771a2.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/6ced6f50eed2a279096f723c5487a9e8a7ad1d3d6a1e94e6ccd01a81bb6771a2.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/9d68cf008a6eb971f1374964fd86ceec08daf6abf7134b87b8733a41b8755bb1.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/9d68cf008a6eb971f1374964fd86ceec08daf6abf7134b87b8733a41b8755bb1.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Deploy an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database with the aws_s3 extension, use AWS Step Functions to invoke Lambda functions that synchronize department-specific S3 data into tables, and grant database permissions by department</strong> - Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL with the aws_s3 extension can import or export data between PostgreSQL and Amazon S3. This can support database-centric workflows that need to move files into or out of PostgreSQL.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option creates a separate database copy of data lake content and requires custom synchronization logic. It does not provide centralized lake governance across S3 datasets, Glue Data Catalog metadata, table permissions, and column-level access.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use S3 Access Points for each department, configure one access point policy per department, and require data engineers to update the access point policies whenever datasets, columns, or department permissions change</strong> - S3 Access Points can simplify access management for shared datasets by creating named access points with distinct policies. They can be useful when multiple applications or teams need different access paths to the same bucket.</p>\n\n<p>However, this option still requires data engineers to manage access point policies as datasets and department permissions change. It also does not provide the same fine-grained Data Catalog permissions, column-level controls, and tag-based governance model that Lake Formation LF-TBAC provides.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Redshift and Amazon Redshift Spectrum as the only query layer for the data lake, and configure one Redshift IAM role with access to the S3 datasets</strong> - Amazon Redshift Spectrum can query data stored in Amazon S3, and Redshift can assume an IAM role to access external data. This approach can provide a controlled SQL entry point for some analytics workloads.</p>\n\n<p>However, a single Redshift IAM role with broad S3 access does not enforce department-specific least privilege across the data lake. It also makes Redshift the primary access layer instead of using Lake Formation to govern access centrally across supported analytics services.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/tag-based-access-control.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/tag-based-access-control.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/managing-tags.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/managing-tags.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/TBAC-assigning-tags.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/TBAC-assigning-tags.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-catalog-perms-TBAC.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-catalog-perms-TBAC.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/easily-manage-your-data-lake-at-scale-using-tag-based-access-control-in-aws-lake-formation/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/easily-manage-your-data-lake-at-scale-using-tag-based-access-control-in-aws-lake-formation/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/</a></p>"},{"id":60,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A retail analytics team needs to deploy a serverless pipeline that processes inventory files. Store systems upload CSV files to a source Amazon S3 bucket, and each upload must invoke an AWS Lambda function. The Lambda function converts the CSV content to JSON and writes the transformed output to a separate destination S3 bucket.</p>\n\n<p>A data engineer has already created an AWS Serverless Application Model template that defines the Lambda function. The data engineer wants AWS SAM to package and deploy the complete pipeline, including the S3 upload trigger.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will package and deploy this serverless data pipeline?</p>","answers":["<p>Create the Lambda function in the SAM template, use EventBridge Scheduler to scan the source bucket, and deploy the schedule with sam sync</p>","<p>Add the source S3 bucket and S3 event source to the SAM template, run sam build, and deploy by using aws cloudformation deploy</p>","<p>Add the source S3 bucket and S3 event source to the SAM template, run sam build, and then run sam deploy --guided</p>","<p>Run sam deploy with the --s3-bucket option to upload the Lambda code, and then manually add the S3 trigger in the console</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Add the source S3 bucket and S3 event source to the SAM template, run sam build, and then run sam deploy --guided</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS SAM can define serverless application resources in a SAM template, including an AWS Lambda function, Amazon S3 buckets, and an S3 event source that invokes the Lambda function when a new object is created. Adding both the source S3 bucket and the S3 event source to the template allows AWS SAM to deploy the pipeline as infrastructure as code.</p>\n\n<p>The sam build command prepares the Lambda function code and dependencies for deployment. It creates build artifacts that AWS SAM can use when deploying the application to AWS.</p>\n\n<p>The sam deploy --guided command is appropriate for guided deployment, especially when deploying a SAM application for the first time. This approach packages and deploys the Lambda function and related resources from the SAM template without requiring manual trigger configuration in the console.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/071009f7beb4f7819b47952768bcf516e7ec437854b5eea440edcd851dce9425.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/071009f7beb4f7819b47952768bcf516e7ec437854b5eea440edcd851dce9425.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/4ace50a92a92f2f8bc0ee4ff0d96f335f3d5adda2765df76ba9bf6b0cf0f4901.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/4ace50a92a92f2f8bc0ee4ff0d96f335f3d5adda2765df76ba9bf6b0cf0f4901.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Run sam deploy with the --s3-bucket option to upload the Lambda code, and then manually add the S3 trigger in the console</strong> - The sam deploy command can upload and deploy application artifacts when the required deployment settings are provided. The --s3-bucket option can identify an S3 bucket for deployment artifacts.</p>\n\n<p>The limitation is that this option leaves the S3 event trigger outside the SAM template and configures it manually. That does not fully use AWS SAM to deploy the pipeline and creates configuration drift between the template and the deployed resources.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create the Lambda function in the SAM template, use EventBridge Scheduler to scan the source bucket, and deploy the schedule with sam sync</strong> - EventBridge Scheduler can invoke targets on a schedule. This can work for time-based automation that runs at fixed intervals.</p>\n\n<p>This does not match the event-driven requirement. The pipeline must run whenever a user uploads a CSV file to Amazon S3, so the SAM template should define an S3 event source for the Lambda function instead of a scheduled bucket scan.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Add the source S3 bucket and S3 event source to the SAM template, run sam build, and deploy by using aws cloudformation deploy</strong> - AWS SAM templates are transformed into AWS CloudFormation resources during deployment. Advanced teams can sometimes use CloudFormation deployment commands as part of custom deployment workflows.</p>\n\n<p>The issue with this approach is that the recommended SAM CLI deployment workflow is to use sam deploy after sam build. The aws cloudformation deploy command does not provide the same SAM deployment experience as sam deploy --guided for packaging, parameter prompting, and SAM-managed deployment settings.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/sam-property-function-s3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/sam-property-function-s3.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/using-sam-cli-build.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/using-sam-cli-build.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/using-sam-cli-deploy.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/using-sam-cli-deploy.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/serverless/sam/\">https://aws.amazon.com/serverless/sam/</a></p>"},{"id":61,"section":"Domain 1: Data Ingestion and Transformation","question":"<p>A company wants to store all of its consumer data on Amazon S3. Before storing the data, the company must clean it by standardizing the formats of a few of the data columns. A single data record might range in size from 500 KB to 10 MB.</p>\n\n<p>Which of these options represents the right solution?</p>","answers":["<p>Use Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to ingest incoming data. Configure an AWS Lambda function to read events from the SQS queue and upload the events to Amazon S3</p>","<p>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest data. Configure an AWS Lambda function to cleanse/transform the data written into the Firehose delivery stream which is then delivered to Amazon S3</p>","<p>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Configure a stream for incoming raw data. Kinesis Agent can be used to write data to the stream. Configure an Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics application to read the raw data and transform it to the necessary format before writing it to Amazon S3</p>","<p>Use Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka. Create a topic for the initial raw data. Use a Kafka producer to write data on this topic. Use the Apache Kafka consumer API to create a consumer application (that can be hosted on Amazon EC2 instance) that reads data from this topic, transforms the data as needed, and writes it to Amazon S3 for final storage</p>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka. Create a topic for the initial raw data. Use a Kafka producer to write data on this topic. Use the Apache Kafka consumer API to create a consumer application (that can be hosted on Amazon EC2 instance) that reads data from this topic, transforms the data as needed, and writes it to Amazon S3 for final storage</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK) is a fully managed service that makes it easy for you to build and run applications that use Apache Kafka to process streaming data. Apache Kafka is an open-source platform for building real-time streaming data pipelines and applications. With Amazon MSK, you can use native Apache Kafka APIs to populate data lakes, stream changes to and from databases, and power machine learning and analytics applications.</p>\n\n<p>Apache Kafka stores records in topics. Data producers write records to topics and consumers read records from topics. Each record in Apache Kafka consists of a key, a value, and a timestamp.</p>\n\n<p>Amazon MSK is preferred over Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and Kinesis Firehose because of the limitation on record size. The maximum record size of an Amazon MSK is 100 MB.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest data. Configure an AWS Lambda function to cleanse/transform the data written into the Firehose delivery stream which is then delivered to Amazon S3</strong> - The maximum size of a record sent to Kinesis Data Firehose, before base64-encoding, is 1,000 KiB. Hence, Firehose cannot be used for this requirement where data can be of the order of 10MB.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Configure a stream for incoming raw data. Kinesis Agent can be used to write data to the stream. Configure an Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics application to read the raw data and transform it to the necessary format before writing it to Amazon S3</strong> - For Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, the maximum size of a data blob (the data payload before Base64-encoding) within one record is 1 megabyte (MB). Hence, Kinesis Data Streams cannot be used for this requirement where data can be of the order of 10MB.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to ingest incoming data. Configure an AWS Lambda function to read events from the SQS queue and upload the events to Amazon S3</strong> - Amazon SQS offers a reliable, highly scalable hosted queue for storing messages as they travel between applications or microservices. It moves data between distributed application components and helps you decouple these components. Amazon SQS provides common middleware constructs such as dead-letter queues and poison-pill management. It is not the best fit as a real-time data stream processing solution.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/limits.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/limits.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/limits.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/limits.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://www.amazonaws.cn/en/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\">https://www.amazonaws.cn/en/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/</a></p>"},{"id":62,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A retail company stores its customer data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The data is accessed by employees from various countries for analytics purposes. The governance team needs to implement a solution ensuring that data analysts can only access customer data from their respective countries.</p>\n\n<p>What solution would satisfy these requirements with minimal operational effort?</p>","answers":["<p>Set up S3 Access Point to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 Access Points policy to dynamically filter data based on the country of the analyst making the read request</p>","<p>Create a separate bucket for each country's customer data. Provide access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves</p>","<p>Configure the S3 bucket as a data lake location in AWS Lake Formation and leverage the Lake Formation row-level security features to enforce the company's access policies</p>","<p>Migrate the data to AWS Regions that are close to the countries where the customers are. Restrict access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Configure the S3 bucket as a data lake location in AWS Lake Formation and leverage the Lake Formation row-level security features to enforce the company's access policies</strong></p>\n\n<p>AWS Lake Formation helps you centrally govern, secure, and globally share data for analytics and machine learning. With Lake Formation, you can manage fine-grained access control for your data lake data on Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and its metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.</p>\n\n<p>Lake Formation provides its own permissions model that augments the IAM permissions model. The Lake Formation permissions model enables fine-grained access to data stored in data lakes through a simple grant or revoke mechanism, much like a relational database management system (RDBMS). Lake Formation provides data filters that allow you to restrict access to a combination of columns and rows. You can use row and cell-level security to protect sensitive data like Personal Identifiable Information (PII).</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/abd2e6464f07aac8723aed0f1f3e5facf05e10e6913b5dfdce8c3f673112371a.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/abd2e6464f07aac8723aed0f1f3e5facf05e10e6913b5dfdce8c3f673112371a.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Set up S3 Access Point to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 Access Points policy to dynamically filter data based on the country of the analyst making the read request</strong> - Amazon S3 Access Points simplify data access for any AWS service or customer application that stores data in S3. With S3 Access Points, customers can create unique access control policies for each access point to easily control access to shared datasets.</p>\n\n<p>Each S3 Access Point is configured with an access policy specific to a use case or application. For example, you can create an access point for your S3 bucket that grants access to groups of users or applications for your data lake. An Access Point can support a single user or application, or groups of users or applications within and across accounts, allowing separate management of each access point.</p>\n\n<p>You cannot use an S3 Access Points policy to dynamically filter data based on the country of the analyst making the read request.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a separate bucket for each country's customer data. Provide access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves</strong> - Creating a separate bucket for each country's customer data is wasteful use of resources and involves significant operational effort to manage the data on an ongoing basis, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Migrate the data to AWS Regions that are close to the countries where the customers are. Restrict access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves</strong> - This option also involves creating multiple buckets (since each S3 bucket is specific to an AWS Region), therefore it would also involve a significant operational effort to manage the data on an ongoing basis, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/register-data-lake.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/register-data-lake.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/registration-role.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/registration-role.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/</a></p>"},{"id":63,"section":"Domain 4: Data Security and Governance","question":"<p>A digital media company has hired you to improve the data backup solution for applications running on the AWS Cloud. Currently, all of the applications running on AWS use at least two Availability Zones (AZs). The updated backup policy at the company mandates that all nightly backups for its data are durably stored in at least two geographically distinct Regions for Production and Disaster Recovery (DR) and the backup processes for both Regions must be fully automated. The new backup solution must ensure that the backup is available to be restored immediately for the Production Region and should be restored within 24 hours in the DR Region.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following represents the MOST cost-effective solution that will address the given use-case?</p>","answers":["<p>Create a backup process to persist all the data to a large Amazon EBS volume attached to the backup server in the Production Region. Run nightly cron jobs to snapshot these volumes and then copy these snapshots to the DR Region</p>","<p>Create a backup process to persist all the data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive in the DR Region to ensure minimum possible costs in both Regions</p>","<p>Create a backup process to persist all the data to an S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this S3 bucket A to an S3 bucket B using S3 standard storage class in the DR Region and set up a lifecycle policy in the DR Region to immediately move this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive</p>","<p>Create a backup process to persist all the data to an S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this S3 bucket A to an S3 bucket B using S3 standard-IA storage class in the DR Region and set up a lifecycle policy in the DR Region to immediately move this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive</p>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a backup process to persist all the data to an S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this S3 bucket A to an S3 bucket B using S3 standard storage class in the DR Region and set up a lifecycle policy in the DR Region to immediately move this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive</strong></p>\n\n<p>S3 Replication enables automatic, asynchronous copying of objects across Amazon S3 buckets. There are two types of Replications:</p>\n\n<p>Cross-Region Replication (CRR) is used to copy objects across Amazon S3 buckets in different AWS Regions.</p>\n\n<p>Same-Region replication (SRR) is used to copy objects across Amazon S3 buckets in the same AWS Region.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/174c54c5dd1c5ff4aeebb5fe39c61b33ecd95918d08e799619b0a9f770c60baf.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/174c54c5dd1c5ff4aeebb5fe39c61b33ecd95918d08e799619b0a9f770c60baf.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/replication.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/replication.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>For the given use-case, you can set up cross-Region replication from S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the production Region to S3 bucket B using the S3 standard storage class in the DR Region and further create a lifecycle policy to transition this data in bucket B from standard storage class to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive.</p>\n\n<p>Please note the allowed transitions for the S3 Lifecycle Policy:\n<img src=\"exams/assets/444648786eb1f2dd1e8938b2f38de01be1af24785dcd6692fe4aeb1fa9373039.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/444648786eb1f2dd1e8938b2f38de01be1af24785dcd6692fe4aeb1fa9373039.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>By default, Amazon S3 stores object replicas using the same storage class as the source object. You can also specify a different storage class for the replicas. This allows you to use something like an S3 Standard-IA for the replica bucket, however, S3 standard IA has a minimum storage duration charge of 30 days thereby making it costlier than using S3 Standard storage class for the given scenario because the data would be moved to Glacier Deep Archive via a Lifecycle policy immediately.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/bfe699ba2ecabe1d5eb86180f5a9ee7a96d294807142f6cc727d5a04f2f080c9.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/bfe699ba2ecabe1d5eb86180f5a9ee7a96d294807142f6cc727d5a04f2f080c9.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a backup process to persist all the data to an S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this S3 bucket A to an S3 bucket B using S3 standard-IA storage class in the DR Region and set up a lifecycle policy in the DR Region to immediately move this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive</strong> - As mentioned in the explanation above, S3 standard IA has a minimum storage duration charge of 30 days thereby making it costlier than using S3 Standard storage class for the given scenario, so this option is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a backup process to persist all the data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive in the DR Region to ensure minimum possible costs in both Regions</strong> - One of the key requirements of the given scenario is to ensure that the backup is available to be restored immediately for the Production Region. However, Glacier Deep Archive has a first-byte latency of hours while restoring data, hence this option is not correct for the given use-case.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Create a backup process to persist all the data to a large Amazon EBS volume attached to the backup server in the Production Region. Run nightly cron jobs to snapshot these volumes and then copy these snapshots to the DR Region</strong> - One of the key requirements of the given scenario is to ensure that the backup is durable but the data in an EBS volume is only replicated within its Availability Zone so it is not highly durable. However, the EBS snapshots are stored on Amazon S3, which is durable. The issue with this option is that it introduces the additional cost of an EBS volume and also does not optimize the storage cost in the DR Region as it does not leverage Glacier Deep Archive for the backup data storage.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/replication.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/replication.html</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/</a></p>"},{"id":64,"section":"Domain 3: Data Operations and Support","question":"<p>A company uses Amazon Athena for ad-hoc analysis of customer data in its data lake hosted on Amazon S3. A data engineer at the company wants to understand the detailed breakdown for each of the SQL query’s run plan and the computational cost of each operation.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following Amazon Athena statements can be used to accomplish this task?</p>","answers":["<p>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</p>","<p>OPTIMIZE</p>","<p>EXPLAIN</p>","<p>PREPARE</p>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</strong></p>\n\n<p>The EXPLAIN ANALYZE statement shows both the distributed execution plan of a specified SQL statement and the computational cost of each operation in a SQL query. You can output the results in text or JSON format. Administrators can benefit from using EXPLAIN ANALYZE because it provides a scanned data count, which helps you reduce financial impact due to user queries and apply optimizations for better cost control.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c205e18b3ce1f5dd428940ffb3f7197f540e24f1e9467910671ce290ffd14efe.jpg\"></p><div><img src=\"exams/assets/c205e18b3ce1f5dd428940ffb3f7197f540e24f1e9467910671ce290ffd14efe.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\nvia - <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html</a><p></p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>EXPLAIN</strong> - The EXPLAIN statement only provides a detailed breakdown of a query’s run plan. You can analyze the plan to identify and reduce query complexity and improve its runtime. You can also use EXPLAIN to validate SQL syntax prior to running the query. Doing so helps prevent errors that would have occurred while running the query.</p>\n\n<p><strong>PREPARE</strong> - Creates an SQL statement with the name statement_name to be run at a later time. The statement can include parameters represented by question marks.</p>\n\n<p><strong>OPTIMIZE</strong> - Optimizes rows in an Apache Iceberg table by rewriting data files into a more optimized layout based on their size and the number of associated delete files.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-federated-query-performance-using-explain-and-explain-analyze-in-amazon-athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-federated-query-performance-using-explain-and-explain-analyze-in-amazon-athena/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/athena-explain-statement.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/athena-explain-statement.html</a></p>"},{"id":65,"section":"Domain 2: Data Store Management","question":"<p>A multinational retail company stores sales and inventory data in Amazon Redshift. The company uses Amazon Quick Suite to build operational dashboards and management reports. Business analysts in different regions have different levels of technical expertise. Several analysts do not know SQL, but they frequently need to create new reports and dashboards for business stakeholders. The company wants analysts to use natural language prompts to create dashboards and reports more efficiently with the least operational effort.</p>\n\n<p>Which solution will meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<p>Build a custom Amazon Bedrock application that converts natural language prompts into SQL queries, runs the generated SQL against Amazon Redshift, and renders the results in a custom dashboard interface</p>","<p>Enable Amazon Q in Amazon Quick Suite and generate Amazon Quick Suite dashboards and reports by using natural language prompts</p>","<p>Use Amazon Quick Suite dashboards with federated query access to Amazon Redshift</p>","<p>Use Amazon Quick Suite dashboards with zero-ETL access to Amazon Redshift</p>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Correct option:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enable Amazon Q in Amazon Quick Suite and generate Amazon Quick Suite dashboards and reports by using natural language prompts</strong></p>\n\n<p>Amazon Q in Amazon Quick Suite is designed for generative business intelligence. It helps business users and analysts build and consume insights by using natural language instead of relying only on manual dashboard design or SQL authoring.</p>\n\n<p>This solution directly addresses the analysts who lack SQL knowledge. Analysts can describe the dashboards, reports, metrics, and visuals they need in natural language, and Amazon Q can help create analyses, refine visuals, generate insights, and accelerate report creation.</p>\n\n<p>This option also has the least operational effort because it uses a managed capability inside the existing Amazon Quick Suite environment. The company does not need to build a custom natural language-to-SQL system, operate a separate BI tool, or create custom Lambda-based report generation workflows.</p>\n\n<p>Incorrect options:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Quick Suite dashboards with federated query access to Amazon Redshift</strong> - Federated query access can help a BI tool query data from a source system. However, it does not provide the natural language dashboard and report generation capability that the analysts need.</p>\n\n<p>This option focuses on connectivity to Amazon Redshift rather than generative BI. Analysts who lack SQL knowledge would still need help authoring reports, designing visuals, and translating business questions into analytical logic.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Use Amazon Quick Suite dashboards with zero-ETL access to Amazon Redshift</strong> - Zero-ETL integrations can reduce data movement between services and simplify some analytics architectures. However, zero-ETL access does not by itself let analysts create dashboards and reports from natural language prompts.</p>\n\n<p>The requirement is not primarily about reducing data movement from Amazon Redshift. The requirement is to make report creation easier for analysts with varying technical skills, which Amazon Q in Amazon Quick Suite addresses more directly.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Build a custom Amazon Bedrock application that converts natural language prompts into SQL queries, runs the generated SQL against Amazon Redshift, and renders the results in a custom dashboard interface</strong> - A custom Amazon Bedrock application could translate natural language questions into SQL and query Amazon Redshift. However, this approach requires the company to build and maintain prompt logic, SQL validation, authorization controls, semantic mappings, dashboard rendering, and operational monitoring.</p>\n\n<p>This option is much more complex than enabling a managed generative BI capability in Amazon Quick Suite. It also creates additional risks around incorrect SQL generation, data governance, and report consistency.</p>\n\n<p>References:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/generate-dashboards-from-natural-language-prompts-in-amazon-quick/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/generate-dashboards-from-natural-language-prompts-in-amazon-quick/</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/what-is.html</a> <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/quick-bi.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/quick-bi.html</a></p>"}]};